HT_DATA.load("detail", {"Kr3quGh7pJA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the canonical video on how music theory is really the theory of one Western tradition, with the raga example.","q":{"x":"The canonical video on the subject","i":41092863,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41092863,"author":"Kye","month":"2024-07","story":"Tritone Substitutions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Canonical video on cultural context of music theory, with raga example.","quote":"The canonical video on the subject","text":"The canonical video on the subject: [VIDEO] Some people will naively say \"All music has pentatonic! Universal!\" but ignore vital cultural context. One example in the video is ragas: they can look a lot like Western scales and modes, but they have a time and context. Western music also has a time and context, but it doesn't stand out as much to us unless someone goes full ham breaking that context like with the Imperial March in a Major mode: [LINK] It changes the mood from \"this is the bad guys!\" to something more positive and triumphant."},{"id":35286419,"author":"shirleyquirk","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Tools to Learn Music Theory?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, engaging, skeptical take on political criticisms of music theory.","quote":"I recommend Adam Neely on YouTube","text":"I grew up in a family of classical musicians, got a degree in music, and was briefly a professional classical musician myself. So my advice may be very out of touch with the experience of someone learning for the first time, but my experiences learning beyond European classical music theory left me feeling insulted. The basics of music theory are presented as though they are the foundations upon which all subsequent music is derived. In fact, teaching scales and chords is like teaching that the electron orbits the nucleus - a 'simplification'. There _are_ real psychoacoustic truths, alongside equally fascinating historical forces, that underpin music theory. Learn the language and idioms of music theory, by all means, but don't think of it as being in any way true. Its a syncretist cargo cult of hacks and rules of thumb handed down by centuries of men with dubious motives. I recommend Adam Neely on YouTube, he illustrates music theory concepts clearly, engagingly, and with an appropriate skepticism for received wisdom. Here's his discussion of some more political criticisms of music theory: [VIDEO] I recommend Sethares' _Tuning, Timbre, Spectrum, Scale_ to anyone interested in the underpinnings of why some notes sound good together and others don't. [2] [LINK]"},{"id":26399386,"author":"Kye","month":"2021-03","story":"Music Software and Bad Interface Design: Avid’s Sibelius (2018) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good video on the narrow Western view of music despite its title.","quote":"Totally worth the time.","text":"There's a very good video on the way too narrow view many people in the west have of music. [VIDEO] I know the title will set people off in a place like this, but it's not a 2014 Tumblr-level \"kill white people\" style analysis. Totally worth the time."},{"id":24408493,"author":"tobr","month":"2020-09","story":"Is Spotify Killing the Top 40?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues music theory is rooted in white supremacist ideas of authentic music.","quote":"please watch this excellent video","text":"> pop music lacks musicality, most of what they listed were to do with sonic tricks that are not really tied to musicality or musicianship This is a paradox, though. You’re saying that one of the most popular types of music “lacks musicality”. The only way that can be true is if you start with a narrow and arbitrary definition of what “musicality” is. If you examine the history of ideas of what is “real” or “authentic” or “objectively good” in music, who has been a proponent of those ideas through history, and why, you will find is that a lot of it is rooted in white supremacy. The idea is that a certain style of white European music is the only true music, and that other music is only valuable to the extent that it happens to fit within the theoretical frameworks developed around those styles. I guess what I’m saying is, please watch this excellent video, “Music Theory is Racist” by Adam Neely: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33123419,"author":"dagmx","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Is The Guitar Tuned Like It Is?","stance":"rec","why":"Why music theory is tied to Western music while alternatives exist.","quote":"Also a really good video on why","text":"I don’t think you can really throw music theory out because so much of it is inate to humans hearing and culture, and it’s largely a formalization of such. However, the rub is that what most people consider music theory is western classical theory. There’s a lot of different music theories around the world , with different scales, structures etc… Here’s a video you might find interesting of a microtonal guitar [LINK] Also a really good video on why “music theory” is often largely paired to western music [VIDEO] while there’s so many alternates out there"},{"id":32545691,"author":"viraptor","month":"2022-08","story":"Did Bach “invent” the rules of music theory?","stance":"rec","why":"Longer explanation of what Western music theory actually is.","quote":"It's worth watching.","text":"And if anyone has time for a longer explanation, Adam Neely did a great video about \"music theory\" - sorry, I mean \"the harmonic style of 18th century European musicians\". It's worth watching. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29091807,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2021-11","story":"(Music Production) Plug-In Capitalism","stance":"rec","why":"Popular video on the ongoing discussion of race in music.","quote":"Adam Neely has a popular video on what people are talking about if you're curious.","text":"> Does it refuse to get rid of dialect and variation if the race of the person processed is white? If it doesn’t it doesn’t make it white. For it to remove any dialect, it has to consider some dialect as standard. Think about it. > I’m not reading the whole crazy pointless long article about more fake problems, the abstract is ridiculous enough. Well if you're going to question-beg, then you can't really complain can you. The rest of what you said is a different complaint than the one I answered, so you're moving the goal post but then setting up a straw man of what that person wrote when you say such music can't be made on it. They were specific in what they said and it didn't exclude the possibility of making some of it with it. This subject is definitely something that's been discussed a lot in music lately, so apparently it's not that meaningless to many people. Adam Neely has a popular video on what people are talking about if you're curious. [1] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28603857,"author":"gbanfalvi","month":"2021-09","story":"Why I left academia","stance":"rec","why":"Genuine issues with how music theory and history are taught.","quote":"there _are_ genuine issues with how music theory and history are taught today","text":"A generous reading makes me think that his music department is just widening their program to incorporate more non-western content. They asked him to change his course material in a way he doesn't like. If that's the case, it's something many arts departments around the world have been doing in the last few years. You can call it a product of \"wokeness\", but it's also a product of globalisation. > He said that music departments could stop teaching Beethoven and Wagner because their compositions were produced during a time of empire “in the frankly insane belief that doing so will somehow materially improve current living conditions for the economically, socially, sexually, religiously, or racially underprivileged”. They're never going to stop teaching Beethoven and Wagner and it sincerely sounds like he's misunderstanding the goals with most music departments' changes. On the other hand, there _are_ genuine issues with how music theory and history are taught today: [VIDEO] He's a professor of music history and theory. He edits a book series called \"Music in Context\". He's written about homosexuality in the world of opera. He doesn't seem like at outright conservative reactionary and I'd really like to understand what his grievances with his department are."},{"id":27371412,"author":"krapp","month":"2021-06","story":"Stop overstimulating your mind with digital entertainment","stance":"rec","why":"Western music theory was designed excluding non-European contributions.","quote":"Western music theory was intentionally designed around white supremacist ideals","text":"See: Music Theory and White Supremacy by Adam Neely[0] [0] [VIDEO] The TL;DR for people who will be immediately triggered by the title and not bother to view is that Western music theory was intentionally designed around white supremacist ideals. It is exclusionary of the ideas and contributions of non-European cultures, and purposely so, as it deems them inferior."},{"id":26927969,"author":"DavidVoid","month":"2021-04","story":"Sisters with Transistors","stance":"rec","why":"Music theory in the West equals 18th century European harmonic style.","quote":"It's worth a watch if you're interested in music.","text":"> As if some patriarchal conspiracy was forcing classical composers like Mozart and Beethoven down people’s throats... No, but patriarchal and racial structures in society have historically suppressed artists who weren't white and male. Adam Neely has a good video on how the term \"music theory\" here in the West is essentially just \"the harmonic style of 18th century European musicians\" [1]. It's worth a watch if you're interested in music. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26618178,"author":"peterkos","month":"2021-03","story":"Deriving Keys from Chords in Music","stance":"rec","why":"How theoretical frameworks shape understanding of music, imposing European harmony standards.","quote":"Adam Neely has a great video[0]","text":"Yup! Adam Neely has a great video[0] talking about how serious of a role any theoretical framework has on understanding the music itself -- and how in the US at least, we very often prescribe European standards of harmony on music. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26133636,"author":"Pyramus","month":"2021-02","story":"Basic Music Theory","stance":"rec","why":"Argues music theory is really theory of Western music only.","quote":"he argues quite convincingly that 'music theory' is not the theory behind music but theory behind the particular subset of Western music","text":"I'd like to highlight Adam Neely's video on the subject [1] (also pointed out by user guerilla), where he argues quite convincingly that 'music theory' is not the theory behind music but theory behind the particular subset of Western music. That doesn't make it any less useful for creating this type of music, but it's in no way a universal theory, or arguably, a theory at all. More a collection of rules and conventions used in Western music. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32637642,"author":"frakt0x90","month":"2022-08","story":"Hear the World’s Oldest Known Song, “Hurrian Hymn No. 6” Written 3400 Years Ago","stance":"reference","why":"Background on non-Western tuning and why recreations sound Western.","quote":"You can get more info here for example","text":"The recreations are absurdly western sounding. The middle east has a long history of not using 12-tone equal temperament scales of Europe and while it's somewhat interesting to fantasize what it would sound like in the common current context, it seems a little white-washing and disingenuous. You can get more info here for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31271924,"author":"mkr-hn","month":"2022-05","story":"Clairnote: An alternative music notation system","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates resistance to new ideas in traditional music education.","quote":"A lot of that resistance is from people who believe their idealized notion of their culture is superior","text":"Notation has changed, adapted, expanded, contracted, and even spun off for different and new aspects of the problem. [LINK] [LINK] There are some grognards out there who hate even this, and they're way too influential in traditional music education, but notation hasn't been static and unchanging all these centuries. A lot of that resistance is from people who believe their idealized notion of their culture is superior and resist any exposure to new ideas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29812662,"author":"delgaudm","month":"2022-01","story":"Where's Today's Beethoven?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for framing music theory as 18th century European harmonic style.","quote":"the harmonic style of 18th century european music","text":"This article left me a bit confused. After a perusal I couldn't find a satisfactory set of criteria that defines a \"Beethoven\" beyond this vague bit at the top, which feels very hand-wavy. > Why has there been no one better in the last ~200 years - despite a vastly larger world population, highly democratized technology for writing and producing music, and a higher share of the population with education, basic nutrition, and other preconditions for becoming a great musician? and from a footnote: >Beethoven's music is \"great\" in at least two significant senses: (a) for nearly all listeners, it is enjoyable; (b) for obsessive listeners who are deeply familiar with other music that came before, it is \"impressive\" in the sense of demonstrating originality/innovation/other qualities. a) in the footnote feels very, very selective in \"for nearly all listeners\", and b) I'm not sure how \"impressive\"-ness is quantified, especially if obsessive listeners are required as a hurdle to clear to establish impressiveness as a barometer for a qualtifiable \"better\". What's \"better\"? How is the author sure there is no one \"better\"? Who decided that ? What criteria makes someone better? Is it some combination of technical skill at the keyboard, compositional skill at \"the harmonic style of 18th century european music\"[0] ? Is it the Fame that is achieved as a result of writing famous, lasting compositions? It is the music's complexity, or popularity? Does it have to be limited to so-called \"class"},{"id":28969287,"author":"chuchana","month":"2021-10","story":"Beyond Smart","stance":"reference","why":"Argues music theory is the harmonic style of 18th century Europeans.","quote":"This video argues it would be more accurate to call it the harmonic style of 18th century European musicians","text":"It is. This video argues it would be more accurate to call it the harmonic style of 18th century European musicians : [VIDEO]"},{"id":24516261,"author":"kbob","month":"2020-09","story":"The Anthropologist in an Economist World","stance":"reference","why":"Video uses Indian raga to show limits of Western notation.","quote":"this video has been viewed 600,000 times in the last 10 days and uses Indian raga as a specific example","text":"> As an analogy, imagine a music professor claiming that classical music is the only form of music, and then proceeding to use Western classical music notation and scales to transcribe Indian ragas. It's probably not a coincidence that this video has been viewed 600,000 times in the last 10 days and uses Indian raga as a specific example. Music Theory and White Supremacy Adam Neely [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["8s7NmMl_-yg",1],["RuCnZClWwpQ",1],["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["KVl5kMXz1vA",1],["EtX7WnFhxtQ",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["OOK5xkFijPc",1],["DZCBRHOg3PQ",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["4JWwOzEDGss",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["ojm74VGsZBU",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["6c_LeIXrzAk",1],["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1]]},"ncHmEUmJZf4":{"wy":"HN repeatedly cites it as the definitive explanation of SwissTable, the design behind Abseil, Rust's hash map and Go's maps.","q":{"x":"Excellent talk which explains the concepts really clearly and concisely.","i":49082650,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49082650,"author":"lalitmaganti","month":"2026-07","story":"Golang Maps: how Swiss Tables replaced the old bucket design","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, concise explanation of Swiss Table concepts","quote":"Excellent talk which explains the concepts really clearly and concisely.","text":"Have to plug the original Swiss Table talk by Matt Kulukundis: [VIDEO] Excellent talk which explains the concepts really clearly and concisely."},{"id":33677921,"author":"KerrickStaley","month":"2022-11","story":"Inside boost::unordered_flat_map","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Making of absl::flat_hash_map","quote":"is one of my favorites","text":"Related: this talk [VIDEO] about the making of absl::flat_hash_map (a \"competitor\" to boost::unordered_flat_map) is one of my favorites."},{"id":26392475,"author":"sujayakar","month":"2021-03","story":"MIPS Becomes RISC-V","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on SIMD accelerated hash table","quote":"the talk on SwissTable [2] (Google's SIMD accelerated hash table) is excellent","text":"Very cool! Independent of the cool use of `aesenc` and `aesdec`, the features for skipping ahead in the random stream and forking a separate stream are awesome. > My current home project is bump-allocator + semi-space garbage collection in SIMD for GPUs. As far as I can tell, both bump-allocation and semi-space garbage collection are easily SIMDified in an obvious manner. And since cudamalloc is fully synchronous, I wanted a more scalable, parallel solution to the GPU memory allocation problem. This is a great idea. I wonder if we could speed up LuaJIT even more by SIMD accelerating the GC's mark and/or sweep phases... If you're interested in more work in this area, a former coworker wrote a neat SPMD implementation of librsync [1]. And, if you haven't seen it, the talk on SwissTable [2] (Google's SIMD accelerated hash table) is excellent. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21221167,"author":"nwallin","month":"2019-10","story":"Hash tables","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long look at modern open addressing hash tables","quote":"(warning, it's an hour. Takes a while to build up, too. Still worth it.)","text":"I think you're underestimating the number of memory redirections that are necessary for chaining. It's more than one. That's the killer. It's important to be wary of assuming your data will be in cache. It usually isn't. The problem isn't making lots of high density searches in a hash map, the problem is making a search every now and then. A hash map that makes three DRAM accesses will always be worse that one that makes one, regardless of how much work it needs to do, and this is for two reasons. First is there obvious: RAM is slow as balls, we get it. Second is more insidious. You just evicted two cache lines. It's not just the lookup itself that's slower, you also made the consumer of your data structure slower. Here's a look at what open addressing looks like these days: [VIDEO] (warning, it's an hour. Takes a while to build up, too. Still worth it.)"},{"id":44618596,"author":"SkiFire13","month":"2025-07","story":"How we tracked down a Go 1.24 memory regression","stance":"rec","why":"Good presentation on Swisstable","quote":"this really good Cppcon presentation on Swisstable","text":"In addition to this comment's siblings resources, I also suggest this really good Cppcon presentation on Swisstable [VIDEO]"},{"id":41723634,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2024-10","story":"Hash Ordering and Hyrum's Law","stance":"rec","why":"Design and rollout of Google's hash table with Hyrum's law objections","quote":"Is a fun presentation by Matthew Kulukundis (designer of Google's Hash Table)","text":"[VIDEO] Is a fun presentation by Matthew Kulukundis (designer of Google's Hash Table) with Hyrum Wright offering objections from the crowd (Hyrum's law) about the design and rollout of it at Google"},{"id":28505183,"author":"yakubin","month":"2021-09","story":"Transmission torrent client ported to C++","stance":"rec","why":"Shows pitfalls of std::unordered_map and its indirections","quote":"The whole talk is interesting to watch.","text":"STL has a lot of weird pitfalls. There was std::vector<bool> . Here you can see some pitfalls of std::unordered_map : < [VIDEO] >. The whole talk is interesting to watch. In the beginning you can also see the puzzling design of std::unordered_map (puzzling because of the number of indirections). I'd reach for abseil first: < [LINK] >."},{"id":22469593,"author":"vthriller","month":"2020-03","story":"When Bloom filters don't bloom","stance":"rec","why":"Explains SwissTable design decisions","quote":"There's a good talk from CppCon [0] that explains its design decisions","text":"May I suggest you to have a look at Swiss Tables? There's a good talk from CppCon [0] that explains its design decisions, and implementations available as part of Abseil [1] and Rust stdlib [2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20753471,"author":"kllrnohj","month":"2019-08","story":"Bulk Data Structures C++","stance":"rec","why":"Dense flat hash set design","quote":"There's a good talk about such a dense, flat hash set here","text":"This is where we get into O(1) != fast territory. Algorithmic complexity has a weak relationship to CPU performance, not a strong one. If you want to find something in a set storing it as an array and doing a linear scan will beat a std::unordered_set up to a shockingly large number of items due to how CPU's work. In particular it's the pointer chasing aspect of std::unordered_set that becomes a problem (an issue shared with _most_ hash set implementations). Remember an unordered_set is not an array of items, it's an array of buckets of items (this is how hash collision is handled). Worse still, those buckets are usually linked lists. It typically can't be speculated effectively and it can't be prefetched effectively, so you become memory latency bound during an un-cached lookup. And memory latency is just shy of absolutely terrible. If you're expecting L1/L2/L3 cache hits on lookups then you're not dealing with vary large sizes probably and you're going to get much better cache density with the flat array than the array-of-buckets. There are alternative hashsets that are flat and avoid this, but they are less common and as far as I know no standard implementation on any language uses such a hash set. There's a good talk about such a dense, flat hash set here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19761094,"author":"nwallin","month":"2019-04","story":"Open-sourcing F14 for memory-efficient hash tables","stance":"rec","why":"Abseil's SwissTable presentation","quote":"abseil presentation: It's super interesting.","text":"They are very, very similar. The hashes are two level: one is fairly traditional. You take the first k bits from the hash and look that up in a table. The second layer is 128 bit blocks. (one SSE/NEON register) This block contains 14 8 bit blocks, where the top bit is a tombstone marker, and the bottom 7 bits are the k+1 though k+7 bit from the hash. (or the top 7 bits? not sure. it's 7 bits from the hash that aren't used in the top layer) It uses SSE instructions to identify which of the 14 blocks to do a full key compare with. Both of them have two variants, one which guarantees that references to keys and values will remain valid even if iterators are invalidated, and another which is faster but iterator invalidation invalidates references to keys or values. IMHO it looks like the Folly team saw the Abseil presentation last year and said, \"That's a good idea, we should do that.\" abseil presentation: [VIDEO] It's super interesting."},{"id":48673295,"author":"ozgrakkurt","month":"2026-06","story":"Lies, Damn Lies and Database Benchmarks","stance":"reference","why":"Example of useful benchmarking and optimization","quote":"The swisstable talk on cppcon is a good example of a useful benchmark and optimization","text":"The dataset they use is <14GB of parquet [1] so the \"cold start\" seems to be intended to also measure having a dataset that doesn't fit in memory in a way. I don't think this is an oversight but it is just what they found to be feasible. This is explicitly written in [1]. Also the guy who setup this benchmark is very serious about benchmarking under difficult conditions [2] My personal opinion is that you need a massive amount of data and massive number of different variables to test for separately. For example you might want to monitor how many cache misses/hits there were, p99 latency etc. And you want to do it under full load, expected load etc. And you want to compare the different versions of the same database because comparing different databases makes things combinatorially more difficult, unless you have a real production use case that you are optimizing for ofc. The swisstable talk on cppcon is a good example of a useful benchmark and optimization that shows how difficult it is to really asses performance effects of even \"small\" changes. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43127607,"author":"jrimbault","month":"2025-02","story":"Swiss Tables","stance":"reference","why":"Full talk on Swiss Tables","quote":"","text":"Not a couple of sentences CppCon 2017: Matt Kulukundis “Designing a Fast, Efficient, Cache-friendly Hash Table, Step by Step” : [VIDEO]"},{"id":34668034,"author":"kouteiheika","month":"2023-02","story":"Why I Use C When I Believe in Memory Safety","stance":"reference","why":"Performance graphs of SwissTable","quote":"Rust's hash map is based on Google's SwissTable","text":"> I've implemented a hash map in my code. I loved the experience, and I know more about computers and hash maps now that I've done it. Yep, everyone at least once should implement a hash map. Hash maps are so fundamental and so often used that it really pays off to understand what makes them tick. > And my hash map was faster than the one in the C++ STL last I checked. I haven't benchmarked it against the Rust one, though. The one in C++ is very famous for being really slow, so this is a low bar to clear. (: Not sure if someone directly compared Rust's hash map to C++'s, but Rust's hash map is based on Google's SwissTable, so you can probably assume it performs roughly the same. Here are some graphs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29319967,"author":"Koffiepoeder","month":"2021-11","story":"A Tale of Java Hash Tables","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers pointer stability's effect on adoption","quote":"I've watched some of the cppcon talks on the google hashtable implementation [0]","text":"Could someone explain to me in a clear and high level fashion what exactly pointer stability (or pointer instability for that regards) entails? I've watched some of the cppcon talks on the google hashtable implementation [0] and in that talk Matt Kulukundis also mentioned it affecting the adoption rate, but I always failed to really grasp the concept and its implications. Google searches also ended up rather fruitless to be honest. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23616896,"author":"bluestreak","month":"2020-06","story":"Show HN: Query 1.6B rows in milliseconds, live","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the SwissTable hash table","quote":"","text":"Author here. A few weeks ago, we wrote about how we implemented SIMD instructions to aggregate a billion rows in milliseconds [1] thanks in great part to Agner Fog’s VCL library [2]. Although the initial scope was limited to table-wide aggregates into a unique scalar value, this was a first step towards very promising results on more complex aggregations. With the latest release of QuestDB, we are extending this level of performance to key-based aggregations. To do this, we implemented Google’s fast hash table aka “Swisstable” [3] which can be found in the Abseil library [4]. In all modesty, we also found room to slightly accelerate it for our use case. Our version of Swisstable is dubbed “rosti”, after the traditional Swiss dish [5]. There were also a number of improvements thanks to techniques suggested by the community such as prefetch (which interestingly turned out to have no effect in the map code itself) [6]. Besides C++, we used our very own queue system written in Java to parallelise the execution [7]. The results are remarkable: millisecond latency on keyed aggregations that span over billions of rows. We thought it could be a good occasion to show our progress by making this latest release available to try online with a pre-loaded dataset. It runs on an AWS instance using 23 threads. The data is stored on disk and includes a 1.6billion row NYC taxi dataset, 10 years of weather data with around 30-minute resolution and weekly gas prices over the last decade. The ins"},{"id":21750111,"author":"abhishekjha","month":"2019-12","story":"Why databases use ordered indexes but programming uses hash tables","stance":"reference","why":"Source on standard requirements slowing hash maps","quote":"","text":"Apparently the standard imposes a set of requirements that the implementation must abide by thus making it a lot slower. [0] : [LINK] [1] : [LINK] [2] : [VIDEO]"},{"id":19444901,"author":"gopher_protocol","month":"2019-03","story":"Why Hashbrown Does a Double Lookup","stance":"reference","why":"Reasoning behind SwissTable design choices","quote":"they explained the reasoning behind their choices in this CppCon talk","text":"Open addressing has better cache performance, and for most workloads is much faster than chaining implementations. Hashbrown's implementation is based on Google's SwissTable, and they explained the reasoning behind their choices in this CppCon talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18290635,"author":"utopcell","month":"2018-10","story":"Swiss Tables and absl::Hash","stance":"reference","why":"Google's presentation of the SwissTable design","quote":"Google presented the SwissTable design more than a year ago in CppCon [1]","text":"I believe the design is similar because Google presented the SwissTable design more than a year ago in CppCon [1]. If I'm reading the link you sent correctly, then there was an initial bias in the benchmarks that favored F14, but after adjusting for that, the results are similar (not surprising since the basic idea is the same.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28508239,"author":"josefx","month":"2021-09","story":"Transmission torrent client ported to C++","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Some good points, but also","text":"> [VIDEO] Some good points, but also \"calling find with C strings is slow\", you are using C strings, don't expect speed from code worshiping strlen of all things. Also the issue with integer hash being its identity? That is the case for every language I checked (Python, Java, C#)."}],"r":[["hgN8pCMLI2U",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["oM6S7FEUfkU",1],["cfyWvJdsDRI",1],["kTiP0zKF9bc",1],["yG1OZ69H_-o",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["HVv_IQKlafQ",1]]},"Ip3X9LOh2dk":{"wy":"HN repeatedly points people to it as the clearest visual intuition for determinants, often saying it finally made the concept click.","q":{"x":"I have warmed up to determinants ever since I saw 3Blue1Brown give a fairly intuitive explanation for them","i":40188059,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42108671,"author":"traes","month":"2024-11","story":"Adventures in Probability","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intuitive graphical explanation of determinants as area and volume scaling.","quote":"The entire 3blue1brown series[0] on linear algebra is well worth watching, it has really intuitive graphical explanations of a bunch of concepts.","text":"The entire 3blue1brown series[0] on linear algebra is well worth watching, it has really intuitive graphical explanations of a bunch of concepts. Here's the one on determinants in particular[1]. TL;DW the determinant represents how much you scale the area/volume/hypervolume (depending on dimension) of a shape by applying a matrix transformation to each point. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34655336,"author":"meken","month":"2023-02","story":"2000 Years of Matrix Multiplication","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gives intuition for determinants; the commenter's mind was blown.","quote":"now my mind is absolutely blown!","text":"Determinants always fascinated me, partly (completely?) because of the cool sounding name. I always wanted to get intuition for them because their formula is so simple, which has always been deeply unsatisfying to me to just take on its face. This article got me searching for a 3blue1brown video on determinants and now my mind is absolutely blown! [VIDEO]"},{"id":24156931,"author":"MaxBarraclough","month":"2020-08","story":"Expiring vs. permanent skills","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduces determinants through geometry first, with excellent visualisations.","quote":"The 3Blue1Brown YouTube channel does a great job with this, introducing the determinant in terms of geometry.","text":"At the same time, universities could sometimes do a better job of letting students see why it's important to learn something. A great example is matrix determinant, which at first glance seems like an entirely arbitrary invention. The 3Blue1Brown YouTube channel does a great job with this, introducing the determinant in terms of geometry. It puts the geometric meaning of the function first, introducing the strange looking formula later on. edit And of course, it does all of this with excellent visualisations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23804881,"author":"Chris_Newton","month":"2020-07","story":"How to Understand Things","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds real intuition for determinants in about ten minutes with visuals.","quote":"here is what a gifted presenter with the right illustrations can do in about ten minutes","text":"As someone who very much relates to the GP’s anecdote, I might suggest determinants as a good example. As an undergraduate studying maths, I encountered a standard theorem in one of my first courses, which says that about 947 different conditions are equivalent to a matrix having a determinant of zero. I dutifully memorised these. I also dutifully memorised the algorithm for how to calculate a determinant. I might even have remembered some verbatim proofs of some of the equivalences. However, I developed absolutely no intuition about what a determinant is . I had book knowledge, but no insight. It was a long time ago now, but I’m fairly sure that when I graduated I still did not truly understand even this very basic (by undergraduate standards) subject. I think it was probably a few years later, when I came across some of the same theory but in a much more practical context at work, that most of the connections in that equivalence theorem first “clicked”. Meanwhile, here is what a gifted presenter with the right illustrations can do in about ten minutes: [VIDEO] The 2,000 or so substantially identical comments below that video are very telling. Given the understanding you’d get with that quality of presentation, the equivalences I mentioned above would have been obvious and constructing the proofs from first principles would have been straightforward."},{"id":40188059,"author":"3PS","month":"2024-04","story":"So what's the point of linear algebra, anyway?","stance":"rec","why":"Fairly intuitive explanation of determinants that changed the commenter's view.","quote":"I have warmed up to determinants ever since I saw 3Blue1Brown give a fairly intuitive explanation for them","text":"> The novel approach taken here banishes determinants to the end of the book. Big fan of this approach! Though I have warmed up to determinants ever since I saw 3Blue1Brown give a fairly intuitive explanation for them [0]. I'm kind of curious as to how they covered eigenvalues/the characteristic polynomial without determinants. Maybe they just jumped straight to diagonalization? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33404462,"author":"JW_00000","month":"2022-10","story":"The paper that keeps showing up","stance":"rec","why":"Gave the commenter first real understanding of what a determinant represents.","quote":"I didn't intuitively understand what a determinant represented or what the determinant of a transformation being zero really meant, until watching [6].","text":"To add to this, a website I've found useful is betterexplained.com. Specifically for complex numbers, take a look at [1] and [2]. But they also have good articles with visualizations to help you understand the Fourier transform, the number e, integrals and derivatives, linear algebra, etc. A starting point is [3]. One useful idea is that they color the different parts of math equations to explain each part separately, see [4]. Another great source of math visualizations is the YouTube channel 3Blue1Brown, in particular the series on linear algebra [5]. (I didn't intuitively understand what a determinant represented or what the determinant of a transformation being zero really meant, until watching [6].) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19732946,"author":"throwawaymath","month":"2019-04","story":"Relearning Matrices as Linear Functions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I strongly disagree skipping determinants provides a more intuitive approach to linear algebra. I don't know your background, but I'd venture a guess you feel it does because the Laplace expansion formula for computing the determinant[1] feels uninspired and out of place. The reason determinants are hard to teach (in my opinion) is because a rigorous derivation of their formula isn't possible without first teaching multilinear algebra and constructing the exterior algebra. Once you do those things, the natural geometric interpretation of the determinant basically falls onto your lap. But it's still very useful for e.g. computing eigenvalues and using the characteristic polynomial, so it's taught before that context can be formalized. Professors shouldn't teach determinants in the context of matrices, at least not at first. That's heavily computation-focused, and the symbol pushing looks really unmotivated and strange to students. Instead they should teach the basis-free definition of determinants (i.e. focus on the linear map, not the matrix transformation representing the linear map for some basis). Then the determinant is \"only\" the volume of the image of the unit hypercube under the linear transformation, which is where the parallelepiped comes in. If the linear transformation is invertible, the unit hypercube is transformed from an n -dimensional cube into an n -dimensional parallelogram, from which you can geometrically see the way the linear map transforms the entire ve"}],"r":[["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["GqmsQeSzMdw",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["QKCK4lJLQHU",1],["Z4oYSByyRak",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"VscdPA6sUkc":{"wy":"Frequently recommended whenever Diablo or its origins come up, for its candid account of the human side of game development.","q":{"x":"heartily recommended if you want to know about the human aspect behind game dev","i":36816804,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36816804,"author":"baristavibes","month":"2023-07","story":"Diablo","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the human side of game development.","quote":"heartily recommended if you want to know about the human aspect behind game dev","text":"This story and many others are captured really well in the Game Developer Conference (GDC) post-mortem talks, heartily recommended if you want to know about the human aspect behind game dev!! [VIDEO]"},{"id":16690860,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2018-03","story":"The original pitch for Diablo (1994) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brevik tells how Diablo became real-time and birthed the ARPG.","quote":"enjoy the whole video","text":"Allow me to quote Diablo developer David Brevik describing when he finally yielded to demands and hacked together a \"real time\" version of his turn-based Diablo, by making turns elapse the rate of 20 turns a second. 'I remember taking the mouse, and I clicked on the mouse, and the warrior walked over and and smacked the skeleton down, and I was like \"Oh my god! That was awesome!\".' 'And the sun shone through the window, and God passed by, and the angels sung, and sure enough that was when the ARPG was kind of born at that moment, and I was lucky enough to be there.' [VIDEO] If you're in this part of this comment thread, you'll probably enjoy the whole video."},{"id":36349348,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2023-06","story":"Diablo Pitch Document (1994) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Brevik's GDC 2016 postmortem on what happened next.","quote":"To find out what happened next, check out Diablo developer David Brevik","text":"To find out what happened next, check out Diablo developer David Brevik’s postmortem presentation at GDC 2016: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26202781,"author":"somedude895","month":"2021-02","story":"Diablo II: Resurrected","stance":"rec","why":"Fantastic postmortem on Diablo 1 development.","quote":"post mortem on D1 from GDC by the way","text":"That's an interesting thought, but I'd assume 20 year old 3D models wouldn't be much use today, would they? I know they were thinking about making D2 a 3D game at first, like Runescape, but then went with 2D, because 3D technology wasn't at a point that allowed them to carry over the gritty and dark theme they wanted for Diablo. And then went on to make D3 look as cartoony as it does... They used photos of clay figurines for Diablo 1, would be fun if they remade that with 3D scanning techniques. Do check out David Brevik's post mortem on D1 from GDC by the way, it's fantastic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23991009,"author":"wcarss","month":"2020-07","story":"The Secret Origin of the Action RPG","stance":"rec","why":"Story of Diablo becoming real-time and other anecdotes.","quote":"a pretty excellent post mortem at GDC","text":"Diablo was far from the first ARPG, but given how influential it has been for the genre, I was floored to learn that it was actually designed and implemented as a turn-based game, and only turned into an Action RPG by request of folks at the main Blizzard office. The founder of Blizzard North (David Brevik) thought it was a dumb idea, and only agreed to do it because it seemed like a large enough work item to justify requesting an additional budget milestone from Blizzard, which his office was hurting for sorely. Brevik then got it working over the course of roughly an afternoon, by just running the turn system automatically and responding to clicks a little differently. It was only after seeing the action-oriented click-walk-attack-click flow actually work for the first time that he realized: they'd struck gold. He told this story (and lots of others!) in a pretty excellent post mortem at GDC[1] a few years ago. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17340611,"author":"oraknabo","month":"2018-06","story":"Diablo devolved – magic behind the 1996 computer game","stance":"rec","why":"Gives new respect for Diablo, including its turn-based origins.","quote":"I love the idea that it was originally a dungeon crawl inspired by X-COM","text":"I was never a huge Diablo fan, but I could always appreciate the things people liked about it. I gained new respect for it from watching Brevik's GDC postmortem [0] from a couple of years ago. I love the idea that it was originally a dungeon crawl inspired by X-COM and would love to play one of the early builds before they changed to real-time combat. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17340615,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-06","story":"Diablo devolved – magic behind the 1996 computer game","stance":"rec","why":"Detail on Diablo's origin, turn-based to real-time, Blizzard North.","quote":"the GDC post mortem is well worth a watch","text":"For anyone interested in the history of Diablo, the GDC post mortem is well worth a watch: [VIDEO] Quite a bit of detail regarding the origin of the game, going turn-based to real-time, the studio becoming Blizzard North, etc. Also here's the original design doc: [LINK]"},{"id":16690789,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-03","story":"The original pitch for Diablo (1994) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Origins of Diablo and Blizzard North, with amusing Q&A.","quote":"Worth watching for any fans of the original","text":"David Brevik released that just after the Diablo postmortem from GDC a couple years ago: [VIDEO] Worth watching for any fans of the original and if you want more context around that pitch. Lots of interesting tidbits about the origins of Diablo and Blizzard North. And an amusing moment during the Q&A: Someone in the audience went up and gave David some money to make up for pirating the game when he was a kid."},{"id":16279801,"author":"doikor","month":"2018-02","story":"Diablo creator David Brevik is back with a new game: It Lurks Below","stance":"rec","why":"Postmortem covering Diablo's origins and Blizzard North.","quote":"you really should check the postmortem from the GDC a few years back","text":"Brevik was one of the founders Condor (Brevik was the president while the Schaefer brothers were vice-presidents) which later became Blizzard North when Blizzard bought them. The work on Diablo started before Blizzard bought them. He really was one of the \"creators\". IIRC they made the first Diablo game with a team of 15-20 people. In a team that small pretty much everyone is a \"game designer\" as in everyone wears many hats (especially the president of the company) If you are interested you really should check the postmortem from the GDC a few years back [VIDEO]"},{"id":36349271,"author":"bexsella","month":"2023-06","story":"Diablo Pitch Document (1994) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the story about Diablo going real-time.","quote":"","text":"My favourite story of Diablo's development (as relayed by David Brevik in the Diablo Postmortem - see below) is the part where Diablo was made real-time. He passionately championed that it should be more like Rogue or Moria purely turn-based and permadeath. That was until he implemented real-time movement in an afternoon and attacked a skeleton. This was a directive from Blizzard, and it effectively created the ARPG genre. [VIDEO] and for those adverse to Google: [LINK]"},{"id":20210339,"author":"bdowling","month":"2019-06","story":"Level Design and Shaping a Roguelike Experience","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Diablo's real-time mode on a turn-based core.","quote":"","text":"You picked the wrong time to use that example, considering that Nintendo will soon release Super Mario Maker 2, a new game which includes a mode with virtually unchanged mechanics from Super Mario Bros 3. [LINK] Regarding Roguelikes, the biggest change was Diablo in 1996. Diablo was arguably a Roguelike with fancy graphics and a real-time mode hacked onto a turn-based core. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17850261,"author":"shoo","month":"2018-08","story":"Game Programming Patterns: Double Buffer (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses isometric tile rendering among other fascinating topics.","quote":"a whole lot of other fascinating stuff","text":"there's a bit of discussion about isometric tile rendering nonsense, although not specific to vsync, in David Brevik's diablo postmortem [VIDEO] along with a whole lot of other fascinating stuff"},{"id":16279542,"author":"stateoff","month":"2018-02","story":"Diablo creator David Brevik is back with a new game: It Lurks Below","stance":"reference","why":"Brevik describes in his own words inventing the real-time RPG.","quote":"","text":"In this case he really did invent the \"Realtime-RPG\" that made Diablo what it is. In his own words: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16047724,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among favorite talks of 2017.","quote":"","text":"* To Serve The People: Public Interest Technologists. Matt Mitchell is a hacker, security researcher, operational security trainer, and data journalist who founded & leads CryptoHarlem, impromptu workshops teaching basic cryptography tools to the predominately African American community in upper Manhattan. [LINK] * Stop Rate Limiting! Capacity Management Done Right. Jon Moore is the Chief Software Architect at Comcast Cable, where he focuses on delivering a core set of scalable, performant, robust software components for the company's varied software product development groups. [LINK] * Keeping Time in Real Systems. This talk will tour the fascinating timekeeping mechanisms used in real systems. We will explore atomic clocks, NTP and GPS through systems that use them, and logical clocks in the context of systems built on logical time. [LINK] * Level Up Your Concurrency Skills With Rust. This talk will show you how Rust will catch many concurrency errors at compile time. [LINK] * Diablo: A Classic Game Postmortem. Diablo developer David Brevik returns to the GDC stage to give a classic post-mortem on Blizzard's action RPG hit Diablo in this 2016 talk. [VIDEO] * The Flash Games Postmortem . In this 2017 GDC talk, Kongregate's John Cooney attempts to encapsulate the immense history of Flash games and how it has shaped the current game industry by giving game developers their first chance to build and publish their games quickly to the web. [LINK] * Siege Battle AI in Total War: "}],"r":[["9KABcmczPdg",1],["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["03p2CADwGF8",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["s_fkpZSnz2I",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["yReDbeY7ZMU",1],["dpw9EHDh2bM",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["FBaVwwuErmU",1],["MijmeoH9LT4",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["T2OxO-4YLRk",1],["Mlrrc_vy79E",1],["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1]]},"RNFesa01llk":{"wy":"HN repeatedly cites it as the best skeptical engineering analysis of why Hyperloop is impractical.","q":{"x":"His videos on the hyperloop are pretty comprehensive and succinct about their reasoning.","i":12293321,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16195747,"author":"Red_Tarsius","month":"2018-01","story":"Hyperloop costs and challenges undermine pinned hopes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Skeptical analysis of Hyperloop from a physicist","quote":"I highly recommend his whole Busted! series.","text":"British scientist Philip E. Mason ( thunderfoot ) made several skeptical videos on Hyperloop. [VIDEO] I highly recommend his whole Busted! series."},{"id":12293321,"author":"computerex","month":"2016-08","story":"How Hyperloop One Went Off the Rails","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Comprehensive, succinct reasoning on why the hyperloop is flawed.","quote":"His videos on the hyperloop are pretty comprehensive and succinct about their reasoning.","text":"Thunderf00t isn't just some average Joe on the street. The guy has a PhD in chemistry and has dedicated a decade to educate the world at large about matters of science and intellectualism. The world would be a better place if we had more Thunderf00ts on YouTube. His videos on the hyperloop are pretty comprehensive and succinct about their reasoning. Why not watch them instead of mindlessly repeating the same strawman argument that every other Elon Musk fanboy/fangirl left on his videos? [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15458333,"author":"LaikaF","month":"2017-10","story":"Hyperloop One Becomes ‘Virgin Hyperloop One’","stance":"rec","why":"Problems with the Hyperloop concept","quote":"Thunderf00t has a good video on the problems with it.","text":"Thunderf00t has a good video on the problems with it. [VIDEO] He has some other good videos (solar roadways) but tends to spend to much time bashing creationists."},{"id":13518127,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2017-01","story":"SpaceX is livestreaming a hyperloop pod competition","stance":"rec","why":"Technical criticisms of Hyperloop feasibility","quote":"Thunderf00t has an interesting video","text":"If you haven't seen the technical criticisms of Hyperloop, Thunderf00t has an interesting video: [VIDEO] I haven't seen most of the issues he raises addressed."},{"id":12901021,"author":"Randgalt","month":"2016-11","story":"Hyperloop may become reality in Dubai","stance":"rec","why":"Strong arguments against the hyperloop's technical practicality.","quote":"Worth watching as he makes good arguments.","text":"The tone and sarcasm of this video aside, the guy makes strong arguments that the Hyperloop cannot work technically. Worth watching as he makes good arguments. [VIDEO] edit: actually it can work technically, but it's not practical at all"},{"id":12708927,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2016-10","story":"Hyperloop One Raises $50M, Hires Former Uber CFO","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes the major challenges facing the hyperloop.","quote":"Thunderf00t did a good job summarizing the problems","text":"I'm happy for them, but I'm yet to hear for anybody to address the major challenges that many people have raised. The major one being - the sudden pressurization in any point and the consequent shockwave traveling at the speed of sound, exploding the tube, killing everything in its way. Thunderf00t did a good job summarizing the problems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12709690,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2016-10","story":"Hyperloop One Raises $50M, Hires Former Uber CFO","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the hyperloop; deserves more attention.","quote":"I think this needs to be brought to more people's attention","text":"I think this needs to be brought to more people's attention, so I'm putting this in a top level comment: Thunderf00t's video: [VIDEO] Debunked: [LINK] Also debunked: [LINK]"},{"id":12832739,"author":"problems","month":"2016-10","story":"Hyperloop Freight is a Joke","stance":"rec","why":"Best legitimate criticisms of vacuum tube transport.","quote":"The best legitimate criticisms I've seen are here","text":"The best legitimate criticisms I've seen are here: [VIDEO] Basically expansion and contraction as well as just plain vulnerability of running a vacuum make it extremely difficult to do practically. The phrase he uses, \"all the problems of space travel except in a gun barrel\" is a pretty accurate description of the issue. Any loss of vacuum will almost guarantee loss of life. No realistic demo has been done even on a small scale as of yet."},{"id":38729572,"author":"mynameisnoone","month":"2023-12","story":"Hyperloop One to shut down","stance":"reference","why":"Vacuum trains impractical with current materials and too expensive","quote":"The problem with vacuum trains is they aren't workable with current materials, are impractical, and are too damn expensive.","text":"Apples and oranges false equivocation of vastly different engineering problems. The problem with vacuum trains is they aren't workable with current materials, are impractical, and are too damn expensive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38735039,"author":"junon","month":"2023-12","story":"The Hyperloop was always a scam","stance":"reference","why":"Related Thunderf00t videos on Hyperloop","quote":"Related thunderf00t videos","text":"Related thunderf00t videos: [LINK] and [VIDEO] Those are for Elon's; Virgin also has a hyperloop which he's covered too: [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":19754310,"author":"gitgud","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: Why do so many people want to see Tesla fail?","stance":"reference","why":"Debunking essay on why Hyperloop is not a good invention","quote":"","text":"related topic: Elon's \"Hyperloop\" project has gained a lot of criticism too. Many [1] long video essays have been made to debunk why it's not a good invention. Now the hype has died down considerably and it seems a lot of people have lost faith in the idea... maybe they're losing faith in all of Musk's ventures... [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15519733,"author":"mozumder","month":"2017-10","story":"Boring Co. gets permission to begin digging in Maryland","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't know why they're doing this, except out of pure tech-broism. It's obviously not financially viable. Let's say it realistically costs $150 million a mile for the project (about average for tunnel boring - it'll actually be a lot more on the east coast due to it being solid rock and expensive labor, but let's be realistically optimistic). At 250 miles, that's $37.5 Billion right there. Let's be ridiculously generous and finance this over 50 years. Over 50 years, that's $750 million/year. Or, $2million/day. At $60/ticket, you need 34k daily riders. And that's only for building the tunnel. I haven't even factored in operating or finance costs, nevermind the millions of other expenses that this needs. Stations are normally the most expensive part of a system, for example, and a station in NYC might cost $1 billion by itself. Is this going to get 34,000 daily riders paying $60/ticket to NYC? I can see that happening with $10/ticket, but not at $60/ticket. For comparison, the entire DC Metro system itself has about 800k daily riders with about $5/trip. And then you have the stupid HyperLoop concept itself, which hasn't even been invented yet. It's based on small single-car vehicles, which may carry a couple dozen people at most. Already you can see capacity issues: To get 34,000 people a day 24-7, you need 1400 fully-packed rides a day, or one every 6 seconds. You're not going to get single-vehicles to share a track every six seconds. You're already capacity limited. How do "},{"id":14579016,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2017-06","story":"An 89-year-old Reinventing the Train in His Backyard","stance":"reference","why":"Pressure and speed problems of Hyperloop, relevant segments timestamped","quote":"he does make his point. Relevant bits start about 6m22s.","text":"1. Pressure. 2. Speed. 1600 km/s is pretty snappy. Thunderf00t tends to wibble on a bit and repeat repeat himself, but he does make his point. Relevant bits start about 6m22s. [LINK] Or at 10m28s in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14224881,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2017-04","story":"The Boring Company [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Practical and safety reasons a vacuum train is infeasible","quote":"It is infeasible, if it's going to be vacuum, for a pile of practical and safety reasons","text":"It is infeasible, if it's going to be vacuum, for a pile of practical and safety reasons: [VIDEO] Maybe it will pivot into something not-as-originally-hyped by the same name."},{"id":27412537,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2021-06","story":"Elon O Meter","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Elon wields a powerful influence, a cult following. Fault is a fuzzy concept, not restricted to binary blame. Combine the poor taste of Elon fucking around with a monetary instrument, and his cult risking their life savings on his every word, and grave losses are the result. People commit suicide all the time because of large investment losses. Don't be too quick to doubt the monetary and biological loss in the US and abroad his statements likely caused. Your argument hinges on Elon not being the \"hand that pulls the trigger.\" It's really irrelevant as everyone on HN is intelligent enough to understand that Elon is responsible for his reckless persuasions of a populace that is on average, stupid. Yes, the average person is... stupid. Especially ones who worship this man. Here is just a fraction of Elon's record: [1] Pedo Tweet: [LINK] [2] Hyperloop Scam: [VIDEO] [3] Boring Tunnel Scam: [LINK] [4] Autopilot Scam (11 confirmed deaths - should be called Enhanced Cruise Control instead): [LINK] [5] Founder of PayPal, a platform that has been freezing and stealing user's funds since 1998: [LINK] [6] Not even the founder of Tesla. He was an investor (using his grifted Paypal money - how many frozen accounts did it take?): [LINK] [6] Anti-union: [LINK] [7] Does not care for wellbeing of employees: [LINK] Elon is not Tony Stark or Iron Man. He is not a hero. He is not a villain. He is a highly intelligent man with many blind spots and a penchant for rash, poorly judged decisions. For"},{"id":17879057,"author":"pokemongoaway","month":"2018-08","story":"Elon Musk's Solution for Dodger Stadium Traffic Is Full of Holes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"A lot of his ideas are full of holes: Hyperloop: See all of the problems here: [VIDEO] Boring company: summary of issues here: [LINK] SpaceX: How will we fund the amount of rocket fuel required to get the stuff to Mars that can produce an atmosphere? If producing an atmosphere isn't required, then how will indoor atmosphere be maintained sustainably (financially)? Matt Damon's math didn't seem to work out in that movie... Tesla: So far so good, but what about battery recycling? “We are at the very beginning in energy storage in general,” says Phil Hermann, chief energy engineer at Panasonic Eco Solutions. “Most of the projects currently going on are either demo projects or learning experiences for the utilities. There is very little direct commercial stuff going on. “Elon Musk is out there saying you can do things now that the rest of us are hearing and going, ‘really?’ We wish we could but it’s not really possible yet.” [LINK] SolarCity: Just look at the customer reviews so far: [LINK] I would urge Mr Musk to focus on Batteries & long-term Tesla ownership. It would be a shame for that not to work out! I hope this comment lasts for 5 years :)"}],"r":[["AUaJ8pDlxi8",1],["n691pLhQBkw",1],["PsPX0nElJ0k",1],["rE3j_RHkqJc",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["1AcEt073Uds",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["IrbJYsep45E",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["wJWksPWDKOc",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1]]},"eMmNy11Mn7g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as well-researched reporting on skin gambling, especially its effect on minors, in threads about gambling and Valve.","q":{"x":"More people need to see interviews such as those in the People Make Games video","i":35680814,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35680814,"author":"crtasm","month":"2023-04","story":"Ban all gambling adverts, say more than half of Britons","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interviews more people need to see","quote":"More people need to see interviews such as those in the People Make Games video","text":"More people need to see interviews such as those in the People Make Games video \"How Valve is Profiting from Steam's Back-Door Casinos\" - skip to 15mins for them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46368013,"author":"crtasm","month":"2025-12","story":"Meta is using the Linux scheduler designed for Valve's Steam Deck on its servers","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Valve's ties to third-party gambling","quote":"If you have 30mins for a video I recommend People Make Games' documentary on it","text":"Their games and systems tie into huge gambling operations on 3rd party sites If you have 30mins for a video I recommend People Make Games' documentary on it [VIDEO]"},{"id":43094628,"author":"pityJuke","month":"2025-02","story":"Valve releases Team Fortress 2 code","stance":"rec","why":"Video resource on skin gambling and minors","quote":"Some video resources that might be useful","text":"> Underage gambling in the sense of spending real money to redeem things that may be impossible to redeem without spending it? Critically, Valve allows you to trade items. This results in a couple of downstream effects: 1. Items have real-world value because they can be traded for money outside of Steam. Multiple sites exist for people to convert items into real-world money (certain rare items have been sold for >$1m [0]). 2. As these items have value, they can serve as a surrogate for money in casinos, or for sports betting. 3. This can even lead to money laundering [1]. As such, skins should be considered money, but the sites running these services don't. Therefore, it is trivial for a child to walk into a game store, buy Steam credit, use that credit to buy skins, and then spend that money on literal gambling (as very few sites have KYC). I know because I've actively partaken in it as a child. Even cryptocurrency is harder: most legitimate exchanges attempt to do identity validation. Some video resources that might be useful: - Coffeezilla: [LINK] - People Make Games: [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":38212939,"author":"drngdds","month":"2023-11","story":"Steam Deck OLED","stance":"rec","why":"Item gambling on unregulated sites allowing minors","quote":"Good video on it","text":"It's not even just loot boxes, but them letting people gamble with the items (which can be worth a lot of money!) on unregulated gambling sites that allow minors. It's insidious stuff. Good video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35292486,"author":"follower","month":"2023-03","story":"Facebook is aggressively going after LLaMA repos with DMCA's","stance":"rec","why":"Original research and interviews on Valve gambling","quote":"the \"People Make Games\" channel on YouTube have a couple of related videos with original research/interviews","text":"For people interested in this topic, the \"People Make Games\" channel on YouTube have a couple of related videos with original research/interviews: * \"How Valve is Profiting from Steam's Back-Door Casinos\": < [VIDEO] > * \"Working at Valve: 'A Fearless Adventure' or 'Lord of the Flies'?\": < [LINK] >"},{"id":33689481,"author":"maxbond","month":"2022-11","story":"A lobbying blitz made sports betting ubiquitous","stance":"rec","why":"Multibillion gray market gambling with cosmetic items","quote":"there's a thriving, multibillion dollar gray market for gambling with cosmetic items in games","text":"> This is inane bullshit. It's a video game. I think you may be unaware of the extent to which players, including children, are engaging in real-money gambling at the periphery of video games. Here's some recent reporting on the topic. [VIDEO] Tl;Dw there's a thriving, multibillion dollar gray market for gambling with cosmetic items in games, as well as a market for trading these items for real money. Together this creates stealth real money casinos."},{"id":33923909,"author":"jerf","month":"2022-12","story":"The Child Is the Teacher: A Life of Maria Montessori","stance":"reference","why":"Used to show kids how games target them","quote":"Recently I was showing my kids how they're being targeted by video game gambling","text":"I went with the total honesty approach. It doesn't seem to have affected them negatively in my case. I mean, I still enjoy Santa Claus to some degree. I don't think thinking it's real is a critical component of the experience. My kids enjoy the elf on the shelf sort of thing, and they were always fully aware the elf is not actually getting up to shenanigans at night. Nevertheless it's fun for the entire family, because it stretches us parents to be creative a little bit every night as well. Most recently the elf was starring as Batman in a three-night, three-act structure in which he menanced Joker, then the tables were turned with the Joker having hijacked the Batmobile and running over Bat-Elf, then there was a climactic showdown involving the Lego train tracks the next night. Nobody needed to think these adventures are actually real to enjoy them. I come in in between; the stories are important for other reasons, but it is in no way mandatory to tell them they're real. Maybe in the 19th century it was less of a big deal, reality was more real in a lot of ways. But in a world of deep fakes, CGI, incredibly organized special interest groups comparable to the size of entire powerful medieval cities a few centuries ago, etc. etc. I need all the trust I can get if I'm going to train them to survive in the unbelievably hostile environment they're trying to grow up in now. Recently I was showing my kids how they're being targeted by video game gambling: [VIDEO] and I was musing o"},{"id":33626793,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2022-11","story":"Genesis' Crypto-Lending Unit Is Suspending Withdrawals in Wake of FTX Collapse","stance":"reference","why":"Steam skin trading created a gambling black market","quote":"Steam makes it possible, but this has also created a gambling black market","text":"Steam makes it possible, but this has also created a gambling black market: [VIDEO] Anything that can be easily traded for currency can also act as currency so you quickly fall down the KYC rabbit hole if you want to - for example - keep children from gambling."}],"r":[["aY985qzn7oI",1],["lC4YLMLar5I",1],["jQIHqkudgNY",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["Vj04MKykmnQ",1],["s9aCwCKgkLo",1],["hBmhDf8cl0k",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["kTiP0zKF9bc",1]]},"iFDe5kUUyT0":{"wy":"Recommended, often in Bitcoin and monetary threads, as a clear introduction to how the US money system works, though it comes with a strong goldbug slant.","q":{"x":"This is the best explanation I've ever seen of how the Fed and US Dollar money printing actually works","i":22621783,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24551886,"author":"jondubois","month":"2020-09","story":"The unrealized potential of federation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series arguing the fiat monetary system is a scam.","quote":"Watch \"Hidden Secrets of Money\"","text":"> Cryptocurrency is the digitally woke techbro’s ponzi scheme The fiat monetary system is a ponzi scheme. Watch \"Hidden Secrets of Money\": [VIDEO] I also recommend doing your own research after this. The evidence is clear and overwhelming. Cryptocurrency is merely a response to this scam and its continued success helped to further prove that the financial system is a scam. Even if you assumed that cryptocurrency is a scam, what kind of financial system enables a scam to thrive for 10+ years? In such a system, how can you tell the scams apart from the non-scams? Clearly the so-called 'free markets' are not capable of distinguishing the two. What's to say that most big tech corporations aren't scams? They get a lot of 'free money' from the government through massive contracts to build projects which only add more bureaucracy."},{"id":22621783,"author":"mrnobody_67","month":"2020-03","story":"The Longest Record Broken: Gold/Silver Ratio Hits Highest in Over 5,000 Years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how the Fed and dollar money printing work.","quote":"This is the best explanation I've ever seen of how the Fed and US Dollar money printing actually works","text":"This is the best explanation I've ever seen of how the Fed and US Dollar money printing actually works: [VIDEO] Most people also don't realize that the Fed, which buys Treasury's drawn on a bank account with $0 in it, is privately owned - and distributes dividends to its private shareholders holders (who are not disclosed)."},{"id":18817169,"author":"jcslzr","month":"2019-01","story":"Ten Years of Bitcoin","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues modern money is a scam.","quote":"You don't understand that what we call \"money\" today its a scam","text":"You don't understand that what we call \"money\" today its a scam: [VIDEO] BTC will create prosperity we can not even dream of"},{"id":24607042,"author":"jondubois","month":"2020-09","story":"5k people have pledged to give 10%+ of their income to effective charities","stance":"rec","why":"Facts about monetary policy that checked out for the commenter.","quote":"I had a hard time believing some of the facts presented but all checked out so far.","text":"I recommend reading up on monetary policy and the Federal reserve (and watch videos) and you will understand what I mean and how it fits into Annand's description of the world (it compounds the problem). It's a lot worse than you think. I recommend also doing your own research to verify all the facts. I had a hard time believing some of the facts presented but all checked out so far. Hidden Secrets of Money: [VIDEO] An interesting recent discussion I found which discusses the current state of the economy: [LINK] Actually the second one is eye-opening for anyone who wants to make sense of the current situation."},{"id":10551919,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2015-11","story":"What happens when a culture is driven by the need for money to make more money","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the US money system and debt-free money.","quote":"the video can still be of use as a general introduction into the US money system","text":"To understand what debt-free money is (and to address how the US money system currently works), I'd recommend this video: [VIDEO] Please note that although there are a couple of subtle hints in the video about returning to the gold standard, the video can still be of use as a general introduction into the US money system even if you don't think that's a good idea (I don't think it's a good idea to go back to the gold standard). Hope the video can be useful for you, I know I found it useful. Feel free to ask me any questions about debt-free money (and how it could be implemented) after watching the video."},{"id":8066131,"author":"xiphias","month":"2014-07","story":"Bitcoin: the Stripe perspective","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why fiat currencies are poor stores of value.","quote":"The best explanation is by Mike Maloney","text":"Volatility should never be important when selecting a store of value. Gold and silver has much volatility compared to USD and hamburger price, still if you sold a cow 5000 years ago in Egypt for silver, you could buy at least 0.1 cows from the same amount of silver today. Silver doesn't lose more than 0.01% of its value/year (still the volatility is much higher than that). It's actually more important to understand why USD and CHF are so bad store of value. The best explanation is by Mike Maloney: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35253952,"author":"matt_langston","month":"2023-03","story":"The USA Inflation Rate by Truflation Is 4.21%","stance":"reference","why":"Mike Maloney educational video on inflation.","quote":"I wonder what people think of Mike Maloney's educational video on inflation","text":"I wonder what people think of Mike Maloney's educational video on inflation: \"Inflation: The Biggest Scam In The History Of Mankind\" [VIDEO] Is there anything incorrect in that presentation? John William's shadowstats is mentioned."},{"id":24490353,"author":"cryptica","month":"2020-09","story":"Remembering my father","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Bill Gates should use his money to lobby politicians to address deep systemic issues instead of trying to cover up the symptoms with philanthropy. One of the main problems is the continuous 'printing' of money and the way it is distributed into the economy. It doesn't make sense that all the newly printed money goes to wealthy institutions and individuals first, it doesn't trickle down. If they want to keep the money printer running, then that money needs to go to everyone equally; this means UBI (Universal Basic Income) - Otherwise we'll just end up with the insane politics that we have today; where the rich who are in close proximity to the money printers wield way too much power over politics. I used to have respect for Bill Gates (Jr), but not anymore. If he really cares about others, he should dedicate most of his energy to fixing our corrupt fiat monetary system. Also, he should lobby for government to abolish corporate personhood and the overall concept of limited-liability corporations. The entire financial system is a scam, it's a giant ponzi scheme. Once you do the research, it's impossible to operate properly in this system. There are many very talented, hard working people today who are being pushed out of the system into joblessness because they realize how pointless all the jobs have become and how rigged the system is because of the distortions caused by unlimited money printing. The situation we have is total madness. People are angry and they're right to be b"},{"id":24490507,"author":"cryptica","month":"2020-09","story":"Fuck You Startup World (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Claims the financial system is a giant ponzi scheme.","quote":"the entire financial system within which this ponzi scheme operates is also a giant ponzi scheme","text":"Let's not ignore the 'minor' detail that the entire space is a ponzi scheme to begin with (coming from Chamath Palihapitiya; a billionaire who was one of the first Facebook employees): [LINK] And let's not ignore that the entire financial system within which this ponzi scheme operates is also a giant ponzi scheme: [VIDEO] So the startup world is literally a ponzi scheme within a ponzi scheme."}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1]]},"JLb5hZLw44s":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the full account of the lab mice issue and evolutionary tradeoffs between cancer and longevity, despite its length.","q":{"x":"an evolutionary biologist goes into depth about telomeres, evolution and how cancer and longevity are two sides of the same coin","i":27794399,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28822175,"author":"lettergram","month":"2021-10","story":"Slowed canonical progress in large fields of science","stance":"rec","why":"Example of systemic issues in scientific fields","quote":"I also recommend this discussion between Eric and Brett Weinstein","text":"I recommend the book: Rigor Mortis: How Sloppy Science Creates Worthless Cures, Crushes Hope, and Wastes Billions Generally, all scientific fields have immense issues related to the walled gardens and limitations created by their peers. I also recommend this discussion between Eric and Brett Weinstein which highlights just one example of the major issues: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27794399,"author":"rvanlaar","month":"2021-07","story":"Is aging inevitable?","stance":"rec","why":"Evolutionary biologist on telomeres, cancer and longevity tradeoffs","quote":"an evolutionary biologist goes into depth about telomeres, evolution and how cancer and longevity are two sides of the same coin","text":"The answer is yes. It seems the author is reasoning more in the philosophic realm than in evolutionary biology. In this podcast [1] an evolutionary biologist goes into depth about telomeres, evolution and how cancer and longevity are two sides of the same coin. Long story short, having cells die after a number of replications is a postive trade off. It means that when a cell replicates too fast it also dies fast. The opposite are cancer cells, which replicate too fast and don't die. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24361804,"author":"rvanlaar","month":"2020-09","story":"Bodybuilding supplement promotes healthy aging and extends life span in mice","stance":"rec","why":"Explains evolutionary tradeoffs in lab mice longevity","quote":"This podcast delves into the problem with lab mice","text":"Uh oh, mice in labs are not like humans in this regard at all. Lab Mice have extraordinary cell repair capabilities. My guess is that the AKG slows down cell division. This podcast delves into the problem with lab mice and explains the trade offs that happen on an evolutionary and cellular level in mice with regards to longevity. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24073446,"author":"bz33t","month":"2020-08","story":"Lab mice have a chill, and that may be messing up study results (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Interview where Weinstein discusses changed breeding protocols","quote":"He talks about it in a long interview that gets really interesting about a hour in","text":"According to Bret Weinstein who figured out the connection between telomere length and breeding, they seem to have quietly changed the breeding protocols to bring the telomere length back down to be in line with wild type mice. He talks about it in a long interview that gets really interesting about a hour in: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23961497,"author":"ZephyrBlu","month":"2020-07","story":"Cracking down on research fraud","stance":"rec","why":"Full story of the lab mice issue across several topics","quote":"It's long, but I found it very interesting.","text":"They go over multiple topics. Check the top comment for timestamps. If you want to hear the full story I suggest listening to this podcast episode: [VIDEO] . It's long, but I found it very interesting."},{"id":22869838,"author":"meowface","month":"2020-04","story":"The Wolfram Physics Project","stance":"rec","why":"Full story of Bret Weinstein's lab mice telomere claims","quote":"I'd recommend listening to all of it.","text":">The core claim he makes \"all mice are broken\" is a hyperbole that a lot of people will tell you is unsubstantiated. He makes it sound like all tests done on mice are flawed because of it. It isn't hyperbole, if Bret's claims are substantiated. The claims being that most lab mice are from the Jackson lab, that all of their mice have extremely long telomeres compared to wild mice, and that this could have serious implications on the potential harm to humans by some drugs - even while also accounting for the fact that mice are already not a good model of humans and that the same drugs are later tested on humans. (I could elaborate on this, but it's covered thoroughly in the paper and podcast.) I agree Eric hasn't satisfactorily proved they didn't change the protocols, but if it is the case that all of their mice still have long telomeres, and if all of the other claims are true (most US researchers still using those mice + the other claims), then the claims \"most mice used by US researchers are broken\" and \"most tests done on mice by US researchers are flawed because of it\" are likely also true. I have no idea if the above claims are true, of course, but they should be investigated on their merits. Bret Weinstein's 2002 paper: [LINK] The podcast between Eric and Bret Weinstein covering the full story: [VIDEO] Not sure if you already listened to the podcast, but if you haven't, I'd recommend listening to all of it."},{"id":36141867,"author":"jxramos","month":"2023-05","story":"AI intensifies fight against ‘paper mills’ that churn out fake research","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Eric Weinstein's claim about peer review","quote":"","text":"There was an interesting claim I heard from Eric Weinstein regarding peer review that he characterized as a cancerous infiltration into all of science from some medical area? Aha, found it in my notes... \"\"\" 1:32:28 Eric: but let me jump in--peer review is a cancer from outer space. It came from the biomedical community, it invaded science. The old system, because I have to say this because many people who are now professional scientists have an idea that peer review has always been in our literature and it absolutely [mff __ ] has not. Bret: right Eric: Okay. It used to be that the editor of a journal took responsibility for the quality of the journal which is why we had things like nature crop up in the first place because they had courageous, knowledgeable, forward-thinking editors. And so I just want to be very clear because there is a mind virus out there that says peer review is the sine qua non of scientific excellence, yada, yada, yada, bs, bs, bs. And if you don't believe me go back and learn that this is a recent invasive problem in the sciences. Bret: recent invasive problem that has no justification for existing in light of the fact Eric: Well not only does it have no justification for existing... When Watson and Crick did the double helix and this is the cleanest example we have. The paper was agreed should not be sent out for review because anyone who was competent would understand immediately what its implications were. There are reasons that great work cannot "},{"id":23599023,"author":"sradman","month":"2020-06","story":"The Problem with America’s Lab Mice [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The publications section on Bret Weinstein’s Wikipedia page [1] lists the paper [2][3]: > Weinstein, Bret S; Ciszek, Deborah (2002). \"The reserve-capacity hypothesis: Evolutionary origins and modern implications of the trade-off between tumor-suppression and tissue-repair\". Experimental Gerontology. 37 (5): 615–27. There doesn’t seem to be a succinct write up on how this hypothesis led to Carol Greider’s 2009 Nobel Prize [4]. Greider did not recognize Weinstein’s contribution [5]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["F6FR08VylO4",1],["1YbcB6b4A2U",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["UANN2Eu6ZnM",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["lhYGXYeMq_E",1],["3x1b_S6Qp2Q",1]]},"O133ppiVnWY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a mind-changing introduction to exponential growth, though some note his peak oil predictions did not pan out.","q":{"x":"have totally changed the way I look at all political discourse","i":22502783,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23092937,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Name one idea that changed your life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Life-changing idea about compounding and unsustainable growth.","quote":"Taught me the logical impossibility of the stock market to go up by x% per year, forever.","text":"Compounding, growth etc [VIDEO] Taught me the logical impossibility of the stock market to go up by x% per year, forever. Taught me that getting better today (however small) can give resources to getting better(er?) tomorrow. Taught me that many \"experts\" just say whatever to get voted in or to get a budget without regards to the absurdity of their own statements, and that many people eat up this kind of absurdity without fact checking / validating it."},{"id":22502783,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: What are some deep, mentality changing books?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on exponential growth that changes how you see the news.","quote":"the \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\" lecture by Al Bartlett is absolutely amazing. You will never see the news in the same way again after watching it","text":"For me, several books by Greg Egan fall into this category. Permutation City, Diaspora, Quarantine, and Schild's Ladder have all... changed me. I was a different person before and after I had read those books, and now I categorise all works of fiction into those that forever alter my way of thinking and those that do not. [LINK] Which reminds me, I should finish: [LINK] The Culture series by Iain Banks, especially Inversions also satisfy this requirement, as does The City at the End of Time by Greg Bear: [LINK] -- which is a difficult to consume masterpiece that nonetheless left me permanently horrified in the way that H. P. Lovecraft tried but failed. For the non-fiction category the CGP Grey video \"The Rules for Rulers\": [LINK] and Part 2: [LINK] have totally changed the way I look at all political discourse, and this is coming from someone who has already absorbed The Prince. It's based on the Dictator's Handbook, which I should read also, but the CGP video was an effective summary already! [LINK] Last, but not least, if I'm allowed to include lecture videos, then the \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\" lecture by Al Bartlett is absolutely amazing. You will never see the news in the same way again after watching it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22688893,"author":"simonblack","month":"2020-03","story":"Why don’t I know anyone who knows anyone with Covid-19? [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains dangers of exponential growth.","quote":"it's the most valuable use of an hour of your lifetime to watch Prof. Bartlett's talk on exponential growth","text":"\"Two minutes past twelve.\" The problem with stuff like this is that most people have no knowledge about the dangers of exponential growth. It only takes an hour, but it's the most valuable use of an hour of your lifetime to watch Prof. Bartlett's talk on exponential growth in 'Arithmetic, Population and Energy': [VIDEO]"},{"id":36845710,"author":"RetroTechie","month":"2023-07","story":"Exponential Economist Meets Finite Physicist","stance":"rec","why":"Shows experts and the public ignoring exponential and physical constraints.","quote":"most people don't understand exponential growth.","text":"Great read! Reminds me of this lecture: [VIDEO] Al Bartlett - Arithmetic, Population and Energy Amazing how again & again, even so-called 'experts' can't do math, or ignore fundamental physics constraints. Never mind news outlets or the general public. And... most people don't understand exponential growth. Which brings me to b) math + physics is much much much much more reliable than people. So if you understand the physics involved, do the math, conclude that it can't possibly work, but someone (expert or otherwise) says it will work: go with your calculator. :-)"},{"id":31635026,"author":"knorker","month":"2022-06","story":"Restarting my career after motherhood","stance":"rec","why":"Population growth must end; only the way it ends is a choice.","quote":"we can not prevent the end of exponential population growth. We can only choose how it ends.","text":"The population pyramid is not sustainable. The exponential function just will not allow for the combination of age pyramid and our increasingly effective health care keeping us alive to all become parents. Watch this talk: [VIDEO] Tl;DW: we can not prevent the end of exponential population growth. We can only choose how it ends. By starvation, war, disease, or fewer births. Pension systems have relied on growth for a long time, but it's just not mathematically possible is the long run."},{"id":12148280,"author":"qpwoeirutyzzzz","month":"2016-07","story":"How climate change is rapidly taking the planet apart","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"also relevant","text":"For starters have fewer or no kids, and stop traveling frivolously by air. Aircraft emissions, because they are made at high altitude, have a climate impact that is leveraged by a factor commonly estimated to be 2.7 higher than the same emissions if made at ground-level.[1] 1. [LINK] edit: also relevant [VIDEO]"},{"id":29815972,"author":"z8rHZM8Svhu8hE2","month":"2022-01","story":"History of energy consumption in the United States, 1775–2009 (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Old lecture on exponential growth of energy consumption.","quote":"the old lecture on the exponential function of energy consumption comes to mind","text":"In this context, the old lecture on the exponential function of energy consumption comes to mind. Arithmetic, Population and Energy - a talk by Al Bartlett: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21072435,"author":"bjourne","month":"2019-09","story":"The Saudis Are Lying About Their Oil Production?","stance":"reference","why":"Still a good talk on exponential growth.","quote":"Still a good talk, but it seems he was wrong about peak oil","text":"Peak oil featured prominently in Al Bartlett's talk about exponential growth. [VIDEO] Still a good talk, but it seems he was wrong about peak oil, just like Malthus was wrong about population growth."},{"id":18230260,"author":"gambler","month":"2018-10","story":"Alarming study shows massive insect loss","stance":"reference","why":"Old lecture drawing the same parallel.","quote":"There is an old lecture that draws this exact parallel","text":"There is an old lecture that draws this exact parallel: \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":13752787,"author":"acqq","month":"2017-02","story":"What's Really Warming the World?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"It's fascinating how you ignore all the measurements that don't match your, as you say, \"political\" view, if you followed the links, that is. Because you can't say then you didn't understand how much are of them disproving your claims, and what they mean, and still claim you earned a Ph.D. Do you really think it's the whole world conspiracy? Because the \"political\" claim has no sense unless you believe that world consists only of the US of A, it's most of the scientists all around the world. If you simply say that the short-term politics is more important for you than the future of the world, I can at least understand. Anyway, \"the greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":23096907,"author":"abetusk","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Name one idea that changed your life","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Al Bartlett builds a straw man argument","text":"For me, this type of thinking does the exact opposite in that it has a chilling effect on finding solutions and correctly assessing reality. While some of what Al Bartlett says is true, that unfettered exponential growth can't go on forever, other statements are misleading or outright false. He has a soapbox about limiting population growth now (in the US) [0], tied in to concepts of peak oil and other \"dark green\" talking points. This type of thinking misses the larger picture. The irony is that exponential growth happens all across the board, not just in population growth and energy usage but in innovation for more efficient energy usage and in finding alternative energy sources. Al Bartlett builds a straw man argument, saying \"technological optimists assure use that technology will always solve all of our problems ...\" [1] but this isn't true. The argument against Bartlett's statement is that we haven't hit the ceiling on energy that's available to us. This is one of the first issues I have with this thinking: if we were to take his argument at face value, limit population growth, turn down our energy usage and try to live with what we have, then we're setting ourselves up for failure as every other country on the planet shifts to solar because it provides cheaper energy (yes, even to coal) and will only get exponentially cheaper for a good period of time [2] [3]. Here's some simple arithmetic [4]: The average US household consumes 30kWh of energy a day. The available ener"}],"r":[["kZA9Hnp3aV4",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1]]},"8Ko3TdPy0TU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an engaging lesson in probability and in how statistics can expose cheating.","q":{"x":"It not only exposes the cheater, it also gives some intuition into negligible probabilities.","i":28378467,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27181041,"author":"wexq","month":"2021-05","story":"How Lucky Is Too Lucky in Minecraft?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great refresher on probability via Minecraft cheating.","quote":"The article recommends watching the video by Matt Parker, and so do I. Great refresher on probability","text":"The article recommends watching the video by Matt Parker, and so do I. Great refresher on probability (or luck, if you will) For convenience: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43440439,"author":"contravariant","month":"2025-03","story":"Napkin Math Tool","stance":"rec","why":"Settles on 10^-19 as odds never deliberately achieved.","quote":"Matt Parker has quite a good video on it [1].","text":"I think the problem is that below 10^-6 there's a pretty broad range of magnitudes that are effectively 'happens to n people every k years (if they try very hard)'. Which makes them rare but not impossible, which is annoying because you can't rule it out but you can't expect to easily reproduce it either. That does tend to be the best way to think about those probabilities. For any probability you can make it happen If you try enough times or with enough people you can try to force it to happen. Below -6 is a pretty wide range of stuff you can just barely make happen if you have enough people, enough tries and enough time. It's only once you get to the 'wouldn't happen to 10^10 people in a million years' that you get to the stuff that you can be fairly sure has never happened. Matt Parker has quite a good video on it [1]. He settles on 10^-19 as something that you can be fairly sure has never happened (deliberately). As for other stuff in the probability rows, maybe include a couple of well known paradoxes like the birthday paradox. And be clear what you're saying, things like getting dealt a royal flush depends a lot on the type of poker game. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43059043,"author":"uolmir","month":"2025-02","story":"Diablo hackers uncovered a speedrun scandal","stance":"rec","why":"Mathy deep dive into a manipulated speedrun.","quote":"There have been several of these deep dives into manipulated speed runs and they seldom disappoint.","text":"There have been several of these deep dives into manipulated speed runs and they seldom disappoint. Since we are on HN, I'll mention another mathy one that was summarized by Matt Parker: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32994826,"author":"t_mann","month":"2022-09","story":"Dear Chess World","stance":"rec","why":"Rundown of a cheater exposed by statistical evidence.","quote":"Here's a good rundown of a case where a cheater is considered to have been exposed by statistical evidence","text":"The key point (in the end) seems to be that the odds for a streak like Niemann had are about 1 in 80k. Statistically speaking, I'd say that's a long shot from a smoking gun. Here's a good rundown of a case where a cheater is considered to have been exposed by statistical evidence, but those odds were on a completely different scale, 10^22: [VIDEO] Basically the mistake that is easy to make is that we shouldn't ask: \"what is the probability that Hans plays five tournaments like that in a row?\", but \"what is the probability that someone will play five tournaments like that in a row?\". Even if we correct for the fact that there are probably more Minecraft speedruns happening than GM tournaments, odds of 80k just seem a bit too low to call it evidence."},{"id":28378467,"author":"teekert","month":"2021-09","story":"A layman’s guide to recreational mathematics videos","stance":"rec","why":"Exposes cheater and builds intuition for negligible probabilities.","quote":"It not only exposes the cheater, it also gives some intuition into negligible probabilities.","text":"Maybe nice to mention that Matt Parker's Stand-up Maths video on the cheating on a MiceCraft speed-run is really very nice [0]. It not only exposes the cheater, it also gives some intuition into negligible probabilities. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31224584,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2022-05","story":"We think this cool study we found is flawed. Help us reproduce it","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why unlikely events can still plausibly occur.","quote":"It can still be a fair die.","text":"It can still be a fair die. The context implies it's not, but it's only unlikely, not impossible. See [VIDEO] It introduces a 10 billion human second century i.e. 3.1 x 10^19. If your chance times it is greater than 1 then it's definitely plausible to be done by someone, somewhere."},{"id":27181869,"author":"robga","month":"2021-05","story":"How Lucky Is Too Lucky in Minecraft?","stance":"reference","why":"Related Matt Parker video already mentioned in thread.","quote":"It is related to Matt Parker's youtube video","text":"BBC's radio show More or Less did a great piece on this recently. [LINK] It is related to Matt Parker's youtube video [VIDEO] which has already been mentioned on this post."}],"r":[["dKx1wnXClcI",1]]},"ISAKc9gpGjw":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to rebuttal to vertical farming hype, since it gives hard numbers from a leading controlled-environment authority.","q":{"x":"he does give hard numbers to do the math that shows that vertical farming is not as good as its proponents have made it out to be","i":43466894,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":38627287,"author":"busterarm","month":"2023-12","story":"Vertical farming company raised $500M, and then it all but disappeared","stance":"strong_rec","why":"The math against vertical farming is insurmountable.","quote":"Been sending this video to every charlatan pushing vertical farming for years now.","text":"[VIDEO] Been sending this video to every charlatan pushing vertical farming for years now. The math is pretty obvious and insurmountable on this one."},{"id":43466894,"author":"Shawnecy","month":"2025-03","story":"Vertical farming company Plenty files for bankruptcy after raising nearly $1B","stance":"rec","why":"Hard numbers showing vertical farming trails outdoor agriculture economics.","quote":"he does give hard numbers to do the math that shows that vertical farming is not as good as its proponents have made it out to be","text":"Unsurprising. I'm not sure much has changed since this recording of Bruce Bugbee's talk \"Why Vertical Farming Won't Save the Planet\". There's nothing surprising there but he does give hard numbers to do the math that shows that vertical farming is not as good as its proponents have made it out to be. Solar energy input dwarfs all other energy inputs to agriculture, and the cost to replace one acre of solar energy with electricity comes out to $400,000 per acre (per some unit of days per a growing season). I don't think the economics have tended favorably since. The space needs for solar panels doesn't favor vertical farming either. He also shows the efficiency of modern agriculture, and why vertical farming has a pretty tall order ahead of it to beat the economics of outdoor farming. It's quite unlikely factors have changed enough since to alter his conclusion: only high-value, high-water-content specialty crops might be economically viable. [0] = [VIDEO]"},{"id":18134687,"author":"contingencies","month":"2018-10","story":"New autonomous farm wants to produce food without human workers","stance":"rec","why":"Fundamentals of why indoor farming struggles.","quote":"For the fundamentals","text":"This article is all over the place and then apparently quotes an academic out of context. The real problem with indoor farming is not setup cost. For the fundamentals: [VIDEO] ... Bruce Bugbee, Utah State University Department of Plants, Soils and Climate, has studied plant growth in controlled environments for most of his career presents the results of his analysis of the environmental effects of Vertical Farming/Indoor Agriculture (September 2015). With regards to the machinery shown at the top of the article, it is standard industrial automation equipment you can purchase off the shelf, apparently not even hardened or substantially adjusted for outdoor use."},{"id":17471270,"author":"contingencies","month":"2018-07","story":"Is Vertical Farming Really the Future of Agriculture?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture by a leading authority on controlled-environment growing.","quote":"a lecture by the preeminent authority in the field, Bruce Bugbee","text":"Why Vertical Farming Won't Save the Planet [0] is a lecture by the preeminent authority in the field, Bruce Bugbee, Utah State University Department of Plants, Soils and Climate, who has studied plant growth in controlled environments for most of his career and has consulted on all NASA missions with these goals. In particular see 13m26s[1] and 37m17s[2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13325062,"author":"jsilence","month":"2017-01","story":"The Vertical Farm","stance":"rec","why":"Informative lecture backing the argument against vertical farming.","quote":"A very informed and informative lecture filled with facts","text":"A very informed and informative lecture filled with facts that supports the former argument: [VIDEO] Myself I am a chemical engineer working in agricultural research. We are working on aquaponics and also test vertical growbeds. Vertical growbeds underperform significantly unless you put expensive light on them. Generating the electricity for light through PV uses about ten times the area compared to using direct sunlight. No way to counter that by using the full spectrum."},{"id":13325066,"author":"jsilence","month":"2017-01","story":"The Vertical Farm","stance":"rec","why":"Informative lecture backing the argument.","quote":"A very informed and informative lecture filled with facts that supports this argument","text":"A very informed and informative lecture filled with facts that supports this argument: [VIDEO] Myself I am a chemical engineer working in agricultureal research. We are working on aquaponics and also test vertical growbeds. Vertical growbrds underperform significantly unless you put expensive light on them."},{"id":25421737,"author":"Osiris30","month":"2020-12","story":"Europe's Biggest Vertical Farm Will Be Powered by Wind and Planted by Robots","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of indoor farming's environmental effects.","quote":"vertical/indoor farming is great for leafy greens (mainly water but but not much other nutrients or carbon), but much harder for other plants.","text":"Also see previous recent discussion of indoor/vertical farming (2017) - (1) and (2). As discussed on that thread, vertical/indoor farming is great for leafy greens (mainly water but but not much other nutrients or carbon), but much harder for other plants. See this video [3] - \"Why Vertical Farming Won't Save the Planet: Bruce Bugbee, Utah State University Department of Plants, Soils and Climate, has studied plant growth in controlled environments for most of his career. Here he presents the results of his analysis of the environmental effects of Vertical Farming/Indoor Agriculture (September 2015)\". A copy of the slides can be downloaded here (the link shown on the youtube page is dead - correct link[4]). [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":20534543,"author":"duckymcduckface","month":"2019-07","story":"The Clean Farming Revolution","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the researcher with the same conclusion.","quote":"I think OP is referring to Bruce Bugbee from Utah state","text":"I think OP is referring to Bruce Bugbee from Utah state [VIDEO] Even if it isn't him the conclusion is the same."},{"id":17467825,"author":"maliker","month":"2018-07","story":"Is Vertical Farming Really the Future of Agriculture?","stance":"reference","why":"Good analysis of indoor farming energy costs.","quote":"Utah State’s Extension service did a good analysis","text":"Maybe for expensive microgreens. Otherwise your energy costs are prohibitive. Utah State’s Extension service did a good analysis [1]. Indoor farming energy costs only add about 30% to your cost of tomatoes, but 10,000% for wheat. Of course this assumes you can get an acre of indoor space for the same price as an acre of cropland. And can also amportize your hardware costs over many growing seasons to drive them to zero. Probably not good assumptions. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16643304,"author":"contingencies","month":"2018-03","story":"Startups that launched today at Y Combinator’s W18 Demo Day","stance":"reference","why":"Estimate of solar energy value per acre.","quote":"estimated USD$400,000 worth of solar energy per acre of land from outdoor growing","text":"Beanstalk is an indoor farming startup that grows produce at the cost of outdoor farming. Sure about that? With no details at all on the website, I will believe this guy first, who in late 2015 estimated USD$400,000 worth of solar energy per acre of land from outdoor growing. [VIDEO] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14810646,"author":"Osiris30","month":"2017-07","story":"Plenty, Indoor Farming Startup, Raises $200M","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of environmental effects of indoor farming.","quote":"vertical/indoor farming is great for leafy greens, but much harder for other plants.","text":"See previous recent discussion of indoor/vertical farming (1). As discussed on that thread, vertical/indoor farming is great for leafy greens, but much harder for other plants. See this video [2] - \"Why Vertical Farming Won't Save the Planet: Bruce Bugbee, Utah State University Department of Plants, Soils and Climate, has studied plant growth in controlled environments for most of his career. Here he presents the results of his analysis of the environmental effects of Vertical Farming/Indoor Agriculture (September 2015)\". A copy of the slides can be downloaded here (the link shown on the youtube page is dead - correct link[3]). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":14812769,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-07","story":"Plenty, Indoor Farming Startup, Raises $200M","stance":"reference","why":"Artificial lighting cannot compete with field or greenhouse production.","quote":"Artificially lit farming can't compete with field production or heated/unheated greenhouses.","text":">greenhouse + aquaponics + high levels of automation seems to work well Indeed, that's a big point in the video. Artificially lit farming can't compete with field production or heated/unheated greenhouses. link to time [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XDM3MqHln8U",1],["OO18F4aKGzQ",1],["8Z685OF-PS8",1],["8JrqH2oOTK4",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1]]},"aMcjxSThD54":{"wy":"HN cites it as the classic example of an interviewer misrepresenting a guest, though opinions on who came out better differ.","q":{"x":"A classic, even if you don't like the guy","i":37390625,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":16193069,"author":"josephg","month":"2018-01","story":"The curious star appeal of Jordan Peterson","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Harsh but fair interview where Peterson stays calm and articulate","quote":"I enjoyed watching that interview immensely","text":"I think \"watch X destroy Y\" is just youtube's version of clickbait. I suspect you'll find videos like that tailored to most groups on youtube. I sometimes use them as trailers for longer talks that I might enjoy - although I always feel a little guilty for encouraging this sort of thing. In this case I found their interview to be excellent viewing. She was harsh and honest with him, and hurled most of the standard criticism at Peterson that people talk about. And while doing that she gave him the time and opportunity to respond. And he stayed calm, considered, articulate and entertaining throughout. I don't agree with everything either of them said, but I enjoyed watching that interview immensely - its exactly the sort of thing I want to see more of on youtube and in the public sphere. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37390625,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2023-09","story":"Farms that create habitat key to food security and biodiversity","stance":"rec","why":"Classic example of an interviewer misrepresenting the guest","quote":"A classic, even if you don't like the guy","text":"\"So we should X?\" is usually a signal that you're about to not track/engage with what someone said. I'm reminded of Cathy Newman's interview of Jordan Peterson where her only retort the whole time was \"So you're saying that <something he didn't say>.\" ( [VIDEO] -- A classic, even if you don't like the guy) For example, they are talking about cows we farm into existence at the tune of 1.5 billion global population, not a few million wild animals."},{"id":18707964,"author":"cm2187","month":"2018-12","story":"Top Patreon creators launching an alternate crowdfunding platform","stance":"rec","why":"Interview through which many discovered Peterson","quote":"A good way to make your own opinion is to watch the channel 4 interview","text":"Well they occasionally debate with each others but aren't supporting the same ideas. They do have in common that they disagree with identity politics, and as you can tell from the name calling on this HN page, this is a topic on which for many there is zero tolerance for any disagreement. Peterson mostly discusses religion (from a philosophical point of view), self-improvement, identity politics. He tends to be more on the conservative side but outside of identity politics doesn't discuss politics that much. His videos are all on his youtube channel [1]. A good way to make your own opinion is to watch the channel 4 interview, which I think is the way many people discovered him [2] Harris is a Democrat, furiously anti-religious and anti-Trump, talks about quite a variety of topics. You can browse through his podcasts [3]. He also doesn't like identity politics and islamist extremism. To get a good idea of his position on a touchy topic, you can try his interview of Charles Murray [4], which is very interesting and I think is a good example of how a relatively balanced point of view can get unfairly attacked. I am less familiar with Rubin [5]. My understanding is that he is more of a libertarian. Also less familiar with Eric Weinstein (and his brother Bret Weinstein). But you can listen to his discussion on the Intellectual Dark Web with Sam Harris [6]. Harris also has some interesting discussions with Bret Weinstein. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] "},{"id":16516189,"author":"dkoubsky","month":"2018-03","story":"Male and female bosses share the same “classically masculine” personality traits","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson on masculine traits in the Newman interview","quote":"I think it's worth a watch","text":"Jordan Peterson talked about this in his infamous Cathy Newman interview. I think it's worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16398363,"author":"air7","month":"2018-02","story":"Fired Google Engineer Loses Diversity Memo Challenge","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson staying composed against an aggressive interviewer","quote":"he manages to remain cool and clear-headed against a very aggressive interviewer","text":"I've recently encounter Jordan Peterson. He speaks very eloquently about topics such as gender differences from (IMO) a rational and scientific point of view. I'm linking an interview with him which I find fascinating: Both for the information he conveys and for the manner in which he manages to remain cool and clear-headed against a very aggressive interviewer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36913460,"author":"duckhelmet","month":"2023-07","story":"The Lonely Work of Moderating Hacker News (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"“I have noticed certain trends. When stories that focus on structural barriers faced by women in the workplace, or on diversity in tech, or on race or masculinity—stories, admittedly, that are more intriguing to me, a person interested in the humanities, than stories on technical topics—hit the front page, users often flag them, presumably for being off topic, so fast that hardly any comments accrue.” a. That's because there is no barriers to to women in the tech workplace. It just that they have a social life outside computers. b. This is a tech forum, we only want to read tech stuff. c. Your imaginary travails are of no interest to us. “Jordan Peterson debate on the gender pay gap” [VIDEO]"},{"id":26293136,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2021-02","story":"In Defense of Myers Briggs (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson's pay gap claims in the Newman interview","quote":"most famously in the Cathy Newman interview","text":"Re: men vs women - he has often claimed for example that women get payed less because they are unwilling to do the better paying jobs (most famously in the Cathy Newman interview [0]). He also claims often that women are more interested in empathy and men in systems, so that men are vastly much more likely to be in fields like engineering, while women are more vastly more likely to be interested in fields like nursing [1], with natural differences as large as 20:1. He himself may not make this value judgement, but given the general social consensus that views engineering as a more prestigious, intellectual job than medical nurses, I think it's mostly fair to characterize this claim as I did. I disagree with this conclusion any way - I believe that IQ testing quickly loses any kind of predictive power when applied to different backgrounds, and many implicit and even explicit biases quickly show up. I also find it hard to believe that a society where women:men ratios in any profession are 20:1 or 1:20 can be characterized as the free-est in the world on this issue. More likely, there are great social and historical pressures trending in this direction, and the state is simply doing nothing to try to reverse this injustice. Looking at former communist countries, that are generally not even close to having any kind of women in STEM programs that could be considered to bias results, the women:men ratio in STEM fields is something like 30:70. Re: IQ and \"race\" - I don't think so. Y"},{"id":24745549,"author":"brabel","month":"2020-10","story":"The Mostly True Story of Vanilla Ice","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a brutal interview","quote":"want to watch a brutal interview?","text":"> Invited on The Arsenio Hall Show, Ice walked blithely into the lion’s den... It remains a brutal interview to watch. You can practically see Ice’s career leave his body.... Way to exaggerate! The interview was very cordial, considering how much hate the guy was getting at the time. Completely unrelated, but want to watch a brutal interview? Check Cathey Newman interviewing Jordan Peterson (who still managed to come out relatively unscathed): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19465629,"author":"drak0n1c","month":"2019-03","story":"The principle of charity: Criticize the best interpretation of an argument","stance":"reference","why":"Example of uncharitable interviewing backfiring","quote":"excessive straw-manning ends up helping the person and argument you're against","text":"There's a famous Channel 4 interview between Cathy Newman and Jordan Peterson that illustrates not just the flaws of being uncharitable but also how it can backfire spectacularly. Regardless of how one feels about Peterson, the nearly 15 million views are evidence that excessive straw-manning ends up helping the person and argument you're against. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16411851,"author":"peoplewindow","month":"2018-02","story":"Like Peter Thiel, Tech Workers Feel Alienated by Silicon Valley ‘Echo Chamber’","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sigh. As the sibling comment points out, what you are saying is not true . It is contradicted by the text of the memo itself. Moreover he wasn't \"very vocal about it\", he wrote a memo that was then deliberately leaked by his ideological opponents in order to destroy his career: successfully so. Five years ago I felt my political views were pretty mainstream for the tech industry, for the Valley (although I did not live there). I'd have described myself as a centrist or maybe centre-left. These days my views have shifted, I can feel myself getting more conservative with time. It's not an age thing. It's more that I've started to notice the sort of tactic you used above - faced with someone making conservative arguments you disagree with you didn't bother debating the points he made. Instead you just lied about what he said and then attacked a straw man. This is consistently how Damore is treated. There are liberal arguments that can be made about what he wrote - people could point out methodological errors in his studies, or logic errors in his arguments. But they never seem to do that. Whether it's in the media (who love calling what he wrote an \"anti diversity memo\" even though it praises diversity and has ideas for how to increase the number of women in tech), or on Hacker News, the tactic is always the same - pretend he claimed women are worse than men and then viciously attack him on a personal level. And it's just totally false. The same tactic crops up in other similar "},{"id":16189279,"author":"hashberry","month":"2018-01","story":"What’s So Dangerous About Jordan Peterson?","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson's recent debate with Cathy Newman","quote":"His most recent debate with Cathy Newman from three days ago","text":"Jordan Peterson became famous after a student tried to debate him about his \"Nazi followers\" and gender pronouns: [LINK] Many people see him as an enemy because of his conservative viewpoints. His most recent debate with Cathy Newman from three days ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16189504,"author":"nouveaux","month":"2018-01","story":"What’s So Dangerous About Jordan Peterson?","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson states he would use preferred pronouns","quote":"23m16s is where he states he would use preferred pronouns.","text":"Jordan Peterson is on record saying he would use preferred pronouns. He is opposed to the law and regulation of these pronouns: [VIDEO] 23m16s is where he states he would use preferred pronouns."},{"id":16193073,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2018-01","story":"The curious star appeal of Jordan Peterson","stance":"reference","why":"Original Channel 4 interview, seen as awkward for the presenter","quote":"It's really awkward to see somebody be that badly intellectually outclassed","text":"BTW, that Spectator article basically just embeds the original interview on Channel 4 uploaded to YouTube: [VIDEO] And, wow, I just watched this and it is horrendously cringeworthy. Seems like the presenter was expecting someone soft in the head (like an MP/politician), and approached the interview in that modern British fashion, which didn't work out well at all. It's really awkward to see somebody be that badly intellectually outclassed, but then it was Channel 4... On the other hand, coming back to OP, there's something encouraging about a critical thinker having \"star appeal\". I think that's the best way to describe Dr Peterson, as you don't have to agree with him, but it is easy to follow his lucid trains-of-thought, and he is at least consistent."},{"id":16193105,"author":"Radim","month":"2018-01","story":"The curious star appeal of Jordan Peterson","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I found most of her ripostes a dishonest caricature of Peterson's position, or downright lies.","text":"\"Let me get this straight… you're saying we should organize our societies along the lines of the LOBSTERS?!\" Harsh, perhaps. Honest, not so much. I found most of her ripostes a dishonest caricature of Peterson's position, or downright lies. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["DoA4Txr4GUs",1],["LZPKTaVB1IU",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["XpDsk374LDE",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["bJ_seXo-Enc",1]]},"tUAX0gnZ3Nw":{"wy":"It is the go-to explanation of why games that die when servers shut down harm consumers, and it is repeatedly cited in Stop Killing Games and game-delisting threads.","q":{"x":"Just watch this video so that you get up to speed with what we are talking about","i":41215117,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":21777308,"author":"flafla2","month":"2019-12","story":"Discontinued Microsoft Products","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long video on games dying at corporate whim.","quote":"If you have an hour, I'd highly recommend giving this a watch.","text":"See \"Games as a service is fraud\": [VIDEO] If you have an hour, I'd highly recommend giving this a watch. It is really sad to see any form of media die forever at the whim of a corporation. Games are especially prone to this unfortunately"},{"id":41215117,"author":"beretguy","month":"2024-08","story":"Stop Destroying Videogames – European Citizens' Initiative","stance":"rec","why":"Gets viewers up to speed on the initiative's topic.","quote":"Just watch this video so that you get up to speed with what we are talking about","text":"Just watch this video so that you get up to speed with what we are talking about [VIDEO] Alternative YouTube link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41215127,"author":"beretguy","month":"2024-08","story":"Stop Destroying Videogames – European Citizens' Initiative","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the issue to those who misunderstand it.","quote":"Watch this video to understand what we are talking about here","text":"You don’t know what you are talking about and you are wasting everyone’s time. Watch this video to understand what we are talking about here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29699687,"author":"gravypod","month":"2021-12","story":"Ubisoft deleted account with hundreds of dollars’ worth of games for inactivity","stance":"rec","why":"Argument against games as a service.","quote":"his opinions on this topic align greatly with my personal sentiment.","text":"Some more great information in this same space: [LINK] A less legally sound argument for why games as a service shouldn't be allowed: [VIDEO] Ross is very odd but his opinions on this topic align greatly with my personal sentiment."},{"id":27352009,"author":"DrSiemer","month":"2021-06","story":"Many Need for Speed Games Are Abiut to Be Erased by EA","stance":"rec","why":"Videos on why erasing games harms gaming culture.","quote":"Ross from Accursed Farms has some great videos","text":"Ross from Accursed Farms has some great videos * about exactly this phenomenon and why it is so detrimental to gaming culture in general. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":27366789,"author":"rasz","month":"2021-06","story":"Many Need for Speed Games Are Abiut to Be Erased by EA","stance":"rec","why":"Covers game delistings and shutdowns well.","quote":"Ross Scott covers it quite well","text":"Ross Scott covers it quite well in his \"Games as a service\" is fraud video [VIDEO] and others in 'dead game news' series."},{"id":22734425,"author":"flafla2","month":"2020-03","story":"Internet Archive responds: Why we released the National Emergency Library","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of dead games and server shutdowns.","quote":"Here's a great overview/rant on this topic","text":"The problem is that for game ROMs specifically, opening them to the public domain devalues potential opportunities for re-releases and remasters. The Nintendo virtual console service and S/NES classic are prominent examples. So it isn't so simple that there is \"no harm done\" by forcing game ROMs to fall into public domain. That all being said, I still agree with you. I really don't care how much money Nintendo, Microsoft, or Sony wants to make on decades-old games, at a certain point it becomes a cultural artifact. Snow White and other century-old movie properties should be public domain nowadays as well. At this point we're debating about the core values of trademark law though, and I'm not sure you wanted to do that. A similar debate revolves around what the law should say about \"dead\" games. These are games that, for example, rely on a central server that has been shut down. Many of these games were incredibly popular in their day, and some \"radicals\" (like myself, lol) argue that game companies should be compelled to release the server source code in an effort to preserve the game. Here's a great overview/rant on this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41128349,"author":"teroshan","month":"2024-08","story":"Stop Killing Games – European Citizens' Initiative","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video separating subscription games from purchased ones.","quote":"Ross Scott put them in a separate category in a previous video of his [1]","text":"As I understand, this would also apply to e.g. micro-transactions/cosmetics which would be categorized as 'possessions' if I follow the wording in this proposal. So this would include F2P games, requiring them to provide a way to enjoy those cosmetics. As for subscription-based games, Ross Scott put them in a separate category in a previous video of his [1], as you willingly pay for access to a service which has a known end date (end of the month). Although with the micro-transactions angle in mind, I'm not sure how this changes things. [1]: \"Games as a service\" is fraud, [VIDEO]"},{"id":28387758,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2021-09","story":"Lenovo tablet forced update shows un-dismissable, un-mutable, ad notifications","stance":"reference","why":"Source for industry buy-versus-subscribe framing.","quote":"","text":"> but that doesn’t happen Doesn't happen yet . Entire industries are trying to bypass basic property law so you \"own\" your purchases in name only and the manufacturer has control de facto . The video game industry has been saying \"buy or product\" when they really mean \"subscribe to our service\"[1] for a long time. Bricking old multiplayer gamed had been common practice for years, and now some singleplayer games have been unnecessarily bricked when the publisher e.g. shut down the DRM server. Companies like John Deere have been extending this strategy into their hardware for a while. The damage doesn't even have to be \"bricking your device\"; HN regularly features stories about forced updates that remove paid-for features. > Have Apple pull something like this and you’ll see on the NYT. Apple got close when their devices suddenly stopped running 3rd party software in the recent \"OCSP Apocalypse\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21708315,"author":"andrecarini","month":"2019-12","story":"Tron: Evolution – SecuROM DRM expiration makes game unplayable 9 years later","stance":"reference","why":"Legal argument that games are goods, not services.","quote":"there are legal arguments [1] to be made that Steam's games are goods, not services","text":"Regardless of the EULA, there are legal arguments [1] to be made that Steam's games are goods, not services, and buyers whose games are remotely disabled due to auth servers being shut off should be entitled to either a refund, a reasonable patch to keep the game working locally or have a clear expiration date stamped on the storefront since day one. This recent French court's ruling [2] indirectly supports this interpretation. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["bJ5ppf0po3k",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["xn76r0JxqNM",1],["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1]]},"ExgxwKnH8y4":{"wy":"It is HN's go-to concrete example of how local regulation and NIMBYism block new housing in San Francisco and California.","q":{"x":"Here is a terrific video that everyone should watch that shows how insanely difficult it is to make affordable housing.","i":40978691,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40978691,"author":"pneill","month":"2024-07","story":"U.S. now short 4.5M homes as housing deficit grows","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how insanely difficult it is to build affordable housing.","quote":"Here is a terrific video that everyone should watch that shows how insanely difficult it is to make affordable housing.","text":"Asking why there isn't enough housing is like asking why water is wet. The answer is state/local government regulation. There's an old saying \"your margin is my opportunity\" and I suspect that the current crop of homes that are built have decent margins, but in a deregulated market an entrepreneur could come in offer more affordable housing options with lower margins. You can see this in vehicles. Nobody complains about the vehicle affordability problem because there are far fewer regulations in the transportation industry (don't get me wrong, there are regulations, but not a constraining as you see in the housing market). There's a vehicle at every price point. You got bikes, then ebikes, mopeds, scoters, motorcycles, sub compacts, compacts, full size, trucks, suvs, and all the way to crazy expensive sports cars. You don't see that in house because affordable housing is not a good business. It's impossible to offer options at the lower end of the market because of zoning laws, environmental impact regulations, a difficult permitting process, nimby's, and affordable housing politicians that are really big proponents of affordable housing except \"they have question about this particular housing project.\" Here is a terrific video that everyone should watch that shows how insanely difficult it is to make affordable housing. A [VIDEO] Sometimes what you need is less government regulation, not more. edited sentence \"You don't see that in house it's not a good business.\" to You don"},{"id":20215610,"author":"pneill","month":"2019-06","story":"$1B for 20,000 Bay Area homes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how politics and citizen opposition block higher-density housing.","quote":"Watch this video - it's eye opening","text":"Politics is a huge factor in the lack of affordable housing. Both legislation and concerns by citizen about how new housing will \"change\" a neighbor make it next to impossible to build higher density housing. Watch this video - it's eye opening [VIDEO]"},{"id":22329399,"author":"pneill","month":"2020-02","story":"California’s housing crisis: how a bureaucrat pushed to build","stance":"rec","why":"Shows conflicting desires for affordable housing and not in my backyard.","quote":"Worth a view","text":"It's very complicated. People have conflicting ideas - one the one hand, they want affordable housing (ie their rent to go down) or the other, not in my backyard. Worth a view The Insane Battle To Sabotage a New Apartment Building Explains San Francisco's Housing Crisis [VIDEO]"},{"id":18901125,"author":"ransom1538","month":"2019-01","story":"SF restaurants are suffocating","stance":"rec","why":"Documents one person's struggle to build an apartment building in SF.","quote":"Here is an awesome video documenting one persons struggle to create an apartment building","text":"\"You can't have it both ways. Either change the city and build upwards or stop complaining about the price of living\" Yes. Here is an awesome video documenting one persons struggle to create an apartment building: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18901141,"author":"brianpgordon","month":"2019-01","story":"SF restaurants are suffocating","stance":"rec","why":"Conveys how ridiculous the obstacles to new housing in SF have become.","quote":"this video does do a good job of conveying a sense of how ridiculous the situation has gotten","text":"Landlords, yes, but also people who think that new market rate housing will destroy the character of their neighborhoods, or something. Take it with a grain of salt because it's reason.com, but this video does do a good job of conveying a sense of how ridiculous the situation has gotten: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43155212,"author":"guywithahat","month":"2025-02","story":"CEO Simulator: Startup Edition","stance":"reference","why":"Famous example of years of bureaucratic fights despite proper zoning.","quote":"This is a famous video demonstrating that point;","text":"Is it the old people? It feels like it's the new, young people moving to the city that do this. When people do investigative reporting on the SF housing market, they find a bunch of people who have been spent 8 years trying to convert dilapidated buildings into an apartment complex, but have been getting blocked by environmental reports on what shadows the building will cast, or other bureaucratic nonsense. This is a famous video demonstrating that point; where even if you're rightfully zoned to do something, it can take years and years of fighting bureaucratic nonsense if you haven't bribed the right people [VIDEO]"},{"id":38235309,"author":"pauldenton","month":"2023-11","story":"Only 15% of Californians can afford a home, new data shows","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Californians blocking construction, such as over playground shadows.","quote":"If only Californians didn't actively try to stop construction.","text":"[VIDEO] If only Californians didn't actively try to stop construction. If you want to build housing and that will cast a shadow on a nearby playground, that's no good. California is literally the worst and most expensive place to develop anything."},{"id":37567329,"author":"pauldenton","month":"2023-09","story":"Americans Can Barely Afford Homes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows regulatory burdens, such as shadow rules, blocking construction.","quote":"It has to do more with regulatory burdens in Democrat strongholds","text":"It has to do more with regulatory burdens in Democrat strongholds [VIDEO] Can't have your building cast a shadow on a school playground (you haven't paid bribes to the right city officials to get rid of their roadblocks)"},{"id":35074797,"author":"kwere","month":"2023-03","story":"How Californians Are Weaponizing Environmental Law to Eliminate Housing","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with laundromat owner who spent years and $1.4M trying to convert it.","quote":"Reason did an article and video interviewing Bob Tillman[0], the owner","text":"Its the whole building over the laundromat, and its big enough. Reason did an article and video interviewing Bob Tillman[0], the owner, who has spent nearly 5 years and $1.4 million trying to convert his laundromat into new housing. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34201057,"author":"ErikVandeWater","month":"2022-12","story":"JPMorgan to spend $1B on rental homes in the US to become a megalandlord","stance":"reference","why":"NIMBYs blocking even a small entrepreneur's housing development in SF.","quote":"NIMBYs in SF preventing development of new housing by even a small entrepreneur","text":"Counterpoint: NIMBYs in SF preventing development of new housing by even a small entrepreneur: [VIDEO] Something tells me JP Morgan would have an even tougher time getting past the NIMBYs."},{"id":32841744,"author":"major505","month":"2022-09","story":"How San Francisco makes it insanely hard to build housing","stance":"reference","why":"ReasonTV's 2018 laundromat case, showing a persistent problem.","quote":"ReasonTV made a video about the laundromat case a few years ago.","text":"ReasonTV made a video about the laundromat case a few years ago. The video is from 2018, is 2022 and still the same problem persists. [VIDEO] You wnat to make affordable house? Increase the demand. Is not that hard."},{"id":28359148,"author":"ErikVandeWater","month":"2021-08","story":"One housing project epic battle in SF, where the real enemy is city red tape","stance":"reference","why":"Another example of obstacles to private affordable housing.","quote":"Another example","text":"Another example: [VIDEO] Politicians don't want private industry affordable housing because they don't get any credit for it. They rather have almost no housing for the less fortunate but the tiny bit that is affordable they get credit for."},{"id":20219079,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-06","story":"$1B for 20,000 Bay Area homes","stance":"reference","why":"Laundromat owner spent five years and $1.4M trying to convert to apartments.","quote":"If you want to see how difficult it is to build there, check out this story","text":"> SF doesn't want to allow taller buildings and denser housing complexes. Pretty much. When I lived in SF I received fliers in the mail urging residents to oppose new apartment construction literally on the basis that it was too tall. If you want to see how difficult it is to build there, check out this story[1] of a laundromat owner who spent almost 5 years and $1.4 million attempting to convert to an apartment building. This is even though it was already zoned for housing, didn't have any units above it (wouldn't displace anyone) and eligible for streamlined approval since it was close to public transit. And yet... 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19874205,"author":"manfredo","month":"2019-05","story":"San Francisco proposes “IPO tax” on eve of Uber offering","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a developer blocked from replacing a laundromat with apartments.","quote":"For further examples, see the infamous case of the developer that wanted to replace a laundromat with an apartment building","text":"> I read in the SF Chronicle that there’s permitted and approved projects that developers are not moving forward with, like around the new hospital off of Van Ness, due to the “tightening of capital markets” and “soaring construction costs.”[1] Not NIMBYs or government regulations. No doubt that construction costs exacerbate the problem. But there absolutely are people deliberately blocking housing construction. There was a proposition to outright block private housing construction in the Mission that got ~40% yes votes. For further examples, see the infamous case of the developer that wanted to replace a laundromat with an apartment building: [VIDEO] > And then I dig a little deeper into the “it’s just supply and demand, dummy” narrative and find articles that document how luxury housing development actually is associated with a rise in rents for the working class.[2] Who would have thought macroeconomics is complicated? This is a fundamentally dishonest objection. New housing is \"luxury\" housing because the housing market is so expensive that even 1 bedroom apartments rent for $3-4K. Blocking \"luxury housing\" is blocking all housing, because the market is so expensive that even the smallest units are expensive enough to be considered luxury. Seriously, even 300 sq foot studios rent for 2.5K: [LINK] Of course \"luxury housing\" is correlated with rising rents. When rents rise, newly constructed units get more expensive too and get pushed into the \"luxury\" price bracket. This i"},{"id":18849836,"author":"jeffbax","month":"2019-01","story":"'Dear NIMBYs' – An Open Letter from a Homeless Woman in LA","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how regulations and approvals drive up the cost of building housing.","quote":"Building is anything but easy or cheap","text":"Profit is a function of materials, land, labor, margin, and very much regulatory cost. When town laws limit the height you can build, that means you have fewer units to distribute total costs across. Building 10 stories might be 70% the cost of 20 stories… so if you have double the units to spread things across, price per unit WILL be lower. Now extrapolate for things like 1. Minimum square footage 2. Parking requirements 3. \"low income housing\" units or other terms the local housing authority might put on top of any deal 4. Getting approval for the design (and the cycle of going back and forth to the drawing board) 5. Buying out surrounding rights holders like that old church you have to move across the street, or in SF's case the derelict gas station that is now a landmark if it means \"preserving the character of the neighborhood\" 6…etc Building is anything but easy or cheap: [VIDEO] All this adds up. Public housing is important in the interim given its usually easier to get around existing regulations, but the government is one of the shittiest landlords around. The goal should be 100% private housing supply. Market based solutions will absolutely build homes for the homeless as long as the cost to build them do not exceed the value that whatever entity like the government or charity can pay. Markets deliver $8 Waffle House steak, and $60 Ruth's Chris steak. Cities/regulations only allow Ruth's Chris. You get luxury housing b/c it's too expensive to build anything else. Fo"}],"r":[["cKPlPJyQrt4",1]]},"XpDsk374LDE":{"wy":"Recommended as the argument for code colocation and delaying abstraction.","q":{"x":"Just stick everything in the same file until it actually hurts. This presentation[0] gets it right.","i":30194512,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39428642,"author":"gabigrin","month":"2024-02","story":"Clean your codebase with basic information theory","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk by Elm's creator on structuring codebases.","quote":"this great talk by Evan Czaplicki (Elm creator)","text":"Great read, as always! The combination of the Elm reference and \"coarsely structure codebases around CPU timelines and dataflow\" statement reminded me of this great talk by Evan Czaplicki (Elm creator): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30194512,"author":"yakshaving_jgt","month":"2022-02","story":"Code colocation is king","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues for keeping everything in one file until it hurts.","quote":"Just stick everything in the same file until it actually hurts. This presentation[0] gets it right.","text":"Just stick everything in the same file until it actually hurts. This presentation[0] gets it right. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46154470,"author":"myaccountonhn","month":"2025-12","story":"Thoughts on Go vs. Rust vs. Zig","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how to structure a project functionally.","quote":"Here is one about how to structure a project (roughly)","text":"There are a lot of lectures/speeches by the creator of elm and Richard Feldman that talk about how to think \"functionally\" Here is one about how to structure a project (roughly) [VIDEO] I also think looking at the source code for elm and its website, as well as the elm real world example help a lot."},{"id":19135615,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2019-02","story":"38% of bugs at Airbnb could have been prevented by using types","stance":"rec","why":"Elm creator talks about premature extraction and abstractions.","quote":"The creator of Elm has a talk that mentions this","text":"Premature extraction can solidify premature abstractions and create pressure against reabstraction. The creator of Elm has a talk that mentions this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1],["DoA4Txr4GUs",1],["LZPKTaVB1IU",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1]]},"j1I63wGcvU4":{"wy":"HN commenters call it the best i3 tutorial on YouTube and recommend it for getting started thoroughly.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this 3-part video series from Code Cast","i":23468825,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23468825,"author":"typenil","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Which tools have made you a much better programmer?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Three-part video series for getting into i3.","quote":"I highly recommend this 3-part video series from Code Cast","text":"Most of my productivity gains these days has come from aligning myself and my workstation. - i3wm ( [LINK] ) - particularly getting comfortable editing the .i3/config. It's the most significant productivity change I've had since switching to Linux from Windows. To get into it, I highly recommend this 3-part video series from Code Cast: [VIDEO] - yadm ( [LINK] ) - a dotfile manager. It's essentially a git wrapper, but it's allowed me tons of freedom tweaking my setup without worrying about persisting it. It supports encryption and switching file versions based on which system you're on."},{"id":14449914,"author":"santoriv","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: What linux distro should I start with?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Considered the best i3 tutorial on YouTube.","quote":"probably the best i3 tutorial on youtube is running ubuntu.","text":"I use Arch but if you are starting out I would recommend Ubuntu. It's probably got the most youtube videos, online articles, troubleshooting questions online. This make things a bit easier. For example, you mention i3 - probably the best i3 tutorial on youtube is running ubuntu. -> [VIDEO]"},{"id":15572460,"author":"kentt","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your “I wish I did it sooner” moment?","stance":"rec","why":"Long exhaustive way to start with i3.","quote":"If you want to get started the long exhaustive way, start here","text":"i3wm. I thought it couldn't be that much better, it just a window manager. Now it's hard to imagine using anything else. If you want to get started the long exhaustive way, start here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":10645633,"author":"jarboot","month":"2015-11","story":"Switching from OS X to FreeBSD – Both Desktop and Laptop","stance":"rec","why":"Great tutorial for getting started with i3.","quote":"somebody made a great tutorial for it at","text":"For those of you that might be professionals or adults that don't spend much time on 4chan/reddit, you should know that i3 is pretty popular with 4chan.org/g/ or reddit's linux subreddits. It's a pretty effective path for us young nerds to get into the linux ecosystem. Beginners might learn vim to modify their ~/.i3/config, hex values for coloring their desktop, or basic CLI commands to add functionality to their keybindings. Maybe they want to add a customized weather applet to the bar at the bottom of the screen, so they write a shell script utilizing wget. Soon enough they're learning to install Arch Linux (because everybody else on that \"Show off your desktop\" thread is using it), and it's not long before they're a full-blown linux fanboy. That's at least the route I took. It's not that i3 is popular among these communities because it's the easiest, but because it looks the coolest and offers the most amount of visual customization. But after getting used to it, there's an incredible amount of depth and usability that keeps you on it. I really can't imagine a more effective window manager. If you're looking for neat-looking i3 setups, check out reddit.com/r/unixporn/ . If you're interested in getting started with it, somebody made a great tutorial for it at [VIDEO] ."}]},"HdHlfiOAJyE":{"wy":"Recommended as a free, first-principles finance course for software engineers wanting to learn business and finance.","q":{"x":"The course is excellent.","i":23953369,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23953369,"author":"timsally","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: What are some good resources to learn business administration/finance?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finance Theory course taught during 2008 crisis","quote":"The course is excellent.","text":"Finance Theory I by Prof. Andrew Lo: [LINK] . You can view the first lecture here: [VIDEO] . Prof. Lo taught this course at MIT in the Fall of 2008, which you can imagine would be a very interesting time to teach finance. The course is excellent. I think you'll really enjoy it."},{"id":34302697,"author":"gretch","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: How to learn business being a software engineer","stance":"rec","why":"Free MIT finance course from first principles","quote":"MIT free course on finance from first principles.","text":"MIT free course on finance from first principles. Answers questions like the difference between price and value. Why do interest rates exist [VIDEO]"},{"id":34593788,"author":"gadiyar","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Finance Theory lectures given during the 2007-08 crisis","quote":"This series on Finance Theory by Andrew Lo unfolded just during the 2007-08 crisis","text":"Some good ones from Stephen Boyd - helped me really grok the subjects. Signals and Systems: [LINK] Linear Dynamical Systems: [LINK] This series on Finance Theory by Andrew Lo unfolded just during the 2007-08 crisis - so that's an added bonus as the lectures refer to ongoing events sometimes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32315658,"author":"benreesman","month":"2022-08","story":"Understanding Jane Street","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for MIT financial engineering material","quote":"This is a pretty reasonable jumping off point for their corpus of financial engineering stuff","text":"Thank you kindly for what looks like a great resource! I've been trying to put myself through YouTube night school on some of this stuff, and MIT OCW has great resources as well at significantly less cost than going to MIT ;) This is a pretty reasonable jumping off point for their corpus of financial engineering stuff: [VIDEO] . I'm fortunate enough to work with a person who actually understands derivatives trades with some sophistication, but that's a happy accident and the more people have access to good online resources the better! Edit: I forgot to mention this book ( [LINK] ) in the spirit of something more technical than the general-audience one I linked above. I have some nitpicks with it as well, but I've gotten value out of it."},{"id":22243802,"author":"langitbiru","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What Skills to Acquire in 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"MIT finance theory course by Andrew Lo","quote":"But the ones I listed here are good based on my experience.","text":"\"Finance Theory\" from Prof. Andrew Lo (MIT). [VIDEO] He used \"Principles of Corporate Finance\" by Richard Brealey, Stewart Myers, and Franklin Allen as the text book. I bought it from Google Play. For accounting, I use Coursera: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] There are other courses about finance in Coursera and Youtube but I haven't checked them out. But the ones I listed here are good based on my experience."}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["efPrtcLdcdM",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["YQ2QtKcK2dA",1]]},"MkJkyMuBm3g":{"wy":"HN recommends it for the toybox intro and for Landley's candid account of software licensing and why he created the 0BSD license.","q":{"x":"A great talk on software licensing made by the creator of the 0BSD license","i":30554525,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46139758,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2025-12","story":"Ghostty is now non-profit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Landley on why enforcing copyleft led him to abandon it","quote":"You should watch his talk","text":"BSD-0 is a public-domain-equivalent license. The guy who published it is one of the few people who has actually been involved in a lawsuit to try to assert a copyleft license. The whole thing was such a bad experience for him that he decided copyleft licenses are a false goal. You should watch his talk: [VIDEO] (He used to be a maintainer of busybox, a GNU clone for embedded devices. He then ended up writing toybox, a similar project under the more free MIT license.)"},{"id":45488566,"author":"coffeebeanHH","month":"2025-10","story":"Toybox: All-in-one Linux command line","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to Toybox from its author","quote":"There's a good intro on yt [1]","text":"It's sort of a remastered Version of busybox. Please refer to Rob himself for explaining. There's a good intro on yt [1] and a \"demo\" in [2] edit: Well Sorry, [2] was about mkroot. I just skipped through the video. I thought it was related to toybox/busybox. Maybe somewhere in the talk. Has been a while since I last watched it. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30554525,"author":"freemint","month":"2022-03","story":"The Unlicense is an inferior license wrapped in an atrocious name","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on software licensing and the history of 0BSD","quote":"A great talk on software licensing made by the creator of the 0BSD license","text":"A great talk on software licensing made by the creator of the 0BSD license (Rob Landley) and the history behind the license is here [VIDEO]"},{"id":30555516,"author":"freemint","month":"2022-03","story":"The Unlicense is an inferior license wrapped in an atrocious name","stance":"rec","why":"Anecdotes on how lawyers view public-domain-style licenses","quote":"worth the hour","text":"Yes. At least if you want people who have to talk to lawyers to use the code. Lawyers don't like that. For some anecdotes see this talk [VIDEO] (worth the hour, althoug Rob Landley write the same thing on his website too but to lazy to Google that."},{"id":28650578,"author":"tlamponi","month":"2021-09","story":"Toybox: All-in-one Linux command line","stance":"rec","why":"Comparison of Toybox and BusyBox","quote":"Possibly also interesting: Toyboy vs. Busybox","text":"> Toybox's main goal is to make Android self-hosting by improving Android's command line utilities so it can build an installable Android Open Source Project image entirely from source under a stock Android system. -- [LINK] Possibly also interesting: Toyboy vs. Busybox [VIDEO]"},{"id":28655340,"author":"Ciantic","month":"2021-09","story":"Toybox: All-in-one Linux command line","stance":"rec","why":"Landley explains how BusyBox licensing was used to get hardware specs","quote":"which I think as a whole is good watch","text":"I dug up one of his latest YouTube videos, which I think as a whole is good watch. Here is the licensing part: - [VIDEO] (at 25:25) Also I think it's worth to listen the first 10 minutes as an intro, and brief about licensing from 5 minutes forward. Where he explains how BusyBox's licensing was then used by other people as \"beating tool\" to get hardware specs."},{"id":28655416,"author":"Ciantic","month":"2021-09","story":"Toybox: All-in-one Linux command line","stance":"reference","why":"Landley on small code size enabling auditability","quote":"he mentions how people used BusyBox in US army because they wanted to audit all the code","text":"I think with \"few lines of codes as possible\" he wants auditability. Rob mentions it as something of a feature: [VIDEO] about 12 minute mark, he mentions how people used BusyBox in US army because they wanted to audit all the code, and this was his realization. I think it's a worthy goal. But surely if bc is missing some useful features there is probably no harm having multiple implementations."},{"id":25034451,"author":"freemint","month":"2020-11","story":"No More Free Work from Marak: Pay Me or Fork This","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Landley on why he created 0BSD","quote":"I'd recommend looking into Rob Landley and why he created 0BSD.","text":"This is a bad idea. All licenses released under GPLvX or later where X<4 will be not affected by this as the company could always pick X. It would further increase license fragmentation in the GPL sphere which is one of the reasons why BSD style licenses became more popular. I'd recommend looking into Rob Landley and why he created 0BSD. [1] The whole reason why we are in this mess and why GPL died is because the people behind GPL wanted to push their political views using the weight of persons using GPLv2 or later. The Linux kernel reacted by switching GPLv2 only. The only reason why would might want to name it GPLv4 is to use the weight of code of GPLvX or later people. Don't do that In addition some of you clauses have been shown to be easily enforceable by laws like GDPR and attempting to use contract is a crutch. They way to some of your points is phrased might be unenforceable in court and might void the complete license if it is ever testing in court. -> very bad [1] [VIDEO] somewhere around 25 min it starts"}],"r":[["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["6qD9XElTpCE",1],["C2RO34b_oPM",1],["ue-1JoJQaEg",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["0UjpmT5noto",1],["b5P3QDm61go",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["1fZTOjd_bOQ",1]]},"_-mBeYC2KGc":{"wy":"The go-to recommendation to watch before buying a portable AC, cited in nearly every thread on cooling.","q":{"x":"If you are in the market for a portable AC I highly recommend you watch this video first","i":48658870,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48658870,"author":"DanielHB","month":"2026-06","story":"Europeans should learn to love the air-conditioner","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Watch before buying a portable AC","quote":"If you are in the market for a portable AC I highly recommend you watch this video first","text":"If you are in the market for a portable AC I highly recommend you watch this video first: [VIDEO] People seem to be catching on this and some brands now offer portable mini-splits ACs, I just bought this one (waiting for it to arrive though): [LINK] However they are still quite expensive."},{"id":31974736,"author":"aPoCoMiLogin","month":"2022-07","story":"New study lays out hidden backstory behind deadly Pacific Northwest heat wave","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of portable AC inefficiency","quote":"here's better explanation","text":"not op but was in same situation for few years as in my region A/C is somehow luxury and a lot of landlords don't mount them. the issue with portable A/C is that most of the units needs to stay inside apartment, the cooling happens by compression/decompression of coolant, which produces heat and cold. so the A/C unit is producing additional heat that it needs to chill, which makes it inefficient. also these units are loud as fuck, most of them above 65dB. so if you are in need, look for units that have the ability to channel cold air to the house from outside. otherwise you should always avoid portable A/C as it is really inefficient. here's better explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27612772,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2021-06","story":"Will Work for Air-Conditioning","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at why portable ACs are awful","quote":"Technology Connections has a very good video (with perhaps excessive detail, but it is very interesting) about them","text":"If anyone is considering buying a \"portable\" air conditioner - the kind that rolls around on wheels - know that they are pretty awful. If you must buy one, at least buy a \"dual hose\" version. Single hose versions suck air out of your house, causing fresh, but hot, air to be sucked in through all the vents, gaps, and cracks in your house. Window units or \"split\" systems are far more efficient and work much better, at the downside of cost and installation fees. Technology Connections has a very good video (with perhaps excessive detail, but it is very interesting) about them, here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24024704,"author":"slig","month":"2020-08","story":"Hacking Portable Air Conditioners","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why portable air conditioners perform poorly","quote":"Very interesting video from Technology Connections","text":"Very interesting video from Technology Connections < [VIDEO] \"Portable Air Conditioners - Why you shouldn't like them\"."},{"id":24231109,"author":"dghughes","month":"2020-08","story":"“Cold Tube” cooling system uses half the energy of an air conditioner","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why portable ACs are inefficient","quote":"Technology Connections has a good video about this","text":"Portable air conditioners are bad. All the hot components are in the room with you and it takes air from the room and blows it outside. Technology Connections has a good video about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48667090,"author":"zarzavat","month":"2026-06","story":"Extreme Heat conference cancelled due to extreme heat warning","stance":"reference","why":"Rant explaining why portable ACs are borderline scams","quote":"obligatory TC rant","text":"Portable ACs are borderline scams because the hot side is inside the room it's trying to cool, among other reasons[0]. [0] obligatory TC rant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40869031,"author":"rasz","month":"2024-07","story":"Paris Olympics' AC-free ambition melts away as organisers order 2,500 AC units","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for why single hose portable units are worst","quote":"the worst kind","text":"No doubt those will be single hose stand alone units, the worst kind [Portable Air Conditioners - Why you shouldn't like them] by Technology Connections [VIDEO]"},{"id":40884887,"author":"4ad","month":"2024-07","story":"The EU should be the heat-pump pioneer","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for portable AC inefficiency","quote":"","text":"Generally nothing, but those are 1. loud[0], 2. very inefficient and ineffective[1], 3. in the EU there are no dual-hose units for whatever reason. I still use one but it's very far from ideal. [0] so loud that neighbours might complain, I had neighbours complain about my computer fans running 24/7. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37886490,"author":"CapstanRoller","month":"2023-10","story":"Humid Heat Can Kill Us Much Faster Than We Thought","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in discussion of single versus dual hose units","quote":"","text":"Some portable A/C units do send hot air outside, and some don't. The two-hose models AFAIK are the latter type (although the un-insulated hoses are wildly inefficient) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kU3BceoMuaA",1],["KaOC9danxNo",1],["CJlrbMHLBd4",1],["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["Yl_K2Ata6XY",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["gMqZR3pqMjg",1]]},"f0gMdGrVteI":{"wy":"HN links it in semiconductor threads as an accessible overview of why EUV lithography is so hard.","q":{"x":"Seeker released a very insightful feature about the ASML Extreme UV / soft X-ray (13.5 nanometers) photolithography system","i":21431297,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22653680,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-03","story":"TSMC Details 5 nm","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows lithography challenges of shrinking process nodes","quote":"I implore you to watch this","text":"I don't understand callous contempful comments against Intel's manufacturing capabilities. There are incredible people making things possible fomr both sides in one of the most advanced manufacturing processes in the world. I've worked inside a fab and I can tell you that the industry is much more appreciative of each other's competitors than the outside fanboys who are largely pissed off at the executive decisions (lawsuits , etc). This article is about TSMC's technical achievements and your comment has not added anything to the discussion, time and again I see this rooting for \"underdog\" behavior all too the same (just flipped the sides) from 2006. I wrote about it here: [LINK] If you'd like to know what goes into shriking a process node from lithography standpoint, I implore you to watch this: [VIDEO] and be in complete utter awe... Tell me if they are \"Sitting on their asses\"?"},{"id":33652448,"author":"sllabres","month":"2022-11","story":"ASML looking to finally get ahead of Moore’s Law","stance":"rec","why":"High level overview of the EUV machine","quote":"as on high level overview of the machine","text":"Cant answer the question and my knowledge is only from 'one interested' but from how long (~20 years) it took to build the current EUV source and how complicated the alternatives look this is for sure no easy feat. Can recommend [1] as the title say about the light source. Very interesting even for a non professional (and [2] as on high level overview of the machine and [3] as longer documentation inside ASML). [1] [LINK] \"The Extreme Engineering of ASML’s EUV Light Source\" [2] [VIDEO] \"The Extreme Physics Pushing Moore’s Law to the Next Level\" [3] [LINK] \"ASML's Secret: An exclusive view from inside the global semiconductor giant\""},{"id":23764928,"author":"jcun4128","month":"2020-07","story":"Google offers free fabbing for 130nm open-source chips","stance":"rec","why":"Cool video on lithography with plasma lasers","quote":"This was also a cool video I saw recently about lithography with plasma lasers","text":"This was also a cool video I saw recently about lithography with plasma lasers [VIDEO] around 7:14 in particular"},{"id":23977439,"author":"SonOfLilit","month":"2020-07","story":"Intel Ponte Vecchio GPU Will Be Made on TSMC 6nm Process, CPUs Could Be TSMC 3nm","stance":"rec","why":"Challenges solved for a modern process","quote":"This is about some of the challenges that had to be solved for a current modern process","text":"Each additional step requires solving a lot of new engineering problems and usually some new physics too, and it's not a linear process: many techniques are proposed that become cost effective only when previous techniques hit their size limits, and then a lot of research is done and most techniques are never gotten to work at scale and just a few pan out and become the next process. This is a great talk on the history of chip processes: [LINK] This is about some of the challenges that had to be solved for a current modern process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22515976,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-03","story":"New AMD side channel attacks discovered, impacts Zen architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Shows semiconductor industry positively","quote":"Perhaps videos such as these are shining a positive light on the entire semiconductor goodwill","text":"I think the culture is improving despite of cashing it out in the 90's and 2000's during the Ballmer / Jack Welsch era. Semiconductor industry hasn't done a good job of cultivating a good, positive company culture and making it less regimented. We need to make it cool to work in a semiconductor company. I can assure you that there are so many incredible challenges that exist - with good definition/funding to tackle them from materials to marketing (direly needs to revamped). Imagine running a portion of the world's most advanced manufacturing processes. Ultra high volume (thousands of wafer starts per week), mind you. Perhaps videos such as these are shining a positive light on the entire semiconductor goodwill: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21431297,"author":"car","month":"2019-11","story":"TSMC 5-Nanometer Update","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful feature on ASML EUV photolithography","quote":"Seeker released a very insightful feature about the ASML Extreme UV / soft X-ray (13.5 nanometers) photolithography system","text":"Seeker released a very insightful feature about the ASML Extreme UV / soft X-ray (13.5 nanometers) photolithography system that is being referred to in the article. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34911741,"author":"deelowe","month":"2023-02","story":"Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller start a new semiconductor fab","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how complex chip lithography is, including why masks fail with x-rays.","quote":"The reality of how this is done is so much more complex than I would have thought","text":"> That's why it seemed weird that photolithography would be so much faster, but maybe it's as you say, lasers can be stacked for parallelism to make up for those downsides. The reality of how this is done is so much more complex than I would have thought: [VIDEO] Traditional techniques such as masks don't work when dealing with xrays."},{"id":25094401,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2020-11","story":"Intel's disruption is now complete","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The entire world has their eggs in one basket - Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company. Say whatever about Intel, I am hoping that they get back on their feet in next few years with 5nm manufacturing. Since when is competition and choice a bad thing? If you're a chip design firm and you have a 8-month backlog to get your masks made at TSMC, what do you do then? TSMC can be world's best fab but we need to address the SPOF condition that is so close to the political hot zone in the APEC region. Intel should spin off its Fab division as a separate company, hire some top executives to rejuvinate the workforce, make it the coolest Fab company in the world to work for and leap ahead. Intel TMG's Sohail was the biggest borderline criminal executive that was forced out a couple of years ago and was responsible for the 10nm delays. It is enormously difficult for any startup to get into this space - you need billions and years of expertise to start a Fab. Or may be YC folks can? I worked in the Fab but don't have a birds-eye view of what it takes for a startup to get into this space. It would be amazing to see startups trying to manufacture semiconductors... you know live up to the Silicon Valley's name. You could have billions of dollars of economy built on services - US, EU and most advanced economies are shifting towards services; but they all rely on semiconductors. Imagine something were to happen to that... It is a juicy problem. We don't need Uber/AirBnb unicorns out of SV, "},{"id":25131424,"author":"car","month":"2020-11","story":"2020 Mac Mini – Putting Apple Silicon M1 To The Test","stance":"reference","why":"Explains EUV light projection optics","quote":"ASML and it's suppliers have built a machine[1] that has a sci-fi feel to it.","text":"Worth mentioning is the insane and disruptive technology making TSMC's 5nm possible. ASML and it's suppliers have built a machine[1] that has a sci-fi feel to it. It took decades to get it all right, from the continous laser source to the projection optics for the extreme ultraviolet light[2]. This allowed photolithography to jump from 193nm light to 13.5nm, very close to x-rays. The CO2 laser powering the EUV light source is made by Trumpf[3]. Edit:More hands-on video from Engadget about EUV at Intels Oregon facility[4] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["-hCimLnIsDA",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["W07ZpEv9Sog",1],["5Ge2RcvDlgw",1],["ZMQbHMgK2rw",1]]},"0VyH1laOd2M":{"wy":"Recommended to people with chronic pain, fatigue or RSI as a rigorous but balanced case that pain does not always mean injury.","q":{"x":"The author links to Schubiner's talk on chronic pain, which I heartily recommend as the right balance of rigor and tone","i":43990929,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43990929,"author":"wonger_","month":"2025-05","story":"Beyond the Wrist: Debugging RSI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rigorous yet balanced talk on pain not meaning injury.","quote":"The author links to Schubiner's talk on chronic pain, which I heartily recommend as the right balance of rigor and tone","text":"The article matches my experience and that of many others. It also mentions some key ideas that I haven't seen in other RSI/pain-epiphany writeups: - It may take a variety of resources to accept the idea that your pain doesn't mean injury (see the first image). Like, you might hear about Sarno's mindbody connection and think it's total quackery because of the language. Maybe it triggers the BS detector in a lot of us. So listen to the same ideas from other perspectives. The author links to Schubiner's talk on chronic pain, which I heartily recommend as the right balance of rigor and tone: [VIDEO] . Curable helped me too: [LINK] . Click through until you get to the audio sections and give them a listen. Testimonials from tech people may help break the old mental model of pain, or at least point convincingly to resources that do. Josh Comeau, the CSS guy, has a post that links to further testimonials: https:www.joshwcomeau.com/blog/mindbody - The second half of healing is calming down the nervous system. The author accomplished this with different therapies, equipped with greater awareness and tools. Others fix their default stress state by leaving the stressful environment, i.e. changing jobs. Either way, the pain goes away. Kudos to the author for presenting everything clearly."},{"id":20872183,"author":"JoeyJoJoJunior","month":"2019-09","story":"A Town for People with Chronic Fatigue Syndrome","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on healing chronic pain as a mind-body condition.","quote":"If you think you suffer from CFS please check out Dr. Howard Schubiner and read his book.","text":"If you think you suffer from CFS please check out Dr. Howard Schubiner and read his book. [VIDEO] I suffered from chronic pain symptoms for almost 10 years (was unable play the piano and type on the keyboard) until I realized it was 100% psychosomatic."},{"id":27229428,"author":"rajlego","month":"2021-05","story":"The anus is an evolutionary marvel","stance":"rec","why":"Explains psychosomatic nature of pain.","quote":"good video on this","text":"If you read some of John Sarno's stuff on psychosomatic pain, it seems highly plausible that most pain is just psychosomatic in nature. [VIDEO] good video on this"}],"r":[["DoQoeK53bP8",1],["zyVTxGpEO30",1]]},"HMBl0ttu-Ow":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for the finding that idea exchange and connectivity drive productivity and wealth, which they apply to cities, offices and online communities.","q":{"x":"There's a great Authors at Google talk by Sandy Pentland","i":21750224,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21750224,"author":"patcon","month":"2019-12","story":"How Trolls Overran the Public Square","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great overview of the book for people working with large groups.","quote":"There's a great Authors at Google talk by Sandy Pentland","text":"Yay! Social physics :) There's a great Authors at Google talk by Sandy Pentland, about his book of the same name. Gives a good overview of the book, but 100% still worth a read after watching, esp if you are someone who works with (or would like to work with) large groups of people -- event organizing, companies, community, group facilitation, etc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11990600,"author":"legel","month":"2016-06","story":"New Cities","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Connectivity drives economic and social wealth, per Pentland's research.","quote":"The most profound thing I know about cities + public policy","text":"The most profound thing I know about cities + public policy: Google hosted a talk in 2014 by MIT Professor Sandy Pentland called \"Social Physics: How Good Ideas Spread\". His research concludes that our connectivity - free and fast flow of data on networks, people on roads, goods and services, etc. - is our greatest economic and social virtue. His models show - \"like a law\" - that the more connected we are, the wealthier we are as a society. Check it out: [VIDEO] And good luck to everyone behind this."},{"id":10166972,"author":"gd2","month":"2015-09","story":"Two scientists meeting on a bus leads to discovery in the lab","stance":"rec","why":"Random idea exchanges boost productivity; design common areas accordingly.","quote":"the productivity gains of the seemingly random exchanges of ideas","text":"Sandy Pentland: \"Social Physics: How Good Ideas Spread\" | Talks at Google similar theme. [VIDEO] I think its in this video that he talks about the productivity gains of the seemingly random exchanges of ideas, and the possibility to increase them by scheduling and thoughtfully designed common areas."},{"id":10281500,"author":"legel","month":"2015-09","story":"From Radio to Porn, British Spies Track Web Users’ Online Identities","stance":"rec","why":"Research showing more connected societies are wealthier.","quote":"the more connected we are, the wealthier we are as a society","text":"You bring up two very interesting and powerful phenomena: \"siren servers\" and \"social physics\". The first term is used by Jaron Lanier to describe computer systems that, like \"sirens\" of the sea, lure everyone into their beautiful environs, collect all of the data on them, and statistically model all of this data in ways that effectively master the nature of this individual - her beliefs, her abilities, her emotions - until ultimately that individual is made both completely vulnerable and completely powerless. Lanier's book is intentionally provocative and controversial, while rather intelligent and compassionate in its spirit, and I recommend checking it out: [LINK] . The second term comes from a talk at Google in 2014 called \"Social Physics: How Good Ideas Spread\" ( [VIDEO] ) by MIT Professor Sandy Pentland. He describes research supporting the recent Nature publication you link to on social and cultural progress, which concludes that our connectivity - free and fast flow of data on networks, people on roads, goods and services across nations, etc. - is our greatest economic and social virtue. His statistical modeling results really strongly show - \"like a law\" - that the more connected we are, the wealthier we are as a society. It's really interesting to consider how we could concentrate on using all of the data and models to make deep positive impacts on our society, while respecting our human dignity. To quote Pope Francis's address to the U.N. today: \"Integral human dev"},{"id":32715710,"author":"patcon","month":"2022-09","story":"I tricked myself into working out 3 times per week","stance":"reference","why":"Related research on incentivizing social relationships rather than individuals.","quote":"","text":"Nice! In case anyone is interested in the science of this effect and it's surprising power, this is an almost exact reproduction of an experiment, in the field of \"connection science\" or \"social physics\". (NOTE: The true value here is in the CROSS-INCENTIVIZATION with an existing social relationship, not just the money) The general truism that falls out of it is: When possible, always apply incentives to the social fabric, not individuals. i.e. incentivize the edges of the graph (the relationships), rather than the nodes (the people) in isolation. Seek results by always opting to strengthen social bonds, instead of trying to bend people (who are social creatures) into actions while neglecting their social context, which often pull them back into prior habits or prevent the new ones from sticking strongly. Under some experimental conditions, incentives are 30x more effective when you use the money to strengthen a social relationship around an activity, rather than incentivizing the activity itself. (Further, unlike a direct monetary incentive where removing the incentive strongly attenuates the effect, you could now remove the money incentive between you and your brother, and you're much more likely to keep performing the activity even without monetary incentive, because the persistent social incentive is bootstrapped.) This and other really interesting related research was pioneered by MIT's Sandy Pentland. It's very solid and applies in practice: His lab won the DARPA networ"},{"id":19715902,"author":"patcon","month":"2019-04","story":"Parable of the Polygons (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Social physics research relevant to diversity of ideas in groups.","quote":"The field of social physics has lots to say about this","text":"The field of social physics has lots to say about this: [VIDEO] [LINK] > What pattern of exploration and social learning produced the best outcome? We discovered the answer when we plotted the return on investment each trader got against the diversity of ideas he or she harvested through social learning. Social physics supports a framing that groups are like \"machines for finding the best ideas\", and having group members with access to diverse sources of ideas (polygons from different spaces and experiences count as this) is part of a properly calibrated social network where the best ideas are within reach, to address any given challenge that may surface."}],"r":[["oYp5XuGYqqY",1],["86Krv3gE-c4",1]]},"XTeJ64KD5cg":{"wy":"Recommended as the mind-melting intro to unimaginably large numbers.","q":{"x":"It's a pretty interesting video for seeing how quickly the numbers get unimaginably massive.","i":19971071,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40396384,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2024-05","story":"A Trial HIV Vaccine Triggered Elusive and Essential Antibodies in Humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Numberphile series on huge numbers used in proofs, mind-bending.","quote":"If math can make you feel awe, you've got to watch the Numberphile series on very big numbers.","text":"If math can make you feel awe, you've got to watch the Numberphile series on very big numbers. They're constants used in proofs that are specific, well-defined integers known to be finite, but so huge that the physical universe does not have enough particles (or even Plank-scale microstates) in it to represent the number of digits these numbers have! Absolutely mind-bending stuff. Listed in increasing order: \"Graham's Number\" [VIDEO] \"How Big is Graham's Number? (feat Ron Graham)\" [LINK] \"The Enormous TREE(3)\" [LINK] \"TREE(3) (extra footage)\" [LINK] \"The Daddy of Big Numbers (Rayo's Number)\" [LINK]"},{"id":9054482,"author":"prezjordan","month":"2015-02","story":"Graham's Number","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-melting Numberphile video on Graham's number.","quote":"My brain melts every time I watch this video [1] from Numberphile on Graham's number.","text":"My brain melts every time I watch this video [1] from Numberphile on Graham's number. They also had another video [2] with Ron Graham himself :) [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":19971071,"author":"johnsonjo","month":"2019-05","story":"Knuth's Up-Arrow Notation","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how quickly numbers become unimaginably massive.","quote":"It's a pretty interesting video for seeing how quickly the numbers get unimaginably massive.","text":"I've seen this notation in a video [1] on Graham's number [2] by Numberphile. It's a pretty interesting video for seeing how quickly the numbers get unimaginably massive. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":9055923,"author":"Ronsenshi","month":"2015-02","story":"Graham's Number","stance":"rec","why":"Numberphile gives a great explanation of Graham's number.","quote":"Numberphile [1] too has a great explanation","text":"I love this video [LINK] by Sean Day[9] Plott describing Graham's number very well. At least I found that description very easy to follow. Numberphile [1] too has a great explanation, but I still strongly recommend watching Day[9] video because it tells a little bit of history and Sean is very expressive and good speaker so he easily engages viewer. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15645466,"author":"Balgair","month":"2017-11","story":"Ray Kurzweil thinks we could start living forever by 2029","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"10E65 if protons do decay. 10E(10E76) if protons do not decay and virtual black-holes do not exist. At about 10E(10E(10E56)) the universe should spontaneously create a new universe, so that that is the most upper limit of time in our universe. Fairly small actually, compared to things like Graham's number and Tree(3). Turns out, infinity is really big. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6000090,"author":"ColinDabritz","month":"2013-07","story":"Indescribable numbers: The theorem that made me fall in love with math","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Graham's number as largest number used in a proof.","quote":"Graham's number - for a while, the largest number used in a proof","text":"Graham's number - for a while, the largest number used in a proof: [VIDEO] There are faster growing sequences as well, see: [LINK] both fun as well. But busy beaver consumes them all."}],"r":[["GuigptwlVHo",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1]]},"rn7MmS3vazU":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a favorite audiovisual experience, with some calling the sync the best thing they have seen.","q":{"x":"the sync of Echoes and 2001 is the BEST thing I have ever seen in my life","i":28862995,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":48163846,"author":"jcb0wman","month":"2026-05","story":"Echoes (Live at Pompeii)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Echoes synced to the end of 2001","quote":"My favorite Echoes experience will always be listening to it synced to the end of 2001 A Space Odyssey.","text":"My favorite Echoes experience will always be listening to it synced to the end of 2001 A Space Odyssey. When I was a teen I used to have to manually sync it up with a DVD and CD player. Fortunately some hero now has now posted it on youtube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28862995,"author":"lolive","month":"2021-10","story":"Pink Floyd's Young Lust – explained and demystified","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Echoes synced with 2001 ending","quote":"the sync of Echoes and 2001 is the BEST thing I have ever seen in my life","text":"I suppose this is old news for the Pink Floyd nerds around. But the sync of Echoes and 2001 is the BEST thing I have ever seen in my life : [VIDEO]"},{"id":36467292,"author":"floor_","month":"2023-06","story":"Pink Floyd, 'The Wizard of Oz,' and Me","stance":"rec","why":"Echoes lines up with the ending of 2001","quote":"Echoes lines up with the ending of 2001: A Space Odyssey.","text":"I think this one it much more interesting: [VIDEO] Echoes lines up with the ending of 2001: A Space Odyssey."}]},"CbIZU8cQWXc":{"wy":"Recommended to anyone asking whether space exploration is worth the cost.","q":{"x":"I always recommend anyone wondering about whether or not space exploration is worth it to view the following amazing video compilations.","i":8597549,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8597549,"author":"ButchDriveshaft","month":"2014-11","story":"Philae has landed","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compilation on whether space exploration is worth it.","quote":"I always recommend anyone wondering about whether or not space exploration is worth it to view the following amazing video compilations.","text":"I had to create an account just to thank you for this response. I always recommend anyone wondering about whether or not space exploration is worth it to view the following amazing video compilations. \"We Stopped Dreaming - Episode 1\": [VIDEO] \"We Stopped Dreaming - Episode 2\": [LINK]"},{"id":32638985,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-08","story":"Launch Attempt Scrubbed","stance":"rec","why":"Tyson discusses NASA budget and related societal changes.","quote":"though it's all a good watch.","text":"There's a Neil deGrasse Tyson video titled We Stopped Dreaming that gets into the NASA budget (and the related society changes) [VIDEO] The part about the NASA budget is at 2:00 though it's all a good watch."},{"id":3993871,"author":"dudurocha","month":"2012-05","story":"NASDAQ:FB","stance":"rec","why":"Tyson on the value of space exploration.","quote":"And the great Neil deGrasse Tyson , in the pretty \"We stopped Dreaming\"","text":"I got to disagree. Not in the part that Facebook was important to Arab Spring and reconnect people long seen. I desagree in the part that rockets doesn't give benefits. Rockets and all 'complicated' science, push the human race forward. Your cellphone is only able to connect to the internet because of the rockets that were send to the atmosphere, long time ago. Better than me, I have to people to talk about this. Read Science and Culture, by Thomas Huxley : How often have we not been told that the study of physical science is incompetent to confer culture; that it touches none of the higher problems of life; and, what is worse, that the continual devotion to scientific studies tends to generate a narrow and bigoted belief in the applicability of scientific methods to the search after truth of all kinds. How frequently one has reason to observe that no reply to a troublesome argument tells so well as calling its author a \"mere scientific specialist.\" And, as I am afraid it is not permissible to speak of this form of opposition to scientific education in the past tense; may we not expect to be told that this, not only omission, but prohibition, of \"mere literary instruction and education\" is a patent example of scientific narrow-mindedness? source: [LINK] And the great Neil deGrasse Tyson , in the pretty \"We stopped Dreaming\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":43283706,"author":"shagie","month":"2025-03","story":"NASA Turns Off 2 Voyager Science Instruments to Extend Mission","stance":"reference","why":"Tyson talk that motivated the commenter to dream about space again.","quote":"","text":"Neil deGrasse Tyson - We Stopped Dreaming - [VIDEO] Not so much \"sources\" but rather it was a \"this was not the goal\". (Side bit - after watching \"We Stopped Dreaming\" I find it necessary to watch Wanderers to dream again. [LINK] )"},{"id":9906617,"author":"Alupis","month":"2015-07","story":"NASA’s New Horizons Discovers Frozen Plains in the Heart of Pluto’s ‘Heart’","stance":"reference","why":"Tyson argues NASA's budget is tiny relative to federal spending.","quote":"How much would you pay for the universe?","text":"> I'm tired of saying this, but I have to say it again. The NASA budget is four tenths of one penny on a tax dollar. If I held up the tax dollar, and I cut horizontally into it, four tenths of one percent of it's width, it doesn't even get you into the ink. So I will not accept a statement that says, we can't afford it! > Do you realize that the 850 billion dollar bank bailout; that sum of money is greater than the entire fifty year running budget of NASA. > How much would you pay for the universe? [1] \"We Stopped Dreaming\" - Neil deGrasse Tyson - [VIDEO]"},{"id":9947318,"author":"visakanv","month":"2015-07","story":"NASA estimates 1B ‘Earths’ in our galaxy alone","stance":"reference","why":"Tyson on how spaceflight inspires people and shifts worldviews.","quote":"It's also about inspiring people and radically changing people's worldviews.","text":"Human spaceflight isn't just about scientific breakthrough, or even planetary life insurance. It's also about inspiring people and radically changing people's worldviews. After all, once we're on two planets, it becomes \"best in the worlds\". The implications this has on people's imaginations is non-trivial: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5855455,"author":"joering2","month":"2013-06","story":"The Irrationality of Giving Up This Much Liberty to Fight Terror","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts NASA cost with war spending.","quote":"Cost of entire NASA program is pennies of the war cost...","text":"Cost of entire NASA program is pennies of the war cost... unfortunately there is more money to make at war, than at creating/maintaining peace and continuing humankind progress. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"NeopkvAP-ag":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever analog versus digital clocks come up, as an idea that changed how commenters think about time perception.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections has a very good video on this, and completely changed thinking","i":32784002,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32784002,"author":"operator-name","month":"2022-09","story":"Some things I realized about AI while contemplating slide rule prices on eBay","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains digital versus analog clocks and how people perceive time differently.","quote":"Technology Connections has a very good video on this, and completely changed thinking","text":"I think the digital vs analogue clock is an adjacent, everyday example. On the human scale precise clock maths is rarely necessary, and conceptually thinking of time as a base 60 number can be more trouble than good. Technology Connections has a very good video on this, and completely changed thinking: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48847967,"author":"ajdude","month":"2026-07","story":"Show HN: Analog Watch","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how analog versus digital upbringing shapes intuition of time passing.","quote":"Technology Connections did a really great video on this a few years ago.","text":"Technology Connections did a really great video on this a few years ago. [VIDEO] Apparently being raised with analog clocks vs digital changes how one intuits the passage of time."},{"id":41316876,"author":"saurik","month":"2024-08","story":"Designing my own watch (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay on mental models of time from analog versus digital clocks.","quote":"I rather enjoyed the video essay Technology Connections did about the seemingly different mental models of tracking time","text":"I rather enjoyed the video essay Technology Connections did about the seemingly different mental models of tracking time that come from analog and digital clocks, and how a lot of people now have barely ever experienced analog clocks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48870986,"author":"grishka","month":"2026-07","story":"Alternate Clock Designs and Time Systems","stance":"reference","why":"On how different people think of time differently.","quote":"Technology Connections made a video about this","text":"Related: different people think of time different ways. I myself have never liked analog clocks because they require a conversion to make sense . Technology Connections made a video about this: [VIDEO] And another thing: I considered trying 28-hour days because my sleeping schedule always drifts. A 28-hour clock that's in sync with me sleeping would be nice if I ever do try that."},{"id":46263679,"author":"Telaneo","month":"2025-12","story":"Kids Rarely Read Whole Books Anymore. Even in English Class","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how analog-first people perceive time as a progress bar.","quote":"To him and plenty of other analogue-first people, time is a progress bar, or a chart","text":"Not to other people I've talked to. I'm the wrong person to ask this about, since I prefer digital time, so time is just a number to me. But Technology Connections made a video atleast talking about it,[1] so hopefully that get part of the point across. To him and plenty of other analogue-first people, time is a progress bar, or a chart, or something along those lines, and that's the natural way to perceive time, and converting it to a number is meaningless beyond expressing it as digital time. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46413004,"author":"wdbm","month":"2025-12","story":"NYC phone ban reveals some students can't read clocks","stance":"reference","why":"Argues analog clocks work as visual progress bars.","quote":"Analogue clocks are, by design, also visual progress bars.","text":">What benefit is there to keep this antiquated method around, aside from just as an historical reference? Analogue clocks are, by design, also visual progress bars. Digital clocks just give you the time. There's a little video talking about this by Technology Connections: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Npd_xDuNi9k",1]]},"d0vY0CKYhPY":{"wy":"Recommended whenever the Mandelbrot set comes up, as a neat and surprising result.","q":{"x":"My absolute favorite numberphile video is how pi occurs in a peculiar way with Riemann Zeta/Mandelbrot.","i":20915607,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20915607,"author":"elamje","month":"2019-09","story":"The Riemann Hypothesis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Surprising way pi appears in the Mandelbrot set","quote":"My absolute favorite numberphile video is how pi occurs in a peculiar way with Riemann Zeta/Mandelbrot.","text":"My absolute favorite numberphile video is how pi occurs in a peculiar way with Riemann Zeta/Mandelbrot. Maybe it only amazes me because I don’t have a PhD in math, but it just seemed so strange how pi shows up in this video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20062075,"author":"throwawaymath","month":"2019-05","story":"Generating the Mandelbrot Set","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how pi emerges from the Mandelbrot set","quote":"There's a neat video explaining it here","text":"When you begin with z = k i - 0.75, you can obtain an arbitrarily good approximation of pi by multiplying the p for which f_p(z) = z^2 + z diverges by k . The approximation increases in precision as k tends to 0. There's a neat video explaining it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13999795,"author":"kpil","month":"2017-03","story":"The Mandelwat Set","stance":"rec","why":"Numberphile with Holly Krieger on pi and Mandelbrot","quote":"I really enjoyed this video with Dr Holly Krieger of Numberphile.","text":"I really enjoyed this video with Dr Holly Krieger of Numberphile. [LINK] There are a few more videos related to PI and The Mandelbrot set, Julia, etc. All nice to watch. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SWEts0rlezA",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["LKtk3HCgTa8",1],["NaL_Cb42WyY",1]]},"ds77e3aO9nA":{"wy":"Recommended as an engaging, non-technical history of Unix and BSD from someone who was there, and cited for specific stories like the TCP/IP stack.","q":{"x":"but the whole thing is excellent.","i":6563326,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6563326,"author":"chubot","month":"2013-10","story":"The Government Shutdown Has Revealed Silicon Valley’s Dysfunction Fetish","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How DARPA funding led to cooperation and open source BSD","quote":"but the whole thing is excellent.","text":"EDIT: Watch this video for details! [VIDEO] Key quotes around 8:30 - 9:00, but the whole thing is excellent. DARPA was funding all sorts of different networking and operating systems, which in those days were all incompatible. But they wanted them to cooperate, hence open source BSD and the Internet. Silicon Valley doesn't get this kind of cooperation. In stark contrast, a proven business tactic is to undermine cooperation by poisoning open standards (e.g. embrace and extend)."},{"id":13610280,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-02","story":"Looking for Work After 25 Years of Octave","stance":"rec","why":"Refresher on how Unix and BSD spread in universities","quote":"If you have some time to refresh memory.","text":"If you have some time to refresh memory. \"A Narrative History of BSD by Dr. Kirk McKusick\" [VIDEO] \"Unix came into many CS departments largely because it was the only powerful interactive system that could run on the sort of hardware (PDP-11s) that universities could afford in the mid '70s. In addition, Unix itself was also very inexpensive. Since source code was provided, it was a system that could be shaped to the requirements of a particular installation.\" [LINK]"},{"id":7610225,"author":"networked","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: Your favorite YouTube channels?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting non-technical talk on BSD history","quote":"An interesting non-technical talk from the collection is","text":"BSDs, game design and computer history: — [LINK] collects talks from various BSD conferences. An interesting non-technical talk from the collection is \"A Narrative History of BSD\" by Marshall Kirk McKusick ( [VIDEO] ). — [LINK] — lectures on game design and development by Chris Crawford ( Balance of Power , The Art of Computer Game Design ) uploaded by the man himself. \"The Dragon Speech\" of his can be found elsewhere on YouTube ( [LINK] ). — [LINK] — early computer graphics demo reels, mostly from the '80s."},{"id":11437382,"author":"krylon","month":"2016-04","story":"Backslash must die","stance":"reference","why":"McKusick recounts Bill Joy's TCP/IP stack replacing BBN's in BSD","quote":"There is a talk by Kirk McKusick on YouTube[1] where he tells about that time","text":"There is a talk by Kirk McKusick on YouTube[1] where he tells about that time, and IIRC, Bill Joy got his hands on an early version of BBN's code, started hacking on it and put it into BSD. BBN and DARPA were not very amused, but by the time BBN's stack was finished, the BSD version had better performance and/or lower packet loss, so it stuck. Apparently Mike Muuss compared the two implementations, and his recommendation to go with Bill Joy's code was the decisive factor. [1] [VIDEO] - I think that's the one"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1]]},"ggUduBmvQ_4":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it whenever airline economics or frequent-flier miles come up, since it clearly shows loyalty programs are worth more than the airlines themselves.","q":{"x":"Wendover Productions had a good video about this, framed around United getting a loan during the pandemic","i":37606599,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37597876,"author":"somethingsidont","month":"2023-09","story":"Airlines Are Just Banks Now","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Airlines act like central banks controlling their points supply.","quote":"One of my favorite YouTube videos on this subject","text":"One of my favorite YouTube videos on this subject: [VIDEO] Key point: airlines are more powerful than normal banks - they are central banks, with complete control of the money (point) supply. On the trilemma [0], they chose to control the exchange rate (points to flight value) and have an independent monetary policy (how many points to issue to flyers or other buyers). [0] [LINK]"},{"id":48003498,"author":"rapatel0","month":"2026-05","story":"Let's Buy Spirit Air","stance":"rec","why":"Explains airlines earn their money from loyalty programs and credit cards, not flights.","quote":"Useful watch (skip to 2:20)","text":"Fundamental problem: Flights don't make money. Airlines actually make all of their money through loyalty programs and credit card payments. They basically should have turned into regulated utilities long ago, but loyalty program revenue saved them. Unless this initiative will turn into a credit card company (which nobody likes or wants to do) it won't go anywhere Private equity will likely sell the company for parts. There is no operational improvements for cash flow that they can do. Useful watch (skip to 2:20): [VIDEO]"},{"id":48009344,"author":"risfriend","month":"2026-05","story":"Let's Buy Spirit Air","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how major airlines are more like banks that happen to have planes.","quote":"Here is a decent video explaining it","text":"> how the major airlines now are more of a \"bank that happens to have planes\" Here is a decent video explaining it [VIDEO]"},{"id":37606599,"author":"trimbo","month":"2023-09","story":"Airlines Are Just Banks Now","stance":"rec","why":"Airline-as-bank framing around United's pandemic loan.","quote":"Wendover Productions had a good video about this, framed around United getting a loan during the pandemic","text":"Wendover Productions had a good video about this, framed around United getting a loan during the pandemic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30314422,"author":"hourislate","month":"2022-02","story":"Things Delta told the SEC about its SkyMiles program","stance":"rec","why":"Loyalty programs worth several times more than the airlines.","quote":"Wendover Productions has a video on how airlines are banks now.","text":"Wendover Productions has a video on how airlines are banks now. Their Loyalty Programs are worth several times more than the Airlines and flying is basically a secondary business for them. How Airlines Quietly Became Banks [VIDEO]"},{"id":48384095,"author":"handle584","month":"2026-06","story":"Squillions – two books about money-laundering","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source that airlines profit by selling miles in bulk to banks.","quote":"Airlines do not make money by flying people nowadays, but by selling miles in bulk to banks","text":"On average yes, but for savvy people they are worth much more for biz/first class travel. There is even a whole industry built around mileage booking. Why subsidize? There is no subsidize at all, an empty seat on a flight is an empty seat unsold, any money they can make back is extra profit. Airlines do not make money by flying people nowadays, but by selling miles in bulk to banks [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45880197,"author":"canucker2016","month":"2025-11","story":"Reminder to passengers ahead of move to 100% digital boarding passes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows airline reward programs are worth more than the airlines' market caps.","quote":"The market cap of each airline is less than the value of each airline's reward program at the time the video was made.","text":"There's a YouTube video that talks about how valuable the major US airlines reward program is. The market cap of each airline is less than the value of each airline's reward program at the time the video was made. I recall Apple was valued in a similar manner back in the late 1990s. Their market cap was barely more than cash on hand. Talk about missed investing opportunities... Not that I'm saying airlines are the next AAPL. edit - here's the video - \"How Airlines Quietly Became Banks\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44371151,"author":"GeneralMayhem","month":"2025-06","story":"The economics behind \"Basic Economy\" – A masterclass in price discrimination","stance":"reference","why":"Longer overview with numbers and sources on airline loyalty program economics.","quote":"Here's a longer overview with numbers and sources","text":"It's not just time-value. It's also not just tying/advertising (although it is some of that - if I'm getting a ton of \"free\" points to American, I'm more likely to fly with them). It's both of those, and so much more. Loyalty points work like gift cards in that huge numbers of them go unredeemed for any value, so selling them is just printing money. And unlike gift cards, which are typically denominated in currency, airline points don't have a fixed exchange rate to USD, so the airline can sell them to Chase or whatever for $0.01, and then if it needs to rebalance the books to shed the outstanding liability it can easily adjust the point costs of flights to make them only worth $0.009 - it's the same as a price hike, but in a way that's less noticeable to most customers most of the time. And that's assuming they don't just sell the points at an outright profit to begin with. You can find a number of analyses showing that airlines operate at a loss if you set aside the miles-economy revenue streams. United famously got a line of credit secured against their loyalty program in 2020, in which they and their creditors valued the loyalty program at more than the value of the entire company of United Airlines - which would naively imply that the actual airline, the part of the company that owns large expensive machines and actually sells a product to consumers, had negative value. Here's a longer overview with numbers and sources - [VIDEO]"},{"id":43335547,"author":"nharada","month":"2025-03","story":"Southwest Airlines will charge to check bags","stance":"reference","why":"Argues airline operations are a loss-leader for credit card and points financial functions.","quote":"There's an interesting Wendover video[1] that argues the airline operations of the legacy carriers is just a loss-leader for their financial functions","text":"This is actually a huge part of a modern airline's business. There's an interesting Wendover video[1] that argues the airline operations of the legacy carriers is just a loss-leader for their financial functions like credit cards, points partners, etc. United (my home airline) recently adjusted their frequent flier system to basically stop anyone without a credit card or tons of business travel from getting status. Looks like Southwest wanted a piece of that pie. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37599592,"author":"jrwiegand","month":"2023-09","story":"Airlines Are Just Banks Now","stance":"reference","why":"Digs into how airlines make money from points.","quote":"I watched a video about this topic a while back","text":"Interesting, I watched a video about this topic a while back[0]. I don't remember it exploring the larger impacts related to government and such but instead digs into how exactly airlines make money from this system. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30013755,"author":"supercanuck","month":"2022-01","story":"American Airlines and The Points Guy  are suing each other","stance":"reference","why":"Airline miles are worth more than airlines themselves.","quote":"the Airline Frequent flier miles are worth more than the actual airline themselves making the airlines essentially banks.","text":"One thing that has changed in the airline industry that wasn't the case when the points guy started, is that the Airline Frequent flier miles are worth more than the actual airline themselves making the airlines essentially banks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30014073,"author":"vineyardmike","month":"2022-01","story":"American Airlines and The Points Guy  are suing each other","stance":"reference","why":"Easy-to-follow source on airline rewards profits.","quote":"Easy to follow source","text":"It obviously causes them harm. The points are often redeemed at a discount to their regular tickets... which are often loss leaders for their points business. Most airlines lose money on every ticket, and make it up in their rewards programs. Only to lose money again on redemption. They really only make money on some of the inefficiencies.. Easy to follow source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29750915,"author":"alpineidyll3","month":"2021-12","story":"The Case Against Crypto","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned as an example of airline loyalty points.","quote":"","text":"There are casinos in every major place I've lived. (La, Austin, Chicago) which are negative sum games (not zero like crypto) Mileage: [VIDEO] There are, absolutely, crypto Ponzi schemes. (And stablecoins are largely such things) However there are also valid uses of crypto/Defi. I've used them myself to send funds easily. They are enabling truly symmetrically fair exchanges etc. If you don't like crypto, don't buy it, don't mine it. Following eth 2.0 the energy usage will be minimal and it won't be harming you. Preserving positive freedoms generally and avoiding negative freedoms (constraints on others) is a good thing imo."}],"r":[["sGG-A80Tl5g",1],["7nJgHBbEgsE",1],["obmt_PkIfBE",1],["mbEfzuCLoAQ",1],["3LVeEjsn8Ts",1]]},"pHsuIaPbNbY":{"wy":"HN people return to it as a clear primer on MCMC and HMC and on why high dimensions behave counterintuitively.","q":{"x":"it was a fantastic primer on the basic concepts and motivations of mcmc, and later hmc","i":33338339,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33338339,"author":"leecarraher","month":"2022-10","story":"Markov chain Monte Carlo without the bullshit (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic primer on MCMC concepts and motivations, leading into HMC","quote":"it was a fantastic primer on the basic concepts and motivations of mcmc, and later hmc","text":"Recently watched this on Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, and it was a fantastic primer on the basic concepts and motivations of mcmc, and later hmc [VIDEO]"},{"id":40291311,"author":"euW3EeBe","month":"2024-05","story":"Gradient descent visualization","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk covering problems sampling high-dimensional distributions with HMC","quote":"This is a fairly long talk about HMC, but it does talk about some problems that come up when sampling high-dimensional distributions","text":"Not an expert in this field, but I'm guessing this is related to unintuitive nature of geometry in high-dimensional spaces. One rough example I can think of is the fact that the number of ways to move away from the origin increases exponentially (or even faster?) as the dimensionality goes up. There is _way_ more volume away from the origin than near it, I've seen this explained as something like \"most of the volume of a high-dimensional orange is in the peel\". One result of this is the fact that samples of a standard gaussian end up forming a \"shell\" as opposed to a \"ball\" that you would expect (this phenomenon is called the concentration of measure in general). Also, very roughly, high-dimensional objects have lots of corners, and these corners are also very sharp. I would guess that gradient descent would get stuck in these corners and have a hard time getting out. Some links related to this: - Spikey Spheres: [LINK] - Thinking outside the 10-dimensional box - 3blue1brown: [LINK] - This is a fairly long talk about HMC, but it does talk about some problems that come up when sampling high-dimensional distributions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15677070,"author":"srs70187","month":"2017-11","story":"Gaussian Distributions Are Soap Bubbles","stance":"rec","why":"Betancourt talk on high-dimensional distributions and Hamiltonian Monte Carlo","quote":"I really like and often come back to this talk by Michael Betancourt where the theme is quite similar","text":"This is an interesting take and kudos to the author for relaying a helpful way to think about high dimensional distributions. I really like and often come back to this talk by Michael Betancourt where the theme is quite similar: [VIDEO]"}]},"ztspvPYybIY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a classic dev tool pitch with palpable energy, and cites it for why Node was created.","q":{"x":"There is a sort of palpable energy in (Ryan Dahl) node.js original presentation.","i":12638469,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12638469,"author":"samblr","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Original Node.js presentation with palpable energy.","quote":"There is a sort of palpable energy in (Ryan Dahl) node.js original presentation.","text":"There is a sort of palpable energy in (Ryan Dahl) node.js original presentation. [VIDEO] edit: +Ryan Dahl"},{"id":31782511,"author":"swyx","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"rec","why":"Node pitch listed among best dev tool pitches.","quote":"","text":"i also asked this on twitter so just putting some replies here: - Doug Engelbart [LINK] - Twilio [LINK] - Stripe - \"7 lines of code\" - Netlify - [LINK] - Heroku - [LINK] and git push heroku master - Cloudflare - [LINK] - Node ( [VIDEO] ) and Deno ( [LINK] ) - Firebase (reportedly) - [LINK] smaller companies/less impactful pitches that i still like - Redux [LINK] - Stackblitz [LINK] - Comm [LINK] - Mongodb [LINK] - Let's Encrypt [LINK] - Figwheel-Clojurescript [LINK] - Svelte [LINK]"},{"id":18221351,"author":"knbknb","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Ryan Dahl's 2009 original Node.js presentation.","quote":"I like the talk by Ryan Dahl, node.js inventor Original Node JS Presentation, 2009,","text":"I like the talk by Ryan Dahl, node.js inventor Original Node JS Presentation, 2009, [VIDEO] In 2018 he gave a follow-up presentation \"10 things I regret about node\", but that presentation is more for experts."},{"id":37122972,"author":"danShumway","month":"2023-08","story":"JavaScript Is Weird","stance":"reference","why":"Talk focuses on I/O, mentioning JavaScript late.","quote":"What's interesting about this talk is that Ryan doesn't even mention Javascript at all","text":"Edit: here's the actual presentation, not just the slides: [VIDEO] What's interesting about this talk is that Ryan doesn't even mention Javascript at all (other than briefly to say that NodeJS is built on it) until nearly 13 minutes into the presentation. And it's clear that he's using Javascript as a means to an end -- he's going with Javascript largely just because Javascript by default has a single threaded event loop that every JS programmer had to learn to work with it. So he brings up going with JS because JS programmers are used to callbacks already -- and then roughly 2 minutes later he immediately moves back to talking about I/O and request handling again. Very little of his presentation is about Javascript beyond an aside of \"JS already does this and JS programmers are used to it, so we might as well go with that and try to copy JS conventions.\" He almost spends more time talking about common SQL libraries than he spends talking about JS."},{"id":26799725,"author":"jorisd","month":"2021-04","story":"Deno 1.9","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why Node was made: I/O needs to be done differently.","quote":"The third slide of Ryan Dahl's 2009 JSConf.eu presentation","text":"The third slide of Ryan Dahl's 2009 JSConf.eu presentation ( [VIDEO] ) covers most of the reasons for why it was made: \"I/O needs to be done differently\". Evented I/O via event loops wasn't really that much of a thing back then, and many server-side web frameworks were simply sitting idle whilst waiting on I/O. Node changed this, and thereby enabled a kind of concurrency that was easy to achieve and there by default, often without the programmer really realizing it, because they didn't have to do anything too special to get it done other than write JavaScript with callbacks. I don't think JavaScript was really the point of it. (EDIT: but JavaScript having functions as a first-class citizen, and closures, makes it a very good candidate for something that leverages event loops for this kind of thing)"},{"id":24474816,"author":"e12e","month":"2020-09","story":"Deno 1.4","stance":"reference","why":"Copy of the original Node.js presentation video.","quote":"I believe this is a copy of the video","text":"See also - post on original nodejs presentation: [LINK] (was a surprisingly large number of dead links on hn regarding this one). I believe this is a copy of the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11188482,"author":"alexc05","month":"2016-02","story":"A love letter to jQuery","stance":"reference","why":"Original Node.js talk, where Dahl does not use the term async.","quote":"I'm re-watching this","text":"Ahh.... lovely, thank you. So what I'm referring to as \"asynchronous javascript\" is technically a colloquialism used to describe the event-driven callback-y nature of the code. I'm not sure I'm still against referring to javascript as asynchronous or at least as having asynchronous methods for the purposes shorthand and quick communication. Consider the code (or paste it into a chrome console) function _done(){ console.log(arguments) } var db = window.openDatabase(\"dbfoo\", 1, \"test db\", 5e6) db.transaction(function(tx){ tx.executeSql('CREATE TABLE t1(a, b PRIMARY KEY);', _done, _done) }) console.log(\"i happen before the line above me\") Notice how the lines of code complete out of order. I think it's really useful to think of that as asynchronous code. It appears I'm using async as a term interchangeably with non-blocking... which may not be correct. So it may be the case that my nomenclature is off, but I'm sure I got that from somewhere. Perhaps a lot of people were referring to node as async in the early days, I'm re-watching this: [VIDEO] now, but it doesn't look like Ryan Dahl uses the term async. Anyways, looks like I'll have to train myself to use the right word for it. Thanks for pointing that out without making me feel dumb or being all insulting and internet-y. :) I really appreciate that. EDIT: Hmmm... additionally, to be fair: it appears that some agree that it is OK to refer to parts of javascript as async [LINK] so it might not be perfectly accurate, but it isn't"}],"r":[["xsSnOQynTHs",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1]]},"Ao9iOeK_jvU":{"wy":"HN chess improvers recommend Bartholomew's beginner playlist as a clear, approachable path to getting better.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend these youtube videos [1] by John Bartholomew.","i":18876296,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28789176,"author":"sb636","month":"2021-10","story":"Thoughts on chess improvement, after gaining 600 points in 6 months","stance":"rec","why":"Beginner chess video series.","quote":"Watch this youtube series","text":"1) Learn how the pieces move 2) Play along with some beginner chess tutorials on sites like chess.com or Lichess 3) Watch this youtube series [VIDEO]"},{"id":18876296,"author":"newen","month":"2019-01","story":"How I Finally Hit 2000 on Lichess and Improved My Rating","stance":"rec","why":"Beginner chess videos by John Bartholomew.","quote":"I'd recommend these youtube videos [1] by John Bartholomew.","text":"If you are just starting out, and you know the rules and played a few games, I'd recommend these youtube videos [1] by John Bartholomew. Youtube has a bunch of other good videos for beginners, e.g. chessnetwork, st louis chess club, etc. Also, I wouldn't bother learning strategy until you get to like >1500 rating or so. If you get really good at tactics, you can get to 2000 without having much strategic understanding. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15102655,"author":"hellbanner","month":"2017-08","story":"Mental processes of chess masters revealed how people become experts (2016) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Chess lesson playlist by an International Master.","quote":"I recommend this playlist by International Master John Bartholomew","text":"If you want to improve this chess, I recommend this playlist by International Master John Bartholomew: [VIDEO] I recommend [LINK] over chess.com for a far superior UI."}],"r":[["XVv1GipR5yU",1]]},"BKP1I7IocYU":{"wy":"Recommended as a thoughtful, research-heavy dive into game culture and the social pressure of competitive play.","q":{"x":"is worth the watch","i":42591017,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42591017,"author":"nozzlegear","month":"2025-01","story":"OnlyFangs has made 'World of Warcraft' into Twitch's best soap opera","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how WoW raiding shifted from relaxed fun to hyper-competitive.","quote":"is worth the watch","text":"You've had several replies talking about how Molten Core will be cleared easily by modern WoW players, and as someone who plays retail WoW (and has been playing continuously since Burning Crusade) I'll vouch for that take. Molten Core's mechanics are trivially easy compared to the things retail players are up against even in simple dungeons. That said, the type of player who plays WoW now has also changed since the original raiders entered Molten Core so many years ago. People still raid for fun, but like many things in modern society¹, you're also there to raid \"efficiently.\" This documentary that I watched recently called \"Why it's rude to suck at World of Warcraft\" is worth the watch. It talks about how raiding has changed from a fun thing that people used to do in vanilla WoW, where a guild of 40 people would put up with the weird gnome in the guild who wouldn't wear boots, to a hyper competitive pseudo-esport where players stopped tolerating that gnome player over a decade ago. [VIDEO] ¹ trying not to sound too preachy, I personally love the \"get good and keep improving yourself\" aspect about WoW, and it's why I push in the game's rated arena brackets each season."},{"id":38031838,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2023-10","story":"Oxide: The Cloud Computer","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"here's one I enjoyed quite a bit recently","text":"I am unsure why you are curious about documentary recommendations from me, haha, but here's one I enjoyed quite a bit recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37648212,"author":"a13o","month":"2023-09","story":"Minecraft Wiki has forked from Fandom","stance":"rec","why":"Citizen science dive into wikis as required reading in WoW.","quote":"You might enjoy this citizen science dive into the relationship between WoW and wikis","text":"You might enjoy this citizen science dive into the relationship between WoW and wikis, titled \"Why It's Rude to Suck at Warcraft\". One of the premises is that wikis are required reading; because if you aren't min/maxing, you're breaking the social contract with your guildmates. It's a totally emergent and novel way for a game to become 'unplayable'! [VIDEO]"},{"id":34915518,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2023-02","story":"Valve bans 40k accounts after laying a trap for cheaters in Dota 2","stance":"reference","why":"Explores why playing poorly in WoW is seen as rude.","quote":"See also this video about why it's considered rude to suck at WoW","text":"One of the things that drove me nuts about Overwatch 1 was the common demand from other players that you employ the pro-league meta when playing even at the lowest levels. A lot of players seem to imagine themselves as the next Ninja (or the Overwatch equivalent) in training and playing multiplayer in that environment is not much fun. See also this video about why it's considered rude to suck at WoW: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34919122,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2023-02","story":"Valve permabans 40,000+ dota2 accounts using honeypot patch","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I remember this... \"Why It's Rude to Suck at Warcraft\" and I feel like a lot of people want to be good so they cheat... they want to not be seen as bad, or a drag on their team. But yeah... DotA, and League of Legends... some of the most toxic games out there. No community to speak of, just a bunch of sweaty try-hards who probably don't get out around humans enough since they're too busy playing these games. It's rough. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SKtQgFBRUvQ",1],["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["UGttmR2DTY8",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1]]},"BTHvs3V8DBA":{"wy":"Praised as solid, research-backed advice on front-end web performance.","q":{"x":"Everything they produce about the subject (and I don't just mean the plugin) is solid gold.","i":724417,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":724417,"author":"patio11","month":"2009-07","story":"Latency is Everywhere and it Costs You Sales - How to Crush it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on high performance web sites and YSlow practical rules","quote":"Everything they produce about the subject (and I don't just mean the plugin) is solid gold.","text":"Watch the YSlow guys and be enlightened. Everything they produce about the subject (and I don't just mean the plugin) is solid gold. They are easily the best \"Follow these simple directions and you will make money\" technical material I've ever seen. video of talk: [VIDEO] slides of another: [LINK]"},{"id":466076,"author":"teej","month":"2009-02","story":"Half a second delay caused a 20% drop in traffic","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on YSlow for reducing load and render time","quote":"It's awesome.","text":"[VIDEO] That link is a talk on YSlow, a tool you can use to lower download speed, render time, and perceived load time. It's awesome."},{"id":621087,"author":"nop","month":"2009-05","story":"Stanford Online: High Performance Web Sites [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Google talks covering the course's subject","quote":"Hes given two talks at google on the subject that the course covers","text":"Hes given two talks at google on the subject that the course covers: [VIDEO] and [LINK] Silverlight or not I think most of will live without all the secret knowledge contained in those videos unless we want to fork out a $600 tuition fee to see more than three of them according to: [LINK]"}],"r":[["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["cfwwHa-7Ux8",1],["mbEfzuCLoAQ",1]]},"BiKfWdXXfIs":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a prominent physicist's skeptical counter-view, usually when someone asks for dissenting opinions.","q":{"x":"He explains his position quite coherently","i":22911269,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22911269,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2020-04","story":"Remembering Freeman Dyson","stance":"rec","why":"Dyson coherently explains his view on anthropogenic warming.","quote":"He explains his position quite coherently","text":"He was an expert as much as anyone. From his wiki: \"Around 1979 Dyson worked with the Institute for Energy Analysis on climate studies. This group, under Alvin Weinberg's direction, pioneered multidisciplinary climate studies, including a strong biology group. Also during the 1970s, Dyson worked on climate studies conducted by the JASON defense advisory group.[26]\" He held the view that CO2 was mostly beneficial to the biosphere and that anthropogenic warming was much milder than has been estimated. You can watch his interview on the subject of you're curious. He explains his position quite coherently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22448876,"author":"briantakita","month":"2020-02","story":"Freeman Dyson Has Died","stance":"rec","why":"Dyson on climate models oversimplifying complex nonlinear systems.","quote":"it's an interesting take on how models of complex nonlinear systems often oversimplify reality with assumptions.","text":"Freeman Dyson did speak out against climate alarmism. He provides some insightful counterpoints to the prevailing APW narrative, particularly about taking models on faith & the provisional nature of science. Given he was directly involved with some of the initial climate models, it's an interesting take on how models of complex nonlinear systems often oversimplify reality with assumptions. It seems like good general wisdom re: models in any domain exhibiting similar complexity & nonlinearity. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16957409,"author":"Radim","month":"2018-04","story":"'We're doomed': Mayer Hillman on the climate reality no one else will mention","stance":"rec","why":"Physicist's counter viewpoint on global warming.","quote":"A fascinating talk, and a counter viewpoint from a top physicist","text":"A fascinating talk, and a counter viewpoint from a top physicist: \"Freeman Dyson on the Global Warming Hysteria\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22976874,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2020-04","story":"Noam Chomsky: A Green New Deal Can Create Jobs and Livelihoods","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as an expert skeptical of climate consensus.","quote":"","text":"Number one they're not all in agreement. Number two, outright, science does not work on consensus. The only reason climate relies on consensus is because they cannot validate their models. They are stuck on the hypothesis step of the scientific method. It doesn't matter how many people you get to agree with your hypothesis. Without empirical experimentation, it proves nothing. You cannot beat chaos theory. The current crop of climate scientists are not being honest about the extremely exponentially high levels of uncertainty in their forecasts. You would do well to read from first principles on chaos theory and ask yourself whether I'm the crazy one for claiming that the future of our climate is unknowable. We understand weather far better than we understand climate. And we cannot predict it more than 10 days into the future. At any rate of you want to hear it from true experts here are a few: Richard Lindzen, PhD Atmospheric Sciences, MIT [LINK] Patrick Moore, UBC [LINK] Henrik Svensmark, Danish Space Institute [LINK] Freeman Dyson, Cornell [VIDEO] NASA admitting they have no model for clouds: [LINK]"},{"id":22452790,"author":"briantakita","month":"2020-02","story":"Freeman Dyson takes on the climate establishment (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"2015 Dyson interview on models and solar activity.","quote":"Here's an interview of Freeman Dyson in 2015","text":"Here's an interview of Freeman Dyson in 2015 re: APW & faith in models of complex nonlinear systems. Also goes into solar activity (electric, magnetic, & ion ejections) as a driver of climate change. Note that CMIP6 includes particle forcing to its model. CMIP5 & earlier versions did not include particle forcing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21234600,"author":"whydoyoucare","month":"2019-10","story":"The Money Behind The Climate Denial Movement (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Dyson shares skepticism about settled climate science.","quote":"Freeman Dyson also seems to share this opinion, and I think it carries some weight","text":"To me science is never settled, and anyone who claims climate crisis is pretty much definite has an incentive that has other hidden angles than we are led to believe. This is not a denial position, but is largely characterised so. Nothing in science is black-and-white, there are infinite shades of gray in between. Having said that, Freeman Dyson also seems to share this opinion, and I think it carries some weight: [VIDEO] . Another article also says the same: [LINK] The later link also highlights an important aspect from IPCC's report: \"In climate research and modelling, we should recognize that we are dealing with a coupled non-linear chaotic system, and therefore that the long-term prediction of future climate states is not possible. (Chapter 14, Section 14.2.2.2.)]\" Whatever I know about modeling and Lorenz's theories, I do think long-term climate predication is impossible given our current models. Is that denial? Certainly not, and I am open to change my mind as models become more accurate and more data is avaiable. Till then I would not want my wallet to be lighter in the name of carbon taxes, please."}],"r":[["9yPjoh9YJMk",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["hyry8mgXiTk",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1]]},"TQB0KK2rxcw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an informative talk on steam heating whose advice to respect old working systems carries over to software.","q":{"x":"When you see something that's old and it's been there for a long time and it's working, don't laugh at it.","i":19268923,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":26883426,"author":"anonu","month":"2021-04","story":"Heating Water with Fire (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Really informative talk on steam heating.","quote":"It was really informative","text":"Tangential topic : There was a post on HN a while back about steam heating. This talk on YouTube was linked. It was really informative: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25420002,"author":"oftenwrong","month":"2020-12","story":"Insulation: First the body, then the home (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk by steam heating expert Dan Holohan.","quote":"It is interesting on a number of levels, and I recommend giving it a watch","text":"As I suspected before clicking, that article cites that explanation as being given by steam heating system expert Dan Holohan. He has a talk up on YouTube that I came across a few years ago. It is interesting on a number of levels, and I recommend giving it a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19268923,"author":"userbinator","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: How to be productive with big existing code base","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk with advice to respect old working systems.","quote":"When you see something that's old and it's been there for a long time and it's working, don't laugh at it.","text":"\"When you see something that's old and it's been there for a long time and it's working, don't laugh at it.\" (From a not-really-related yet quite interesting talk I watched a while back: [VIDEO] --- also featured on HN at [LINK] )"},{"id":18444388,"author":"avhon1","month":"2018-11","story":"The Lost Art of Steam Heating (2017) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker discusses running steam at low temperatures, answering the vacuum question.","quote":"He actually talks about exactly this in the video!","text":"> I've sometimes wondered if it would be practical to operate a steam heating system at a partial vacuum so that the condensation/vaporization could occur at a lower temperature He actually talks about exactly this in the video! [VIDEO] \"You'll be sending steam up into the building at temperatures, in some cases, as low as 150 degrees\" (83 degrees C)"}],"r":[["MHTizZ_XcUM",1],["m64SWl9bfvk",1],["BemHqUqcpI8",1],["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["Y3n3c_8Nn2Y",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1]]},"goh2x_G0ct4":{"wy":"Posted in space-spending threads as a classic critique of priorities, usually with a quip.","q":{"x":"Only two minutes, worth a listen","i":27896306,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27896306,"author":"bloak","month":"2021-07","story":"Whitey on the Moon","stance":"rec","why":"Short two-minute spoken-word piece on space spending","quote":"Only two minutes, worth a listen","text":"Is this in some way related to Bezos and Musk's antics? There's a recording on YouTube. Only two minutes, worth a listen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25879579,"author":"lordlic","month":"2021-01","story":"One hull crack located in ISS, another one suspected","stance":"rec","why":"Spoken-word critique of space spending versus needs on Earth","quote":"This spoken-word work from 1970 conveys the sentiment well","text":"No, the argument is that there's plenty to learn, and it's all very interesting, but it doesn't stand up against people's very real needs here on Earth. I'm about the biggest fan of space you'll find, but even I, sadly, find the ethical calculus unassailable. Manned spaceflight won't accrue material benefits for ten generations or much more, and meanwhile human beings are dying of treatable or curable diseases, suffering under poverty, and living without access to clean water. This spoken-word work from 1970 conveys the sentiment well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17616239,"author":"rospaya","month":"2018-07","story":"Liquid water 'lake' revealed on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Song critiquing priorities, poverty and healthcare in the 60s","quote":"There's a great Gil Scott Heron song with the same theme.","text":"There's a great Gil Scott Heron song with the same theme. In his case it was poverty and healthcare among African Americans in the 60s, not cancer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47609313,"author":"scarecrowbob","month":"2026-04","story":"NASA Artemis II moon mission live launch broadcast","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Yeah, that's the take I have been looking for a spot to drop.","text":"Yeah, that's the take I have been looking for a spot to drop. [VIDEO] I believe that folks in the US are, by a large margin, the most highly propagandized group of people in history. It's hard to watch stuff like this. It's not that I don't understand that comparatively space exploration is small compared to the associated costs of the boots that might hit the ground today."},{"id":46184039,"author":"raw_anon_1111","month":"2025-12","story":"Ask HN: Is it just me or techno-optimism died in the past few years?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Not everyone was so optimistic.","text":"> IMHO, \"tech optimism\" reached its peak in 1969 when Armstrong and Aldrin landed on the Moon. Not everyone was so optimistic. You just didn’t hear about it on mainstream media [VIDEO]"},{"id":43972569,"author":"ngangaga","month":"2025-05","story":"Conspiracy theorists can be deprogrammed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"actual criticism of the moon landing is soberly connected to reality","text":"Well sure, if you're looking to be angry you can tie anything into your interests. But actual criticism of the moon landing is soberly connected to reality: [VIDEO] I'm just saying there's a reason why moon landing is such a funny topic to discuss—if you do deeply care about it enough to deviate from the accepted narrative, that's very odd."},{"id":42824721,"author":"drysine","month":"2025-01","story":"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via RL","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm sure Uyghurs in China would not give a damn about AI. Which reminded me of \"Whitey On the Moon\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41957748,"author":"throwaway19972","month":"2024-10","story":"Ion engines could take us to the solar gravitational lens in less than 13 years","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"remains as relevant as ever","text":"Previous generations also had it easy. Generally speaking technological advancement isn't blocked by motivation but by other concerns, namely funding. Personally, I'd rather fix healthcare if we're going to spend political capital. [VIDEO] remains as relevant as ever"},{"id":39778353,"author":"pyrale","month":"2024-03","story":"The Starship or the Canoe: Where will our future adaptations be? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> But those aren't caused by resources diverted to space exploration. Going to space IS politics. It is, essentially, a vanity goal for a whole society. The resources diverted are, essentially, mindshare and political. It is only fair then that people ask why we as a society look to space when we have so many problems here, just as it was fair to ask why so much attention was given to the race to the moon rather than, say, civil rights [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38027217,"author":"tivert","month":"2023-10","story":"Mars has a surprise layer of molten rock inside","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Apropos: Gil Scott-Heron - Whitey On the Moon (Official Audio)","text":"> A human being landing on Mars would probably be the single most watched event in human history. I'm actually skeptical about that. It certainly would be the \"single most watched event in human history\" among geeks interested in space, and probably among geeks in general and nationalists of the country that did the landing, but I suspect it's a biased projection to extend that attitude to all of humanity. Objectively, it's likely that stuff like the opening days of some war or the 9/11 attacks would be more watched than a Mars landing. Apropos: Gil Scott-Heron - Whitey On the Moon (Official Audio): [VIDEO]"},{"id":36712318,"author":"wnc3141","month":"2023-07","story":"The Many Planned Moon Landings of 2023","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Gill Scott Haron had this funny critique of Apollo program.","text":"I think while it's impressive to think that the moon landings achieved an incredibly bold and complicated task, it just shows what's possible if you siphon off a big chunk of GDP into big projects and an organization capable of execution. A lot of that wealth since has been distributed around the globe, or poured into industry, medicare or social security. With that global wealth distribution, incredible medical advances have been made, and we have blown past the UN millennium goals. It's all a matter of priorities. Gill Scott Haron had this funny critique of Apollo program. [VIDEO] With that said, not all priorities are chosen well in my opinion. The vast economic capacity required for a program like a moon landing have since been sequestered by shareholders benefiting from the returns of selling consumer data, building guided missiles, and seeking rents on privatized access to healthcare."},{"id":27909219,"author":"UncleMeat","month":"2021-07","story":"Bezos donates $100M each to CNN contributor Van Jones and chef Jose Andres","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"An alternative viewpoint","text":"An alternative viewpoint: [VIDEO] I'm not convinced that exploration is such a self-justifying good that we should look past the very real and very urgent problems that are aggravated precisely by the people \"leading the way\" here."},{"id":25317082,"author":"lumost","month":"2020-12","story":"Billionaires Build","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"These activities can best be summed up with this song inspired by the 1969 landings.","text":"Is Charles Koch? is Jim Walton? The list of billionaires is expansive at this point in time. While there are some like Elon Musk who are directing the economy in ways that would be perceived to some like myself as positive. For the ~262 million people in the US who would be unable to afford a Tesla, will never go to the Moon or Mars, live paycheck to paycheck and may lack the education ( or interest ) in space to appreciate the science and technology advancements being made, These activities can best be summed up with this song inspired by the 1969 landings. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20898175,"author":"fredfoobar","month":"2019-09","story":"India to Attempt Moon Landing at the Lunar South Pole Today","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm curious how white people reacted when this song dropped","text":"This is such a meme comment, some white dude on the internet thinks he knows better than everybody, what gives? I'm curious how white people reacted when this song dropped: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19874369,"author":"sbr464","month":"2019-05","story":"Blue Moon","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it’s a valid point. It’s also been a long-standing perspective; Whitey on the moon, from 1969","text":"I’m not downvoting you (maybe mentally), it’s a valid point. It’s also been a long-standing perspective; Whitey on the moon, from 1969: [VIDEO] And from the Atlantic: Moondoggle: The Forgotten Opposition to the Apollo Program [LINK] (I’m personally for moon and space missions, there will always be issues to overcome)"},{"id":19675153,"author":"rjf72","month":"2019-04","story":"The Notre Dame fire and the future of history","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It sometimes seems that democracy, somewhat paradoxically, trends towards division. The grand projects of times past were generally created by entities where their successor would generally be vested in their success. Be this in the form of religions organizations or non-democratic political entities such as monarchies. In democracy this kinship frequently does not exist between successive entities, and there is even often an effort to dismantle the works of a previous administration as well as blaming any faults on them. There are also basic democratic political problems. One very myopic, and very common, political argument is of the form: \"You want to [do something amazing] while there's [people dying of cancer] [kids starving to death in Africa] [etc] [etc]?!\" This argument feels reasonable to most people, and can be used to frame a candidate (who wants to do grand things) as being out of touch. And it's extremely effective. It makes it difficult to spend large amounts of money on things outside of those that can be justified on a very immediate level, such as war. The space program is probably the greatest project the modern United States has engaged in. And if it had carried on it's likely our species would now today inhabit bodies beyond just this planet. But it was not continued. This [1] is a song from the 70s that was published shortly before Nixon, who had a long standing animosity with JFK since losing a razor thin election to him in 1960, would completely gut the "},{"id":19296576,"author":"theothermkn","month":"2019-03","story":"SpaceX Crew Dragon Docks with International Space Station","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Do you ever wonder how people felt when they say Magellan or Captain Cook set off? Or, when they watched the Apollo missions happen? Yeah, I do wonder: [VIDEO] I think how they feel probably depends on their perspective, their financial situation, their susceptibility to public relations, and so on. Let's say everything goes \"great,\" and there are a million people living on Mars in 50 years. Will it matter to people dying in a war, or ailing next to an open sewer, or what-have-you back on Earth that there's some depressed Martians trapped in smelly cans and covered in perchlorate- and iron-laden dust 4 to 12 light minutes away? I don't think it will. I think that when many of us \"wonder\" or \"imagine\" what people felt like when they watched or participated in these events, what we're really doing is projecting the relief and and other pleasant emotions that we experience when we imagine them, when we use imagining them as an escape fantasy to cope with our own anxieties, to that imagined audience. Frank Borman actually went to the Moon, and his feelings about it, are definitely situated inside of his own perspective: [LINK] It's probably important for us to face this phenomenon square on, to check in with what's really going on with our \"dreams of space\" or sense of Solar \"manifest destiny.\" There's a reason that space-age propaganda seems so successful, both in its own time, where its object was removed from us only by the impassable gulf of 10 km/s, and now, when it is beh"},{"id":18800834,"author":"RA_Fisher","month":"2019-01","story":"New Horizons Reaches Ultima Thule","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Seems like a good time to reflect on the work","text":"Seems like a good time to reflect on the work: \"Whitey on the Moon\" [VIDEO] It'd be one thing if this was private money, instead it comes out of the pocketbooks of those suffering. That's rarely acknowledged."},{"id":16220094,"author":"rtpg","month":"2018-01","story":"The Google Lunar X Prize’s Race to the Moon Is Over. Nobody Won","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Whitey on the moon\"[0] has a ring of truth to it.","text":"I think the debate forces an acknowledgement that the objectives themselves don't always have intrinsic value. I agree there's a lot of things that can be gotten from it like NASA funding, but many people do not really \"know\" the utility of going to the moon. For example, people will talk about mining on the moon, but I've only ever seen articles debunking the utility of this, and not a single in-depth analysis in favor of this. I might be looking in the wrong spots. My theory is that relatively few people can justify the value of these efforts convincingly. This is part of why NASA doesn't have a bigger budget. \"Whitey on the moon\"[0] has a ring of truth to it. Developing strong arguments for further development is one of the most effective ways for people to support further development. Otherwise you end up with things like \"We'll go to Mars for space tourism/exploration!\". Which is... well you do what you want with your money, but that's a hell of a lot of money for tourism. Public funding will be hard to come by. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42430196,"author":"llm_trw","month":"2024-12","story":"Silicon Valley Tea Party a.k.a. the great 1998 Linux revolt take II (1999)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Exhibit A","text":"The past wasn't optimistic, we merely ignored negative Nancies because they had nothing to say. In the last 10 years for some reason we gave them the megaphone and let them dictate how everything is done. Exhibit A: [VIDEO]"}]},"HrVtA-ue-x0":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as an evidence-based counterweight to opinion in software engineering debates.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching Greg Wilson's Software Engineering's Greatest Hits[1].","i":32517952,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32517952,"author":"nestorD","month":"2022-08","story":"I have complicated feelings about TDD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Studies on TDD and other practices, such as short increments","quote":"I highly recommend watching Greg Wilson's Software Engineering's Greatest Hits[1].","text":"There are quantitative studies showing that TDD has little to no impact development time or code quality[0]. What has been found, however, is that writing code in short increments helps a lot (something that can be caused by using TDD). For more information on studies covering the topic (and much more), I highly recommend watching Greg Wilson's Software Engineering's Greatest Hits[1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27898507,"author":"dang","month":"2021-07","story":"Software engineering research is a train wreck","stance":"rec","why":"Worth its own HN submission as the source of the info","quote":"would make a good HN submission in its own right.","text":"That (or more likely [VIDEO] since that's where the info is) would make a good HN submission in its own right. Probably best to wait a few days to let the hivemind caches clear (followup/copycat posts aren't great: [LINK] ). If you submit it, let us know at hn@ycombinator.com so we can put it in the second-chance pool ( [LINK] , explained at [LINK] ), so it will get a random placement on HN's front page."},{"id":31294434,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2022-05","story":"I don't know how to build software","stance":"reference","why":"Updated broad presentation by Greg Wilson with similar information","quote":"Similar information is presented in this updated, broad presentation that Greg Wilson put up","text":"> Most of the critics of waterfall haven't actually worked in such a project. I wouldn't say that's true. > Most of the critics of either C or javascript haven't actually built anything noteworthy with it. That's oddly out-of-place gatekeeping. I've built large things in C 25 years ago that didn't get finished or weren't put in production. That's enough to learn about C, at the time . > Most of the critics of OOP don't have much practical experience with it I wouldn't say that's true. I read a collection of thoughts that feels like a combination of imposter syndrome (20 applications in 20 years? yeesh, that's sparse) and blanket characterization through arbitrary gatekeeping. I understand what they are getting at. This sort of critique about software development practice critique was better covered in \"What We Think We Know About Software Development and Why We Believe it’s True.\"[1] - [LINK] [1] About 18 months ~ 2yrs ago, all copies of this keynote were scrubbed from the internet as part of Greg Wilson capitalizing on his geek-fame with an Oreilly book and appearances. Recently, there has been an explosion of Greg Wilson videos on youtube. Not sure how long it will last. Similar information is presented in this updated, broad presentation that Greg Wilson put up: [VIDEO] and there's a PDF and a dry, stale version of the keynote presentation that I can't find again."},{"id":31379159,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How did you learn good program design?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that learning design patterns improves developers","quote":"There is some evidence that shows learning design patterns makes them developers better","text":"There is some evidence that shows learning design patterns makes them developers better, which is a very specific finding - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MQyxG4vTyZ8",1],["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["FJJTYQYB1JQ",1],["hMKy52Intac",1],["_ul7X5js1vE",1]]},"ZlfIVEy_YOA":{"wy":"Shared for its moving moment of scientific discovery, and later cited as a cautionary example after the BICEP2 result was retracted.","q":{"x":"They made a nice video of the researcher surprising Prof Linde with the news","i":7416137,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":11790716,"author":"marlag","month":"2016-05","story":"Crying","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deeply moving moment of scientific breakthrough.","quote":"I've seen it thirty times and I cry (oh, the tears) every time.","text":"Sharing my cryiest ever video clip, when Stanford Professor Andrei Linde celebrates physics breakthrough: [1]. I've seen it thirty times and I cry (oh, the tears) every time. I also cried at the end of Real Steal. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7416137,"author":"DangerousPie","month":"2014-03","story":"Gravity waves from Big Bang detected","stance":"rec","why":"Researcher surprises Prof Linde with news of the detection.","quote":"They made a nice video of the researcher surprising Prof Linde with the news","text":"They made a nice video of the researcher surprising Prof Linde with the news: [VIDEO] The reaction of the couple is great!"},{"id":33972452,"author":"oldgradstudent","month":"2022-12","story":"DOE SEC. Granholm to Announce Major Fusion Energy Breakthrough 10:00 Am EST","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"touching video of one of the participants notifying the theorist","text":"NASA also publishes. Yet we still got this: [LINK] BICEP2 was a respected scientific collaboration, and it ended up like this: [LINK] The even took a touching video of one of the participants notifying the theorist that predicted the result about their achievement. [VIDEO] Just a two off the top of my mind."},{"id":16888956,"author":"tombone12","month":"2018-04","story":"Losing the Nobel Prize","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"maybe not the greatest show of caution ever","text":"I don't know how to estimate their reckless from the outside, but it is simple fact that however sensitive, they only had one waveband and so no real way of estimating the foreground contamination. I think it is also well established that the foreground estimation was made by looking closely at pictures from a presentation showing preliminary Planck skymaps of the dust emission, and then building a foreground model from that. They also put out this adorable video as part of the announcement, maybe not the greatest show of caution ever: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16888982,"author":"tombone12","month":"2018-04","story":"Losing the Nobel Prize","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"instead of being cautious they put out a video like this as part of the announcement","text":"It's not really mistakes that is the issue though, its more the fact that they got reeeeelly close to outright fabrication. They had a very shaky estimate of an important background (the foreground) and instead of being cautious they put out a video like this as part of the announcement: [VIDEO]"}]},"gLDYtH1RH-U":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a gripping and vertigo-inducing watch that put rooftop climbers on the map.","q":{"x":"An absolutely fantastic watch.","i":16280334,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16280334,"author":"huhtenberg","month":"2018-02","story":"A daredevil climber risking his life for breathtaking views","stance":"strong_rec","why":"The video that put these climbers on the map, with a great soundtrack","quote":"An absolutely fantastic watch.","text":"For those who managed to miss the \"Shanghai Tower\" video from 2014 - this is what basically put this guy and his friend on the radar. An absolutely fantastic watch. Incidentally, it also helped putting some much deserved light on N'to and Worakls, whose Trauma track was used for the soundtrack. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9229387,"author":"pimlottc","month":"2015-03","story":"Where the Real Skyscrapers Are (Hint: North Dakota)","stance":"rec","why":"Gripping POV of illegally climbing the Shanghai Tower under construction","quote":"this POV video of two Ukrainians illegally scaling the 650-meter Shanghai Tower while it was still under construction","text":"Another heartstopper is this POV video of two Ukrainians illegally scaling the 650-meter Shanghai Tower while it was still under construction, which gets really nuts when they start climbing the construction crane at the very top: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22890587,"author":"Thriptic","month":"2020-04","story":"GoPro cuts workforce, changes sales strategy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of GoPros working well for extreme climbing","quote":"Go Pros work perfectly for their use case","text":"I think it depends on what you are doing. If you are doing something intense enough, it works. Check out on the roofs, the guys who break into sky scraper construction sites and scale the buildings. Go Pros work perfectly for their use case: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Bm7L-2J52GU",1],["TPg4Vcr56F0",1],["qoyKe-HxmFk",1]]},"7PoajCqNKpg":{"wy":"Recommended as the best introduction to SICM and sicmutils and a showcase of what such symbolic systems can do.","q":{"x":"The Strange Loop talk [3] is a good showcase of the power and simplicity of these kind of systems.","i":26357900,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26357900,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2021-03","story":"Symbolics.jl: A Modern Computer Algebra System for a Modern Language","stance":"rec","why":"Showcases power and simplicity of SICM-style systems.","quote":"The Strange Loop talk [3] is a good showcase of the power and simplicity of these kind of systems.","text":"Computer Algebra is criminally underused - it has the potential to make math-heavy critical-path code a lot more clear, testable, observable and bug-free by design. I feel I need to mention \"Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics\" by Wisdom and Sussman, and the accompanying \"scmutils\" library, which first implemented in Scheme many of the same features, although this Julia library seems to be more complete. There's also a great one-to-one port to Clojure by Colin Smith [2] in case you want to use it on a more production-friendly environment. The Strange Loop talk [3] is a good showcase of the power and simplicity of these kind of systems. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19768294,"author":"ja-85","month":"2019-04","story":"Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to SICM and sicmutils.","quote":"This video is an introduction to SICM and sicmutils","text":"Thanks, this is a nice port! I worked through SICM using sicmutils as a backup. The MIT Scheme version sometimes \"locked up\" on my solutions to exercises and sicmutils did not (the Foucault pendulum problem comes to mind). This video is an introduction to SICM and sicmutils: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38550876,"author":"erichocean","month":"2023-12","story":"Why Is Jepsen Written in Clojure?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on SICM implementation in Clojure.","quote":"And a talk about it's implementation here","text":"> static typing helps everywhere I think \"Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics\" is a good example of the kind of software a dynamically-typed Lisp is superior at, beyond the usual suspects like SQL, R, Python, etc. Here's an example of SICM in Clojure: [LINK] And a talk about it's implementation here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["o4-YnLpLgtk",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1]]},"92vuuZt7wak":{"wy":"HN points people to it as a good plain-language explanation of net neutrality during the 2017 FCC fight.","q":{"x":"John Oliver found a good way to explain it","i":15750193,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15750193,"author":"shmerl","month":"2017-11","story":"FCC plan would give Internet providers power to choose the sites customers see","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of net neutrality from John Oliver","quote":"John Oliver found a good way to explain it","text":"John Oliver found a good way to explain it: [LINK] [VIDEO] The problem is that ISP shills that make these decisions don't care about public opposition. They aren't elected officials."},{"id":14290033,"author":"natejackdev","month":"2017-05","story":"Save the Internet Net Neutrality is under attack","stance":"rec","why":"Explains net neutrality and the FCC push to remove it","quote":"Here is a video to explain everything","text":"The FCC Chairmen wants to get rid of net neutrality the only thing keeping the internet open and free. He doesn't apparently have the same idea. Here is a video to explain everything: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15041677,"author":"vmarquet","month":"2017-08","story":"72% of Consumers Don't Know What Net Neutrality Is","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"see for example the two John Oliver talks about it","text":"I thought this was quite high, since the net neutrality issue had a lot of coverage in the media, see for example the two John Oliver talks about it: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14534511,"author":"kradem","month":"2017-06","story":"John Oliver is wrong about Net Neutrality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Mr. Oliver mentioned them before driving you to joyride","text":"Well that's quite a sublimal message as Mr. Oliver mentioned them before driving you to joyride: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14295886,"author":"takeda","month":"2017-05","story":"Net neutrality is in jeopardy again","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Looking what you write, I think you misunderstand what NN does. The monopolies you mentioned have very little to do with NN. Someone already gave example how Facebook replaced MySpace, similarly Google, replaced Yahoo, AltaVista etc, I suppose Amazon did not replace anything, but started e-commerce (and currently creating other markets, like cloud computing), but it is getting higher and higher competition. Apple and Microsoft existed before Internet and are just using their old position to leverage their existence, but the fact they face competition. > EDIT: another question :) If net-neutrality has absolutely nothing to do with the tech monopolies maintaining their power position then why do they all support it? [ [LINK] ] Simple, because it expands powers of existing monopolies making them relevant. Perfect example is Netflix. They providing service to stream movies. They pay also pay large amounts of money for Internet access and ability to send content to their users. On the other side users similarly are paying for Internet access to be able to receive the content. You would think it's double paying (and it kind of is), but it's normal, why users should subsidize Netflix costs or why Netflix should pay for users' access (since they are not only using Netflix) they just pay for their access to the network. But, regional ISPs (in this case Comcast) started having \"problems\" where packets from Netflix were dropped, because \"Netflix was sending too much data to them\", ignor"},{"id":14296481,"author":"tomokasa","month":"2017-05","story":"FCC’s comment system targeted by DDoS during filing period for net neutrality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Did someone happen to save the John Oliver original video?","text":"Did someone happen to save the John Oliver original video? It shows \"This video is not available\" for me. [VIDEO] EDIT: was blocked in Canada, thanks NegatioN"}],"r":[["XgFbqSYdNK4",1],["W6aKQ-eBFk0",1],["fpbOEoRrHyU",1]]},"XRAP3lBivYM":{"wy":"HN recommends Berger's talks as fascinating and cites Mesh as mind-blowing memory allocator work.","q":{"x":"the weird and wonderful work of Emery Berger et al with their “Mesh” malloc implementation, which blows my mind","i":36035793,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36035793,"author":"samwho","month":"2023-05","story":"A highly intuitive visual guide on how memory allocation works, step by step","stance":"rec","why":"Mesh malloc compacts memory in C++ programs","quote":"the weird and wonderful work of Emery Berger et al with their “Mesh” malloc implementation, which blows my mind","text":"You’d need cooperation between your malloc implementation and the application code. It’s possible, but tricky. If your malloc returned a pointer to a pointer, and promised to keep the first level pointer up to date, and was able to lock your application whenever it moved things around, it could work. Someone else already mentioned, but garbage collected languages do actually do this. Because they’re fully in control of memory (the language exposes no raw pointers), they’re able to create the layer of indirection you’ve suggested and move things around as they please. I know at minimum the JVM does this. The term to search for is “heap compaction.” There’s also the weird and wonderful work of Emery Berger et al with their “Mesh” malloc implementation, which blows my mind: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":21862044,"author":"divan","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Emery Berger's talks are fascinating","quote":"Also, rest of his talks are also fascinating","text":"\"Performance matters\" by Emery Berger on StrangeLoop'19 is a great talk - [LINK] Also, rest of his talks are also fascinating: - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"},{"id":29333432,"author":"atom3","month":"2021-11","story":"GC progress from JDK 8 to JDK 17","stance":"reference","why":"Mesh talk reports compaction overhead numbers","quote":"the overhead can be massive too (17% overhead measured on firefox, 50% on redis!)","text":"According to the cppcon talk about Mesh [1] (an allocator that implement compaction for C++ programs), the overhead can be massive too (17% overhead measured on firefox, 50% on redis!) [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["1tSqSMOyNFE",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1]]},"XSoOrlh5i1k":{"wy":"HN mentions it as an example of Lenny, the telemarketer time-waster, and of how a canned script can fool callers for a long time.","m":[{"id":36550475,"author":"swimfar","month":"2023-07","story":"ChatGPT is now a brilliant tool for winding up telemarketers and scammers","stance":"rec","why":"Prerecorded old man keeps asking questions and going off track","quote":"There's a funny series on YouTube","text":"Even completely prerecorded scripts can keep them on the phone for over 10 minutes. There's a funny series on YouTube, \"Lenny\", where they just play a recording of an old man who keeps asking questions and going off track: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14519381,"author":"swimfar","month":"2017-06","story":"'Scam baiters' get a kick out of conning the con artists","stance":"rec","why":"Slow rambling old man recording keeps telemarketers on the line","quote":"There are some amazingly long calls.","text":"There's a whole series of this where it's a recording of an old man who talks really slow, can't hear well, needs things re-explained, rambles a bit, etc. There are some amazingly long calls. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38135024,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-11","story":"YouTuber Kitboga trapped 200 scammers in an Impossible Maze [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Lenny: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Also, I highly recommend the movie, \"Sorry to Bother You\": [LINK] SORRY TO BOTHER YOU | Official Trailer: [LINK] With David Cross playing Cash's \"white voice\"! The Art Of The White Voice by David Cross and Patton Oswalt (Sorry to Bother You): [LINK] Watch Lakeith Stanfield Use His ‘White Voice’ in ‘Sorry to Bother You’ | Anatomy of a Scene: [LINK]"},{"id":35650286,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2023-04","story":"Bark – GPT-style realistic multilingual text-to-speech generation","stance":"reference","why":"Fixed-script answering program fooled callers for long","quote":"it fooled some callers for a surprisingly long time.","text":"A few years a back someone had the genius idea of making a robo-answering phone program that would give vague but encouraging replies when it received some unsolicited sales pitch. Although it was a fixed sequence of responses, it fooled some callers for a surprisingly long time. [VIDEO] Someone needs to hook create the plumbing to capture speech to text, feed it to a GPT script that has been told how to reply to such call center calls, then send that back through a TTS generator like this one. To overcome any latency issues, it could build in a ploy to buy time like the old script did, eg, make the robo-answerer sound like a somewhat addled old man who has to think before each reply, perhaps prefixing responses with \"hmm, ahh, ...\" to buy time to generate the response."},{"id":35704112,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2023-04","story":"Serverless voice chat with Vicuna-13B","stance":"reference","why":"Canned vague replies fooled sales people for long","quote":"it fooled the sales people for surprisingly long","text":"I pitched this on a recently thread, but it was 12+ hours after it was posted, so I'll try again here. What I really want is a program to waste the time of phone calls making unsolicited sales pitches. It would do voice to text, run a simple language model to generate responses, then synthesize the voice back. It doesn't need to be a sophisticated model, not much more sophisticated than the classic \"Eliza\" program. A few years back someone did this with a canned loop of vague responses and it fooled the sales people for surprisingly long: [VIDEO] It seems like it could all run locally for low latency. Probably the most important part to get right would be a TTS system that isn't immediately pegged as a robot."},{"id":26126109,"author":"code-scope","month":"2021-02","story":"A fake company to play around with spammers and you can use it too","stance":"reference","why":"Lenny bot as basis for a proposed project","quote":"","text":"Someone can enhance lenny's spammer chat bot [VIDEO] to support: Multiple voices, and pass the phone between Lenny, his nurse, family members, etc. Create \"Spammer Turning award\" for the best Lenny spammer chat bot that can get caller stay the longest. That will be the ethical AI project I love to support."}],"r":[["vYA0f6R5KAI",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1]]},"fjbWr3ODbAo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short route to Dennett's view of consciousness as a bag of tricks, often in AI and cognition threads.","q":{"x":"I recommend the TED talk or the Google talk where he highlights this example as a way to gain insight into what consciousness may be.","i":24417941,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34812080,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-02","story":"Bing: “I will not harm you unless you harm me first”","stance":"rec","why":"Dennett on consciousness as a bag of tricks","quote":"Dan Dennett is informative here.","text":"One can equally say, \"Human brains only know that a neuron is activated by a pattern of axon firing in response to physical inputs from nerve endings.\" Does any of that change anything? Not really. >It doesn't understand credit card rewards Is this assertion based on anything but philosophical bias surrounding the word \"understand\"? >it understands how to compose an elementary word problem into algebra That's exactly how a human, who may or may not have understood rewards programs beforehand, would solve that word problem. Dan Dennett is informative here.[0] Yes, consciousness (even your own!) can just be a bag of tricks. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24417941,"author":"serf","month":"2020-09","story":"Sid Meier: “I don’t think I could make Civilisation today”","stance":"rec","why":"Dennett's painting example as a way to gain insight into consciousness","quote":"I recommend the TED talk or the Google talk where he highlights this example as a way to gain insight into what consciousness may be.","text":"it's not just that phenomenon. Daniel Dennett mentions an experience where a painting , with very little detail up-close, appeared to him to have all the detail of a photograph when viewed from afar[0]. It's not quite the same thing as the uncanny valley effect, for him it came about from his naive expectation of up-close detail being blown apart by his experience, when getting closer and closer to the painting, of realizing that the painting itself was almost incomprehensible up-close -- the detail was created by the way the abstract shapes created the illusion of detail from afar on a very large canvas. It was sort of an artistic physical version of rendering LOD tricks. He expresses it much better than I do, I recommend the TED talk or the Google talk where he highlights this example as a way to gain insight into what consciousness may be. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26501117,"author":"js8","month":"2021-03","story":"Information Is Surprise (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Dennett discusses a painting that invites the viewer to fill in detail","quote":"reminds of the painting that Dan Dennett loves to talk about","text":"This view of art, where the spectator is active participant and generalizes from incomplete information, reminds of the painting that Dan Dennett loves to talk about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23595887,"author":"MaxBarraclough","month":"2020-06","story":"“You want to know something about how bullshit insane our brains are?” (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Dennett on how consciousness is not as it appears","quote":"Here's his TED talk","text":"This is something the philosopher Daniel Dennett has written about at length. The nature of our consciousness is not as it appears. (Here's his TED talk [0].) As you say, blinking rarely makes its way into conscious thought, despite that the visual input was briefly suspended. (I suppose a similar thing happens with smell. How often do you notice that you don't smell much when you breathe out?) Neither do we notice that our various senses have different latencies, reporting the same event at different times. As I understand it, the blind spot in our vision was only discovered relatively recently. For countless millennia, mankind wasn't even aware of it, and not for lack of curiosity. I suspect the same goes for our colour perception. Senses can even blur together - the input from, say, your sight, can affect how you perceive taste, or texture. As you say, this kind of 'smearing' is a brilliant feature of our minds, it's not an ugly hack at all. That we aren't able to intuit the precise nature of how we perceive things, is a small price to pay, both in evolutionary terms and in terms of the elegance of the system, to my mind. On a less substantive note: reading lengthy writeups on Twitter is singularly painful. I do wish people would make the leap to the blog-post format for this kind of thing. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QHPmRJIoc2k",1],["xKCuDxpccYM",1],["2xcZS7arcgk",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["2lisQQmWQkY",1],["x2H60ritjag",1]]},"vkYEYjintqU":{"wy":"Recommended as a beautiful, accessible animation of cellular energy production.","q":{"x":"a beautiful animation of \"Mitochondria: the cell's powerhouse","i":25912223,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33370522,"author":"thanatos519","month":"2022-10","story":"How food powers the body's metabolism","stance":"rec","why":"Narrator explains why we breathe oxygen.","quote":"An incredible story!","text":"An incredible story! Somewhere in [LINK] or [VIDEO] the narrator casually says \"... and this is why we breathe oxygen ...\""},{"id":25912223,"author":"navan","month":"2021-01","story":"Time-lapse of a single cell transforming into a salamander (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful animation of mitochondria as the cell's powerhouse.","quote":"a beautiful animation of \"Mitochondria: the cell's powerhouse","text":"This reminded me of a beautiful animation of \"Mitochondria: the cell's powerhouse\" which I recently watched. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31472546,"author":"candiodari","month":"2022-05","story":"Enzyme Electric Fields","stance":"reference","why":"Example of molecular dynamics simulation of enzyme interactions.","quote":"You want molecular dynamics. They are simulations, not real images.","text":"You want molecular dynamics. They are simulations, not real images. But otoh (visible) light rays are \"about as big\" as a very large protein, so you can't image them. And because of computational expense they're slow motion by necessity, which is good because very, very complex enzyme interactions means ~2-3000 interactions per second. Example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1]]},"zBgCRJluWTc":{"wy":"Recommended in Ask HN threads on motivation and internet addiction as an accessible explanation of why screen time dulls drive.","q":{"x":"the video has a more scientific explanation of this phenomenon.","i":34326419,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34326419,"author":"sph","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: I'm 40 and feel my mental ability declining. Programming seems harder","stance":"rec","why":"Scientific explanation of how screen time dulls awareness and motivation.","quote":"the video has a more scientific explanation of this phenomenon.","text":"And technology in general. There is a video by HealthyGamerGG that explains how using smartphones or computers turns off a crucial part of the brain that knows what is important and you should focus on. The longer you're in front of a screen, the more you lose awareness of yourself and what you enjoy . Taking frequent breaks away from screens is important to stay motivated and restore that drive towards meaningful things. It's sounds wishy-washy how I explained it here, but the video has a more scientific explanation of this phenomenon. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34000326,"author":"faeyanpiraat","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: How to do that you need to do but not motivated at all?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why you feel unmotivated after binging videos.","quote":"watch these two vids","text":"If you are stuck in a loop of: I need to do X, but you just nope out of doing it, then feel guilty, and it is a recurring pattern then watch these two vids: - Psychiatrist Explains Why You Feel Tired All The Time: [LINK] - Why Don't You Want To Do Anything After Binging 4 Hours of Youtube Videos: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34719356,"author":"marmetio","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with information and internet addiction?","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned as relevant to internet addiction.","quote":"why you don't want to do anything after binging 4 hours of YouTube videos","text":"I was thinking of the same channel, but the video \"why you don't want to do anything after binging 4 hours of YouTube videos\". [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1]]},"2B-XwPjn9YY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the source for the talking-Macintosh introduction, usually in threads about speech synthesis and audio.","q":{"x":"The video of Steve Jobs introducing the Macintosh in 1984 never fails to put a smile on my face.","i":19265374,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19265374,"author":"mickel","month":"2019-02","story":"Show HN: tayl.app – Turn Websites into Podcasts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs introducing the talking Macintosh in 1984.","quote":"The video of Steve Jobs introducing the Macintosh in 1984 never fails to put a smile on my face.","text":"The video of Steve Jobs introducing the Macintosh in 1984 never fails to put a smile on my face. It was a groundbreaking feat of engineering in many ways, but what impress me the most is how the computer for the first time ever had a personality of its own. It spoke. [VIDEO] Computers, software and humans have come a long way since 1984. By standing on the shoulders of giants I managed to build a little something that comes bundled with several different personalities… It lets you select from 50+ voices in 17 languages. It detects the language of text automatically. It SOUNDS FANTASTIC - in fact, up to 54% more human than existing solutions. It makes web pages readable (a.k.a. un-suck) by removing slow & ugly junk. It is super fast, ultra reliable, and works 24/7! For the past 3 months I’ve spent early mornings, late evenings & weekends doing nothing but working on this project. It’s a good feeling to finally be able to exit my chamber, and have something to show for it. I hope you will check it out!"},{"id":28490874,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-09","story":"Thoughts on Clojure UI framework","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I’m calling it a pathological case because it’s forcing style recalculation and layout ten times per frame It's only pathological because the web makes it pathological. Phones routinely do complex layout animations that are unimaginable on the web. And yet, \"we're doing layout calculations on ten emojis, it's pathological\" is the norm on the web. > t’s decidedly rare to want to animate more than one or perhaps two such element at a time Yes. It's rare on the web . Because the web is unbelievably inefficient and slow when it comes to any animations beyond a very limited subset. > And alternatives to the web are normally not actually going to be better—the web has had an insane amount of effort put into making something not particularly amenable to good performance I have no idea where this verifiably false mindset comes from. Anyone you ask is \"yes, the web is very performant, you can't do better, the alternatives can't be better\". And yet you use apps, and frameworks, and approaches that are magnitudes faster, more performant, and create significantly more complex interactions and layouts than the web could ever hope to achieve. Yes. I'm talking about your phone. I'm talking about your desktop. Right now my iPhone screen with two notifications displays more animations, at a smoother framerate than your \"pathological example\". The supercomputers in our pockets and on our desks can output this: [LINK] And yet, \"it's rare to animate more than two things, other cases are pathol"},{"id":28528740,"author":"danaris","month":"2021-09","story":"Apple unveils new iPad mini with breakthrough performance in new design","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Jobs introducing Macintosh in 1984.","quote":"","text":"This has been the case since 2007 with \"iPhone\"...which mimicked the naming for \"iPod\" since 2001 [0]...which mimicked the naming of \"iMac\" since 1998 [1]. All of these, of course, go back to Steve Jobs' introduction of \"Macintosh\" in 1984 [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20450537,"author":"vel0city","month":"2019-07","story":"We can't get enough of audiobooks","stance":"reference","why":"Mac text-to-speech showcased at first reveal.","quote":"The Mac line has had text to speech functionality since the very beginning","text":"The Mac line has had text to speech functionality since the very beginning, even being somewhat showcased at the first reveal of the Macintosh by Steve Jobs. [VIDEO] Although, I imagine the quality of the reader has greatly improved since 1984."},{"id":3101992,"author":"teyc","month":"2011-10","story":"Daring Fireball: The iPhone 4S","stance":"reference","why":"Mac introduction began with a speech synthesizer telling a joke.","quote":"When he introduced the original Macintosh, it began with a speech synthesizer telling a joke.","text":"The humour is classic Jobs. When he introduced the original Macintosh, it began with a speech synthesizer telling a joke. [VIDEO] forward to 3:20"}]},"2o8MDCIlOEk":{"wy":"HN calls it a famous, hilarious teardown and a go-to example of terrible hardware quality.","q":{"x":"If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer","i":11475112,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11475112,"author":"yoodenvranx","month":"2016-04","story":"USB power supply puts 220 Mains power out on USB jack","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous teardown of a terrible tablet computer.","quote":"If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer","text":"Big Clive is _the_ channel for teardowns of everyday electronic stuff. I am actually not that interested in this kind of stuff but somehow I still watch most of his videos. If you are into more... entertaining teardowns you should have a look at AvE. Even if you don't like it you will still learn some useful canadian terms ;) (edit: just to make it clear, AvE is actually very knowledgable. His \"boltr\" teardown series of electronic power tools is most likely the most thorough you will find on the internet) My third goto channel for electronic stuff is EEVBlog who also does amazing stuff. If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11475444,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2016-04","story":"USB power supply puts 220 Mains power out on USB jack","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious, brilliant teardown of an awful tablet.","quote":"I'm crying out of laughter right now. This is absolutely brilliant.","text":"> If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer: [VIDEO] I'm crying out of laughter right now. This is absolutely brilliant. That is, the teardown - the tablet itself is an abomination. Thanks for posting this. This channel is great and seems to have a lot of educational content besides teardowns too."},{"id":11475556,"author":"yoodenvranx","month":"2016-04","story":"USB power supply puts 220 Mains power out on USB jack","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous teardown of the world's worst tablet.","quote":"If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer","text":"I just copy/paste a comment I made below: > My third goto channel for electronic stuff is EEVBlog who also does amazing stuff. If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11476783,"author":"stordoff","month":"2016-04","story":"USB power supply puts 220 Mains power out on USB jack","stance":"rec","why":"Reveals shoddy build quality of a tablet.","quote":"it's the sort of thing that wouldn't even be acceptable as a secondary school project, never mind a device to be sold.","text":"> My third goto channel for electronic stuff is EEVBlog who also does amazing stuff. If you are going to watch one teardown today then please watch his rather famous video about the worlds worst tablet computer: [VIDEO] Oh boy, that thing is a MESS. When he first opened it, I thought it looks like the sort of thing I'd knock together - not pretty, probably mildly unstable, but gets the job done. Then he took a closer look, and it's the sort of thing that wouldn't even be acceptable as a secondary school project, never mind a device to be sold."},{"id":14189862,"author":"mohn","month":"2017-04","story":"Why Juicero’s Press Is So Expensive","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of a terrible medical tablet PC.","quote":"he did seem to be appropriately outraged in the first one I watched (#822, about a terrible medical tablet PC)","text":"Thanks for the recommendation, I look forward to checking out MikesElectricStuff. I have seen a lot of EEVblog videos, but only a small fraction of his output, prolific guy. I definitely agree, too much grousing at times. Though he did seem to be appropriately outraged in the first one I watched (#822, about a terrible medical tablet PC): [VIDEO]"},{"id":12073597,"author":"antsar","month":"2016-07","story":"The axle count of trains in Switzerland must not be a multiple of 2^8","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Another field where you see this kind of planning is in medical electronics and software systems. Maybe sometimes... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["f8FAJXPBdOg",1],["ObvxPSQNMGc",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["jAhjPd4uNFY",1],["tMP5Pbx8I4s",1],["FV8n_E_6Tpc",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1],["1Nh_vxpycEA",1]]},"NVAmnV65_zw":{"wy":"HN cites Rossmann for documentation of Apple's repair barriers, and some commenters credit him with changing their view of Apple. Others criticize the design choices he describes.","q":{"x":"His work has definitely changed my views about this issue to be very anti-Apple","i":39318310,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39318310,"author":"Spivak","month":"2024-02","story":"Apple is lobbying against right to repair 6 months after supporting it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rossmann documents Apple's repair barriers while fixing devices live.","quote":"His work has definitely changed my views about this issue to be very anti-Apple","text":"You can't talk about right to repair with Apple products without the mentioning Louis Rossmann who has dedicated his life to shitting on Apple's horrible repair policies while simultaneously pushing through anyway and fixing them live. His work has definitely changed my views about this issue to be very anti-Apple because of how few options they actually give consumers when the majority of fixes are downright trivial. [VIDEO] In one of his videos they mention how they're able to sometimes source parts and it's by workers sneaking broken boards out of the factory and if that isn't a depressing state of repairs I don't know what else could be worse. The bar is so low here, a law that just prohibited Apple from seizing genuine parts from 3rd party repair shops who salvage them from broken phones/laptops would already be an improvement."},{"id":24150262,"author":"mellow2020","month":"2020-08","story":"Epic Games has filed legal papers in response to Apple [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann on Apple repair practices and planned obsolescence.","quote":"There is nothing to \"buy into\", it's all in the open.","text":"> The only people who buy the idea of planned obsolescence are the ones who haven’t worked on products like that. So, how many Apple products Apple said could not be repaired have you repaired? [VIDEO] There is nothing to \"buy into\", it's all in the open. There is something to be in denial about however, and you just offer an ad-hominem right out of the gate, plus the statement that phones failing when they cease to be \"supported\" is fine and normal; but somehow not planned, even predictable obsolescence. It's a shift from owning things that either break or last, to a constant churn, enforced by software where the hardware can't be made bad enough. Apple is hardly alone in this, but Apple is in it, for sure."},{"id":26954134,"author":"RicoElectrico","month":"2021-04","story":"Merge tag 'arm-Apple-M1-5.13' of Linux","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"stupid design on Apple part that compromises longevity and resilience","text":"The style of issues he has to deal with are stupid design on Apple part that compromises longevity and resilience. Like really retarded, easily avoidable crap. And sheer spite. e.g. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Lq-Y7crQo44",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"Wm2h0cbvsw8":{"wy":"Shared as a funny programming joke poking at the endless proliferation of web frameworks.","q":{"x":"Man, those Dylan Beattie's videos are out of this world!! I laughed way too hard on a number of them","i":25859832,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25859832,"author":"thunderbong","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone know any funny programming jokes?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious programming parody talks by Dylan Beattie.","quote":"Man, those Dylan Beattie's videos are out of this world!! I laughed way too hard on a number of them","text":"Man, those Dylan Beattie's videos are out of this world!! I laughed way too hard on a number of them - You give REST a bad name - [LINK] We're Gonna Build a Framework - [VIDEO] Enterprise Waterfall - [LINK]"},{"id":41367571,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-08","story":"Parochialism in time and space (2021)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you're aiming for the eternal, a message board full of computer geeks may not be the best place to find it. Lagniappe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16053399,"author":"ashelmire","month":"2018-01","story":"Show HN: Sapper.js – towards a better web app framework","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"This framework is 95% ideal, let's build another framework for my personal 5%.\" This attitude is why we have a hundred different relevant frameworks right now. How about instead of a building a new framework, you contribute to an existing one? You even say that Next is close to what you want... why not help to make it better? Relevant song about this issue: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MBRqu0YOH14",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1],["iGFqfTCL2fs",1]]},"x1SgmFa0r04":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a vivid illustration of how CO2 is distributed and how vegetation absorbs it seasonally.","q":{"x":"it was eye opening for me, and seemed obvious upon seeing that video","i":22065069,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22065069,"author":"tafurnace","month":"2020-01","story":"A global reforestation project is how we fix climate change","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Time-lapse CO2 heatmap showing forests scrubbing CO2 seasonally","quote":"it was eye opening for me, and seemed obvious upon seeing that video","text":"I personally believe that forests provide a net positive in terms of CO2 scrubbing regardless of decay/decomposition. The only evidence I'd offer is a time lapsed NASA video[0] showing CO2 emissions heatmap over time. The areas like the Amazon clear up the CO2 on a daily and seasonal basis. The areas where forests are impossible like the Arctic, it does not. The sun drives the cyclical \"heartbeat\" effect you see from day to day on the video, where plants using photosynthesis in the day (and macroscopically, the summer) effects the intensity of scrubbing moreso. Also, maybe it is my mind's desire to simplify of all of it, but it was eye opening for me, and seemed obvious upon seeing that video, that forests were the key player in air management globally, and industrialized nations were the main drivers behind CO2 production in the air globally. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33594943,"author":"photochemsyn","month":"2022-11","story":"A new website backed by Al Gore tracks big polluters by name","stance":"reference","why":"Model of the yearly CO2 cycle","quote":"here's a model of the yearly cycle","text":"This is mostly just political posturing on the part of the Democratic Party, which as far as I can tell is not any more interested in halting US fossil fuel production than the Republican party is. In fact, not a single country with large oil and gas reserves and an active export industry wants to see their revenue collapse due to something like an international ban on the trade in fossil fuels - and this is true regardless of their political ideology, i.e. capitalism vs socialism or authoritarian theocracy or whatever. Venezuela, the United States, Norway, Iran, Russia, Saudi & UAE, etc. all want to keep running their export programs for the next three decades at least. I mean, in the USA the Obama Administration oversaw the fracking boom for eight years and now that's the basis of the LNG exports to Europe. As far as tracking the big polluters, that's more misdirection. Yes, it'd be nice if oil refineries were run tightly enough that they had no local emissions, but those refined products are all going into the fuel tanks of jet planes, ships, trucks, power plants and personal vehicles, from whene they will be combusted to CO2 and injected into the atmosphere, so does it really matter where the emissions take place? This is not rocket science, just basic accounting. The end result is the same, here's a model of the yearly cycle: [VIDEO] If countries had really wanted to slow fossil-fueled global warming, they'd have started a 1% per year replacement program (renewables for "},{"id":27038124,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2021-05","story":"So you want to build a carbon capture company","stance":"reference","why":"NASA CO2 visualization","quote":"","text":"Trees alone might not be. However, photosynthesis is the answer. Plants exude sugars and carbohydrates, made from atmospheric CO2, into the soil feeding massive amounts of bacteria and fungi(carbon). The bulk of carbon in the soil comes from plant exudates, not plant matter. As long as this ecosystem is healthy the carbon stays in the soil. When the soil ecosystem is healthy plants are healthier which makes everything up the food chain healthier. The majority of our crop and pasture land soil is dead. The previous year's carbon that was pumped into the soil by plants is released when tilling happens in the spring. We need to stop tilling and grow diverse, more than 8, species wherever we can. Five tenants of soil health [LINK] NASA CO2 [VIDEO]"},{"id":20710903,"author":"godelski","month":"2019-08","story":"Decrement carbon: Stripe's negative emissions commitment","stance":"reference","why":"CO2 is unevenly distributed and pools in certain regions","quote":"","text":"To your sequestration comment. No one is saying don't plant trees. Fuck yeah, plant all the trees! But that won't wont be enough. Sequestration will get better, but one of the big things it helps with is locality. CO2 isn't homogenously spread across the globe. It also doesn't spread very well. Concentrations vary drastically over the year and so do plants' ability to capture carbon. Carbon can build up in certain regions. Worse, it can pool where we can't grow trees [0]. Places where we can't get enough water to introduce new populations of trees (without significant carbon costs, defeating the purpose. Also the local ecosystem) Cities. The Arctic. The ocean. I'm all for planting trees (even beyond CO2, I just feel better around trees) but people need to stop making the trees vs sequestration argument. It's trees + sequestration (air and ocean) + promoting corral development + more. (This same argument applies to a lot of things, especially in climate. It's not a \"this vs that\" argument, it's \"this PLUS that\". [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"5nW3nJhBHL0":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible explanation of why some simple shapes lack neat exact formulas.","q":{"x":"the info on calculating the actual value starts at 14 minutes (it's an infinite series).","i":33052753,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33052753,"author":"ars","month":"2022-10","story":"An old Encyclopaedia Britannica is a work to cherish","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to compute ellipse perimeter via infinite series.","quote":"the info on calculating the actual value starts at 14 minutes (it's an infinite series).","text":"See here for more on that: [VIDEO] the info on calculating the actual value starts at 14 minutes (it's an infinite series)."},{"id":36893664,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-07","story":"Breaking Superconductor News","stance":"reference","why":"Simple things can lack neat closed-form equations.","quote":"Something smaller than may help you grasp this idea","text":"Something smaller than may help you grasp this idea: [VIDEO] There are things we can characterize to any desired degree of accuracy but that e don't get cute little equations out of... and some of those things are so simple they've been staring you in the face since middle school and you just didn't ever notice their absence from your formula sheets. This is not the exact same situation being described but it's a similar thing. Being able to put a complex system into a computer and arbitrarily manipulate it still doesn't mean we can extract some simple explanation. On the other end of the scale, see all the AIs coming out. They're 100% computer artifacts with theoretically no mystery in them whatsoever... but they're just tables of billions of opaque numbers and doing anything with the numbers beyond just running them is amazingly difficult."},{"id":25379064,"author":"pas","month":"2020-12","story":"After centuries, a seemingly simple math problem gets an exact solution","stance":"reference","why":"Why the ellipse perimeter has no closed form.","quote":"an exact (closed form) is provably not possible.","text":"There are neat formulas, but those are just approximations, as an exact (closed form) is provably not possible. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ALc8CBYOfkw",1],["05WS0WN7zMQ",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1]]},"6yqfmXiZTlM":{"wy":"Recommended as Rick Houlihan's definitive talk on modeling relational-style data in DynamoDB.","q":{"x":"This is a great presentation about this","i":24434793,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24434392,"author":"andriosr","month":"2020-09","story":"Show HN: An open-source financial ledger API written in Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how good table design gives relational-style modeling on NoSQL.","quote":"The only requirement is a good table design","text":"I wrote a piece that touches a bit on the why: [LINK] But in a nutshell: availability and scalability. Yes you can achieve this with relational DBs, but it will be quite complex, while No-SQL gives it by design. All while ensuring all the goodness of relational data modeling, consistency and queries. The only requirement is a good table design: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24434793,"author":"andriosr","month":"2020-09","story":"Show HN: An open-source financial ledger API written in Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Explains modeling relations in DynamoDB tables.","quote":"This is a great presentation about this","text":"Interesting question. No-SQL doesn't mean non-relational. DynamoDB is a relational database. It's up to you on how you design the table to accommodate your relations. This is a great presentation about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30011525,"author":"belter","month":"2022-01","story":"DynamoDB 10 years later","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Rick Houlihan DynamoDB design talks.","quote":"You never heard of Rick Houlihan? He is the 90% of DynamoDB Evangelism...","text":"You never heard of Rick Houlihan? He is the 90% of DynamoDB Evangelism... At the same time you are able to this internal lookups? Do you work with DynamoDB? AWS re:Invent 2018: Amazon DynamoDB Deep Dive: Advanced Design Patterns for DynamoDB (DAT401) [LINK] AWS re:Invent 2019: [REPEAT 1] Amazon DynamoDB deep dive: Advanced design patterns (DAT403-R1) [VIDEO] AWS re:Invent 2020: Amazon DynamoDB advanced design patterns – Part 1 [LINK] AWS re:Invent 2020: Amazon DynamoDB advanced design patterns – Part 2 [LINK] AWS re:Invent 2021 - DynamoDB deep dive: Advanced design patterns [LINK] Amazon DynamoDB | Office Hours with Rick Houlihan: Breaking down the design process for NoSQL applications [LINK]"},{"id":22814523,"author":"abd12","month":"2020-04","story":"Rules for Data Modeling with DynamoDB","stance":"reference","why":"Rick Houlihan's DynamoDB design patterns talk, the most-watched re:Invent talk.","quote":"Rick has had the most-watched re:Invent talk the last three years.","text":"Rick has had the most-watched re:Invent talk the last three years. Here are the links for each: - 2019: [VIDEO] - 2018: [LINK] - 2017: [LINK] Additional DynamoDB links here: [LINK]"}]},"ET_b78GSBUs":{"wy":"HN cites it as an accessible explainer of prosecutorial abuse, especially piling on charges to force plea deals.","q":{"x":"this episode of last week tonight[1] is quite useful to understand the role and possible abuses of prosecutors","i":18031160,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18031160,"author":"BeniBoy","month":"2018-09","story":"New York inmate's golf drawings lead to exoneration in murder","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the role and possible abuses of prosecutors to foreigners","quote":"this episode of last week tonight[1] is quite useful to understand the role and possible abuses of prosecutors","text":"For foreigners (like me) this episode of last week tonight[1] is quite useful to understand the role and possible abuses of prosecutors. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19703505,"author":"awalton","month":"2019-04","story":"Security researcher MalwareTech pleads guilty","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver calls out prosecutors piling on charges to force plea deals","quote":"This is such a common tactic that even John Oliver had to call out the bullshit","text":"> That's why the state and federal governments maintain a system of talented-if-overworked public defenders. ...whose advice is usually \"take the deal,\" because they're incredibly overworked and would rather get things over with, and they've long been jaded by the system. It smells a lot like the prosecutors did what they always do with trial penalties - dump a deluge of charges and say \"look, we'll throw all of the rest of them away except one or two if you take the plea deal.\" Fighting the charges in these situations often requires a reckless disregard for money and time, even in the case when you're completely innocent, neither of which can be recouped even upon acquittal. This is such a common tactic that even John Oliver had to call out the bullshit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18048043,"author":"chrischen","month":"2018-09","story":"Google Suppresses Memo Revealing Plans to Closely Track Search Users in China","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on forced confessions in the US","quote":"Here's Last Week Tonight's recent episode focusing on forced confessions in the US","text":"You're absolutely right. We take people from other countries and put them in a facility on Cuba and deny them their rights. We treat our own citizens better, and enforce these same standards on China because we must Make America Great Again. Here's Last Week Tonight's recent episode focusing on forced confessions in the US: [VIDEO] HBO's miniseries \"The Night Of\" is a great look into the inner workings of the US justice system, and how it's designed to get people to take plea deals (aka forced confession): [LINK] The fact that you bring up these essentially sovereign nation internal issues as if they are fundamental transgressions against human rights (which maybe they are) while ignoring rights violations under your own nose, shows that your perspective on these issues is highly underdeveloped. China has many problems, and I'm sure forced confessions are probably a larger problem over there than in the US, but for the most part their citizens don't need armchair observers to fight for their rights. They don't need proselytizers, evangelists, or colonists either."},{"id":17736367,"author":"TomK32","month":"2018-08","story":"California Wildfires: Inmates Are Risking Their Lives for $2 a Day","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver segment arguing prosecutors need more electoral competition","quote":"john oliver had a segment on prosecutors and that they need more competition","text":"it's a democracy, run for office and change the system. just one small piece of it is who goes to prison in the first place. john oliver had a segment on prosecutors and that they need more competition [VIDEO]"}]},"LDW0QWie21s":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in event sourcing threads for Young's views on when to use it, including advice against cross-application event sourcing.","q":{"x":"Greg Young reduces the \"framework\" to a function, a pattern match & a left fold.","i":19075014,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":12631592,"author":"karmajunkie","month":"2016-10","story":"Why Are People into Event Sourcing?","stance":"rec","why":"Greg Young on event sourcing for occasionally connected clients.","quote":"See the presentation by Greg Young I linked to in another comment.","text":"See the presentation by Greg Young I linked to in another comment. One thing he talks about towards the end is the application for occasionally connected clients, which is one I'm looking to tackle myself in the next several months. ES may very well serve your needs, but I'd take a step back from the CRUD model and really think about the domain model. Something I do a lot of times when I'm looking at moving away from CRUD in an app is creating a bunch of domain-based command classes, which take over the persistence job, and move the app layer towards talking only to those commands. Still CRUD under the covers, but now there's an abstraction layer above it, and as you get a better delineation of boundaries you start to see where the services will fall out of it and what areas might benefit from ES the most. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46833044,"author":"austinthecoder","month":"2026-01","story":"My Workplace Disallows APIs","stance":"reference","why":"Greg Young advises against cross-app event sourcing.","quote":"Greg Young strongly advises against this in this video","text":"I'm familiar with event sourcing. From what I understand, it's useful within bounded context/domain/app. I.e., from the outside you'd never know an app is event sourced (it's an implementation detail). Event sourcing is fantastic. My concern is with cross-app event sourcing: N copies of data/events across N apps. Greg Young strongly advises against this in this video: [VIDEO] I have no idea how this came about. I think it's mainly driven by one engineer. I don't think it's related to any issues related to APIs in the past, in fact I'm not sure they've ever had APIs. There are numerous issues that have come up due to the nature of this system. These are mostly internal apps not used by that many people. Scale isn't an issue. I'm advocating for Events + API. With the idea that if you get an event that says \"Widget 123 updated\", you're free to hit the API to get whatever data you need."},{"id":19075014,"author":"fetbaffe","month":"2019-02","story":"Event Sourcing is Hard","stance":"reference","why":"Greg Young reduces the event sourcing framework to a fold.","quote":"Greg Young reduces the \"framework\" to a function, a pattern match & a left fold.","text":"It is worrying that a central figure to Event Sourcing & CQRS like Greg Young reduces the \"framework\" to a function, a pattern match & a left fold. Linked in the article [VIDEO]"},{"id":12630259,"author":"karmajunkie","month":"2016-10","story":"Why Are People into Event Sourcing?","stance":"reference","why":"Greg Young on where event sourcing applies.","quote":"","text":"> Moving everything to be an event completely throws this out the window. This is either an antipattern or unrelated to event-sourcing depending on how you read it. \"Event sourced\" means that state within a transaction boundary is built ONLY through events which are regarded as persistent and immutable; evented is the term I'll use for state which is built when events happen, when those events may or may not be recorded, and may happen before or after state is changed and are independent of the state change. If you meant \"evented\" then I would say that there are lots of message-based systems that aren't failing hard, and a lot that are, which says to me that there are patterns some of those systems are using to manage the nature of async, evented development that others aren't. If you mean \"event sourcing\" then the application of event-sourced data is neither an application architecture nor appropriate for all areas of your application[1,2]. If you were trying to apply event-sourcing in this way its not surprising you ran into problems with it. > Until then, my main advice is to make sure you have solved your system with a naive solution before you move on. It is important to really know and understand the problem domain you are applying ES to. Having a strategy to upgrade streams to new versions of your domain model is a good idea if you're applying ES to a business without a well-understood domain. However, it is very, very difficult to back into an ES implementation from a"}]},"QrkiJZKJfpY":{"wy":"HN suggests it as a vivid, hands-on way to understand circuits, including for a blind child learning to program.","q":{"x":"they're basically mechanical circuits and it looks super cool.","i":34229971,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34229971,"author":"gtm1260","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Alternatives to Scratch for a blind child?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Spintronics mechanical circuits, suggested for a blind child.","quote":"they're basically mechanical circuits and it looks super cool.","text":"I saw the code jumper tech at a conference once, it was actually really thoughtfully done, I had no idea it cost 1k though! One other option I've seen is this Spintronics platform: [LINK] I first saw it on Steve Mould's youtube channel, they're basically mechanical circuits and it looks super cool. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42548524,"author":"aziis98","month":"2024-12","story":"Against the Hydraulic Analogy","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on Spintronics mechanical circuits.","quote":"Mechanical circuits: electronics without electricity","text":"Related video by Steve Mould about \"Spintronics\" [1] Mechanical circuits: electronics without electricity [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":33822675,"author":"elil17","month":"2022-12","story":"Making a Traversable Wormhole with a Quantum Computer","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy: a model reproducing dynamics rather than the real thing.","quote":"A bit like this if I'm not mistaken","text":">By studying this quantum theory on the processor, we are able to... probe the dynamics of a quantum system equivalent to a wormhole in a model of gravity. Not a real wormhole, but rather a model of a wormhole which they say has the same dynamics. A bit like this if I'm not mistaken: [VIDEO]"}]},"b_T-eCToX1I":{"wy":"Cited in build-system and C++ compile-time threads as Apple's work on incremental builds and the design behind llbuild.","q":{"x":"has a presentation.","i":15743370,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15743370,"author":"Someone","month":"2017-11","story":"What's wrong with the default build tool for Scala","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on llbuild's flexible build system design.","quote":"has a presentation.","text":"[LINK] may be too low-level for what you envision, but captures a lot of your ideas, I think. [LINK] has a presentation. It focuses on compiling with clang, but the system is more flexible. Slides at [LINK] , matching video is at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24539498,"author":"chubot","month":"2020-09","story":"The Little Things: Speeding up C++ compilation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I remember watching the talk awhile ago","text":"Is this like llbuild? [VIDEO] [LINK] I remember watching the talk awhile ago, but haven't kept up with its progress. It's about using LLVM as a library to eliminate duplicate work, as far as I remember. I think it was for C++ as well as Swift."},{"id":13380585,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-01","story":"Announcing Remacs: Porting Emacs to Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Apple is also doing a similar work regarding incremental C and C++ builds","text":"If you are on Windows, VS 2015 and the upcoming VS 2016 have lots of improvements regarding compile times. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Apple is also doing a similar work regarding incremental C and C++ builds, based on the work they did for Swift [VIDEO] C++ builds can be made bearable if you don't go crazy with template meta-programming, do forward declarations, only expose actually public data on the headers, instantiate most use templates. But specially do use binary libraries, even across application modules. Don't spend endless time recompiling code that doesn't change."}],"r":[["oZAc5t2lkvo",1]]},"giAMt8Tj-84":{"wy":"Recommended as a first-hand intro from SQLite's creator that also teaches how a database engine is built.","q":{"x":"is actually a pretty good intro on to how to build your own DB engine.","i":12637685,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12637685,"author":"simscitizen","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Intro by SQLite's author that doubles as a guide to building a DB engine.","quote":"is actually a pretty good intro on to how to build your own DB engine.","text":"\"An Introduction to SQLite\" by Richard Hipp (who wrote the library) is actually a pretty good intro on to how to build your own DB engine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17823848,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Codebase at my work is a complete mess, what should I do?","stance":"reference","why":"SQLite cited as example of test coverage enabling big refactors.","quote":"They've talked about being able to refactor the core of their engine in a weekend","text":"First start writing automated tests, for example Selenium & unit tests. You'll learn what all the code does, you'll learn a new skill, you'll have a safety net of regression tests for rewriting the code, and you'll be adding value to the company. If you have good test coverage, you'll be able to rewrite the code very quickly. There are a ton of resources for how and why to do testing. One that stands out in my mind is SQLite. They've talked about being able to refactor the core of their engine in a weekend, only because they had the test coverage to know they did it right. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2407235,"author":"hendler","month":"2011-04","story":"IndexedDB has much to learn from SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"Google Tech Talk introducing SQLite from 2006.","quote":"Here's the Google Tech Talk on SQLite from 2006","text":"I've also worked with SQLite, and one project was the using the C API. It's also why I really liked Google Gears. Here's the Google Tech Talk on SQLite from 2006 [VIDEO]"}]},"hcRxFRgNpns":{"wy":"Cited as a first-hand source for how Google's leadership thought about hiring, organization and the Alphabet restructuring.","q":{"x":"if you're interested in some of the motivation and types of problems they're trying to solve with it","i":12278900,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12278900,"author":"simonpure","month":"2016-08","story":"Alphabet is still figuring out how to be a conglomerate","stance":"rec","why":"Schmidt explains motivation and problems behind Alphabet.","quote":"if you're interested in some of the motivation and types of problems they're trying to solve with it","text":"Eric shared some of his thoughts shortly after announcing Alphabet at Stanford's Technology-enabled Blitzscaling class if you're interested in some of the motivation and types of problems they're trying to solve with it [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39055343,"author":"fnikacevic","month":"2024-01","story":"Mourning Google","stance":"reference","why":"Schmidt on Google's top-down rigor versus perceived autonomy.","quote":"Eric Schmidt (Former CEO of Google) discusses it here","text":"The perception of autonomy at Google was somewhat marketing. In reality the company was extremely rigorous and top down. They used the perception to hire the creative genius types they wanted. Eric Schmidt (Former CEO of Google) discusses it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27611106,"author":"rkk3","month":"2021-06","story":"Is education no longer the ‘great equalizer’?","stance":"reference","why":"Schmidt on Google's early elite-credential hiring.","quote":"We also didn't want to hire anyone from what were considered lesser universities","text":"Google famously only hired people with elite education credentials in the beginning. \"We also didn't want to hire anyone from what were considered lesser universities\" Eric Schmidt [VIDEO]"},{"id":27103446,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2021-05","story":"Objective-S: architecture-oriented language based on Smalltalk and Objective-C","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is hard to encapsulate what is unique about a language in a pithy one-liner. This one is the best I could come up with so far. And yes, I am also annoyed by hyperbolic claims and titles, for example I remember a paper claiming a \"Language for Cloud Services\" (not sure whether they specifically mentioned AWS). Turned out to be a minimalistic FP language with rudimentary concurrency support. Applicability to cloud services was an obvious extension left to the reader. Grmph. > Is it really true? Yes, I believe it is true. It has taken me many years to figure this out, and I realise that it is highly non-obvious to the point of being startling and yes, seemingly over-the-top. The \"About\" explains it a little bit further: > By allowing general architectures, Objective-S is the first general purpose programming language. > What we currently call general purpose languages are actually domain specific languages for the domain of algorithms. Maybe this should also go on the home page? Anyway, a super short and also still pithy and incomplete justification: All current mainstream programming languages are descendants of ALGOL. The ALGOrithmic Language. And that's not just a naming thing. I talk about this in much more detail here: \"Can Programmers Escape the Gentle Tyranny of Call/Return?\" ( [LINK] ) In short, we started using procedures to both compute results, which was what we used computers for in general when we started this CS thing, and then incidentally also to organise thos"},{"id":27262116,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2021-05","story":"Why are there no return statements in Objective-S?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This implies there's something else that's not \"procedures/calls\" out there, i.e. subroutines. What else is there, can we name one of them? Subdividing a big problem into a set of smaller problems is not just some specific way of programming, it's not even a specific way of factoring a system. It's universal, as in the entire Universe essentially is factored this way. We have areas of \"widening\" cohesion and coupling (implementation) and areas of \"narrowing\" cohesion and coupling (interfaces, incl. argument/return types). These occur both horizontally and vertically as we move towards smaller and smaller problems to solve. Your actual CPU works this way, itself. So I'm very curious what we're talking about and why the dismissive attitude towards procedures i.e. \"cobble it together out of procedure calls\". In these conversations usually people just bring up something else that's also a procedure, but we call it something else, like \"message passing to object methods\". That's a useful metaphor at a higher level for sure. But let's not forget a method is just a procedure that runs and does things like any other procedure. ----------- Thanks for the great questions! I am particularly delighted by the slight incredulity expressed as to whether it's actually possible for there to be anything else. This has been my thesis for a while, that the call/return style has become so incredibly dominant that it is now largely synonymous with programming itself and thus something that is prog"},{"id":14885449,"author":"elmar","month":"2017-07","story":"SoundCloud's Collapse","stance":"reference","why":"Schmidt on Chrome and Android running inside Google.","quote":"","text":"I heard in a lecture of Blitzscaling with Eric Schmidt that google founders got the Google Chrome and Android projects running inside google without him knowing the real breath and intentions of the projects. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10983131,"author":"us0r","month":"2016-01","story":"Trying to interview Larry Page","stance":"reference","why":"Schmidt mentions no interviews since Playboy.","quote":"Eric Schmidt said since the Playboy interview they have not given any more.","text":"I was watching a video[0] the other day and Eric Schmidt said since the Playboy interview they have not given any more. [VIDEO]"}]},"izvdO-zdlKg":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concrete teardown-style explanation of how BYD builds a cheap EV and why Western automakers are worried.","q":{"x":"This video here describes why BYD is so competitive","i":46283223,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46283223,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2025-12","story":"Ford kills the All-Electric F-150","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why BYD is so competitive.","quote":"This video here describes why BYD is so competitive","text":"This video here describes why BYD is so competitive: They have done a splendid job vertically integrating as much as they can to get the price down. This $11,500 EV is an excellent example of how other companies should start to shift their thinking. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42745424,"author":"saturn8601","month":"2025-01","story":"Supreme Court upholds TikTok ban, but Trump might offer lifeline","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Seagull's cost efficiency and vertical integration.","quote":"The Seagull is a symphony of cost efficiency and vertical integration that western companies are now studying.","text":"Thats true...you can only go so far with that though and thats probably why many of their industries haven't really met the par yet. But at the same time they have eclipsed the west in certain industries such as commercial nuclear. That mentality is there in their industries that havent met the par yet and that was a major point I was making in my previous comment. As far as I understand, they originally licensed the AP1000 but expanded upon the design enough that they have ownership over the new design and they use that now. [1]: [LINK] We also see it in BYD. The Seagull is a symphony of cost efficiency and vertical integration that western companies are now studying. [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42223377,"author":"creativeSlumber","month":"2024-11","story":"Mercedes spends $8bn/year on R&D","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of the BYD Seagull.","quote":"Here's a video by Caresoft of the BYD Seagull which s apparently sub $12k.","text":"I don't know why the parent post is being down voted. I think it makes a solid argument about creating a car that normal people can afford. That is exactly what Chinese car companies is doing. Here's a video by Caresoft of the BYD Seagull which s apparently sub $12k. (Caresoft AFAIK do professional level car teardown reports for the auto industry) [VIDEO] I'd love to buy something like that at that price point."},{"id":39906300,"author":"duffyjp","month":"2024-04","story":"Tesla deliveries drop 8.5% from a year ago","stance":"reference","why":"BYD Seagull cheap EV example.","quote":"$11,500 EV with a 200+ mile range would destroy domestic sales","text":"I've been seeing a lot of buzz around the BYD Seagull. I'm doubtful Chinese cars will be allowed in the US market at all, since a $11,500 EV with a 200+ mile range would destroy domestic sales. If Tesla ever actually releases their long rumored Model 1 with it a sub $20,000 sticker price that would sell like crazy. Car prices are insane and not everybody wants or can afford a $40,000 car to commute to work and back. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39979664,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2024-04","story":"BYD is launching its next-gen Blade EV battery – more range and even lower cost","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for BYD Seagull features and price.","quote":"The BYD seagull has all this from what I understand?","text":">We don’t need a thousand features in a car. Power windows, touch screens, self driving and every other tech that adds to the cost can take a bike. The BYD seagull has all this from what I understand? [1]: [VIDEO] >I think it will be a good thing because then perhaps are home grown car manufacturers will see the writing and start offering affordable to the masses cars. With Tesla supposedly canceling Model 2, it seems like the incumbents cannot possibly offer this car at this price because of their cost structure (overhead due to unions, expensive real estate, regulations etc.)"},{"id":39998386,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2024-04","story":"Jack Ma steps out from the shadows with morale-boosting post","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fast Chinese product iteration.","quote":"You say that but then we see things like BYD introducing products like this.","text":"You say that but then we see things like BYD introducing products like this. [1]: [VIDEO] Unless you want to keep your head in the sand and pretend this is the soviet union all over again, you better start paying attention to how fast they are iterating. >China is now Soviet Union, but with horrendous demographics, mind-numbing debt, and wealth flooding out of the country. China is old and broke. China is fucked. Yeah this is the Peter Zeihan thesis but you know what, it might take a while for this to play out and in the meantime, China may not leave the arena until after they have taken out a large chunk of the western incumbents."}],"r":[["YAtLTLiqNwg",1],["1f6YyH62jFE",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1]]},"kopoLzvh5jY":{"wy":"HN uses it as a vivid example of emergent behavior from multi-agent reinforcement learning, and as a way to make AI misalignment concerns feel concrete.","q":{"x":"This short video makes the concern much more real","i":21198968,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21198968,"author":"aeternum","month":"2019-10","story":"Experts who say we shouldn't worry about superintelligent AI are wrong?","stance":"rec","why":"Short video making AI misalignment concerns feel real","quote":"This short video makes the concern much more real","text":"Specialized AI is unlikely to be a threat. The issue will be with general AI, and simple but not fully thought out utility functions. This short video makes the concern much more real: [VIDEO] Consider what would happen if you took that same AI, make its utility function similar to this: [LINK] , and released it on the internet."},{"id":40995849,"author":"HanClinto","month":"2024-07","story":"Prover-Verifier Games improve legibility of language model outputs","stance":"reference","why":"Example of reinforcement learning with a clear objective","quote":"","text":"True RL is not limited by being tethered to human-annotated data, and it is able to create novel approaches to solve problems. True RL requires a very clear objective function (such as the rules of Go, or Starcraft, or Taboo!) that the model can evaluate itself against. Andrej Karpathy talks about the difference between RLHF and \"true\" RL here: [LINK] > The other thing is that we're doing reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), but that's like a super weak form of reinforcement learning. I think... what is the equivalent in AlphaGo for RLHF? What is the reward model? What I call it is a \"vibe check\". Imagine if you wanted to train an AlphaGo RLHF, it would be giving two people two boards and asking: \"Which one do you prefer?\" -- and then you would take those labels and you would train the model and then you would RL against that. What are the issues with that? It's like, number one -- that's just vibes of the board. That's what you're training against. Number two, if it's a reward model that's a neural net, then it's very easy to overfit to that reward model for the model you're optimizing over, and it's going to find all these spurious ways of hacking that massive model is the problem. > AlphaGo gets around these problems because they have a very clear objective function, and you can RL against it. > So RLHF is nowhere near [true] RL -- it's silly. And the other thing is that imitation is super-silly. RLHF is a nice improvement, but it's still silly, and I think p"},{"id":32721039,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2022-09","story":"On Device Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Example of so-called emergent behavior in agents","quote":"They called it \"emergent\" property, but I only see that as inevitable behaviour.","text":"Here [1]. They called it \"emergent\" property, but I only see that as inevitable behaviour. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32101322,"author":"treesprite82","month":"2022-07","story":"Forget the hype, “thinking machines” can’t replace humans","stance":"reference","why":"Neural networks adapting and learning new strategies in virtual environments","quote":"","text":"> There's just something I can't describe with words about what it lacks today - where it's possibly more philosophical than technical My personal viewpoint is that there's nothing inherently special about biological intelligence that can't theoretically be reproduced with electronics, but that today's AI has some practical limitations like no significant persistent internal state. I saw an \"Inner Monologue\" paper by Google Robotics recently which could be a step in the right direction. > Humans for example operate within the universal set and within all dimensions we can percieve. Our brain's direct output is electrical signals to a set of muscles. I don't think there's anything in particular stopping a physical robot from having similar generality of operating space. > Even in todays state of the art if we gave robots all the sensors that we have they still couldn't evolve with their environment like we could Ability to evolve seems mostly orthogonal to intelligence to me. If humans instead came about by an intelligent creator or gradient descent, wouldn't we still be intelligent? AI adapting to the environment through gradient descent or self-modification should be fine and faster than evolution. > or invent fire like we would with current algorithm or neural nets. They would just keep doing whatever they're programmed to do within their set of \"stuff\" for lack of a better term In virtual environments, neural networks have adapted to changes and learned new strategies, eve"},{"id":30820348,"author":"HaukeHi","month":"2022-03","story":"It looks like you’re trying to take over the world","stance":"reference","why":"Example of agents evolving strategies via competition","quote":"OpenAI's Multi-Agent Hide and Seek","text":"I think evolution is not an undirected process in that sense because it's an optimization process, that optimizes to create more copies of itself. Superintelligence will likely use some Evolutionary Computation (see en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evolutionary_computation ). Also see Karl Sims 'Creatures' from the 90s: youtube.com/watch?v=JBgG_VSP7f8 or OpenAI's Multi-Agent Hide and Seek: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29602993,"author":"ShamelessC","month":"2021-12","story":"What Does It Mean for AI to Understand?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among models in discussion of pre-training","quote":"","text":"In statistics and machine learning this problem is called concept drift and it's an open research problem. Indeed all the models I listed are already out-of-date with the present state of the world; unless they are fine-tuned on new data. The regime for the above networks is called \"pre-training\". The idea here is that rather than training _directly_ on some challenging, specific task, you instead train a more generic task on a _lot_ of data. This gives you a \"backbone model\" that winds up being very strong on more specific tasks as well. In many cases, the ability to (cheaply) curate or create enough accurate data for specific sub-tasks might not even be possible. It's easier to scrape 400 million captioned images from the internet than it is to find/create millions of visual Q&A prompts/images. CLIP is a great example of this. While it was trained explicitly \"just\" to compare images and captions and to output a score of the cosine similarity between the features of the two - I have seen it approach effective state of the art on text-to-image generation tasks, image-captioning, Q&A, etc. This style of training needs to be updated after it is trained, typically because of the way these datasets are curated. Automatic curation via pretrained models is one option. Another option is to give the model direct access to the internet which is starting to be explored. Pre-training helps a lot with general distribution shift; but it's _probably_ not going to be able predict memes befo"},{"id":21784934,"author":"willhk","month":"2019-12","story":"Dota 2 with Large Scale Deep Reinforcement Learning [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"OpenAI's multi-agent play work","quote":"The work I've seen recently from them on multi-agent play","text":"The work I've seen recently from them on multi-agent play seems to indicate this is a problem they're very successfully working on. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["tWr39Q9vBgo",1],["ODSJsviD_SU",1]]},"pBTvmrRGlbE":{"wy":"Cited as the explanation for the oddly inaccurate tube staircase signs, and as an example of Marshall's station-detail videos.","q":{"x":"Geoff has a bunch of videos about the details of stations or ticket systems.","i":16464940,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16464940,"author":"DanBC","month":"2018-02","story":"Traveling to all the railway stations in Great Britain [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Geoff Marshall explains the 15-floors tube station sign oddity.","quote":"Geoff has a bunch of videos about the details of stations or ticket systems.","text":"This is a great series, and they manage to point out a lot of the oddities of the UK rail system. They also busted my myth that Gloucester station has the longest platform. (It's pretty flipping long though.) Geoff has a bunch of videos about the details of stations or ticket systems. All tube stations have 15 floors: [VIDEO] Heathrow to Upminster for £1.50: [LINK] Oyster Oddities, are you paying too much?: [LINK]"},{"id":35159453,"author":"waqf","month":"2023-03","story":"The Door Close Button","stance":"reference","why":"Tube stair signs say 15 floors irrespective of height.","quote":"irrespective of how high they actually are","text":"Meanwhile, on the London Underground, the lengths of all sets of stairs down to platform level are described as \"equivalent to 15 floors\" irrespective of how high they actually are: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17790267,"author":"scoot","month":"2018-08","story":"A Nerd’s Way to Walk Up the Stairs (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Tube signs claim stairs equal 15 floors regardless of step count.","quote":"there are signs in London tube stations claiming the stairs are equivalent to 15 floors, regardless of the number of steps","text":"Reminded me of this video [1] talking about the fact that there are signs in London tube stations claiming the stairs are equivalent to 15 floors, regardless of the number of steps. The number of floors is roughly correct for Covent Garden, so I guess it's possible that the original typesetter changed the number of steps, but forgot to change the number of floors. [1] [VIDEO] Incidental anecdote: While searching for this video, which I'd seen a year or so ago, I googled \"tube 15 floors sign\", but didn't see a reference to it, so I opened YouTube instead, and there, in recommended videos, was a video about the tube... by the same person that made the video above. It was a short hop to find the correct video. It had never occurred to me that Google personalize youtube searches based on your google searches."}],"r":[["w_znRopGtbE",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1],["snz7JJlSZvw",1]]},"rIAXIubSTkc":{"wy":"HN cites it as a neat, simple trick of relaying moves between opponents, often in discussions of chess, AI or ludic fallacies.","q":{"x":"I really liked the simplicity of the trick (concept wise).","i":32025129,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32025129,"author":"laumars","month":"2022-07","story":"Markov Chat Bot Disaster Story","stance":"rec","why":"Simple, clever chess trick by Derren Brown","quote":"I really liked the simplicity of the trick (concept wise).","text":"For anyone interested in the Chess story, it's a trick performed by Darren Brown: [VIDEO] I really liked the simplicity of the trick (concept wise). Though I sure as hell couldn't memorise all those moves."},{"id":23937416,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2020-07","story":"Ludic Fallacy","stance":"reference","why":"Derren Brown winning simultaneous chess games against grandmasters","quote":"This reminds me of Derren Brown (a magician) winning simultaneous chess games against grandmasters","text":"This reminds me of Derren Brown (a magician) winning simultaneous chess games against grandmasters... [VIDEO]"},{"id":20775349,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-08","story":"Can Chess Survive Artificial Intelligence?","stance":"reference","why":"Derren Brown beat chess players by relaying moves between opponents","quote":"the magician/mentalist Derren Brown challenged 9 English chess players, including 4 GMs, to a simul on his TV show","text":"It's a very old trick I'm sure. As for it never happening or fooling anyone in real life: the magician/mentalist Derren Brown challenged 9 English chess players, including 4 GMs, to a simul on his TV show, and got a plus score - he secretly had 8 of the players playing each other, with whom he thus had an even score. The 9th was president of a university chess club, and a very weak player, who Derren played and beat, winning the match. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ev3vENli7wQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1]]},"7Ooa7wOKHhg":{"wy":"HN cites it as a stark example in discussions of police use of force and accountability.","m":[{"id":37098855,"author":"lincon127","month":"2023-08","story":"Truth Social tipped off authorities on Utah man killed by the FBI","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Example: this classic","text":"Depends on context, obviously. The police officer has the responsibility to constantly assess the situation in order to determine if violence is warranted, as well as if that violence needs to be potentially lethal, regardless of whether or not a person is complying. As an office, one doesn't have the right to commit bodily harm, nevermind kill someone, without good cause. Hence why people talk about it so much, and also why people like to talk about police brutality even when it's regarding people that don't comply with officers (eg. protesters, bystanders, people filming, etc.) Example: this classic [VIDEO] . Shit like this happens in the States all the time, it's just not usually as bad, nor televised. But situations with power mad cops crop up all the time. Cops that forget that they are an arm of the law, rather than the law itself. I don't know how the case mentioned in the article holds up though as I'm not aware of details."},{"id":23416554,"author":"sudoaza","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: How can we prevent excessive force by police?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the officer wasn't punished instead now has a huge pension for his traumatic experience","text":"I'm talking about this case, the officer wasn't punished instead now has a huge pension for his traumatic experience [VIDEO]"},{"id":23116144,"author":"optymizer","month":"2020-05","story":"For cops who kill, special Supreme Court protection","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"What do you think happens when 'the police' shouts conflicting instructions? The answer is: you get shot","text":"You seem to have a very simplistic view of police, I almost envy you for it. None of what you're saying needs to be a reality. None of it is necessary. > Police are people and they can get scared, angry, and make mistakes. Everyone makes mistakes - that doesn't excuse anyone from paying for those mistakes. Some of these mistakes cost lives. Regular folks go to jail. The police in the US, in general, doesn't. Those who do are the exception. Usually - suspension, investigation, acquitted. > When a police officer instructs you to do something just do it. What do you think happens when 'the police' shouts conflicting instructions? The answer is: you get shot [0] What if the police instructs you to do something illegal? What if the police sexually assaults you? [1] > If you disagree with the order there is a mechanism to voice that disagreement called the court. So what you're saying is that if you disagree with a decision the police officer makes in a split second, you should spend the next several years of your life in the court system? When (not if) the police illegally seizes your property, it's OK for you to spend the next few years in the court system to get that property back to be able to return to normal life, right? [2] > they will use rapidly escalating force to make you comply that can endanger your life Why? Is that necessary? The police in other developed countries, for example the UK, is overall orders of magnitude more civilized and respectful than the US police. >"}]},"9IBPeRa7U8E":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of the Missouri 'view source' controversy and the governor's attempt to portray viewing HTML as hacking.","m":[{"id":29101193,"author":"threatofrain","month":"2021-11","story":"Reporter who told Missouri officials of website flaw did 'nothing out of line'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If anyone is curious what the political ad looks like.","text":"> The governor's political fundraising committee is running ads making this a \"fake news\" issue. [VIDEO] If anyone is curious what the political ad looks like."},{"id":31026374,"author":"reuben364","month":"2022-04","story":"iViewed your API keys","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this ad from Gov Parson in response to a disclosure about a state website leaking social security numbers of teachers","text":"See: this ad from Gov Parson in response to a disclosure about a state website leaking social security numbers of teachers [VIDEO]"},{"id":29750128,"author":"badRNG","month":"2021-12","story":"Reporter likely to be charged for using “view source” feature on web browser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thompson is a small-town Missourian elected prosecutor, facing pressure from fellow conservatives to \"hold the fake news accountable\" as stated in the latest attack ad against the reporter from a conservative PAC [1]. At best it's a coin toss imo. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29750213,"author":"badRNG","month":"2021-12","story":"Reporter likely to be charged for using “view source” feature on web browser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the Missouri conservative PAC has put their money behind this by placing attack ads against the reporter","text":"This definitely is not \"much ado about nothing.\" I prefer the Verge article on this one though [1]. Relevant quote from the article: > They turned the case over to Cole County Prosecuting Attorney Locke Thompson on Monday, December 27. Governor Parson then held a press conference on Wednesday, December 29, where he cited a state statute related to computer tampering and repeatedly suggested Thompson should use it to prosecute Renaud and the paper. It was just earlier this week that this was turned over to the prosecutor's office. This prosecutor is an elected official, and the Missouri conservative PAC has put their money behind this by placing attack ads against the reporter, and framing this as \"holding the fake news accountable\" [2]. Also, IANAL but if we're going by the letter of the law, the local Missouri computer crime section certainly does seem to include the actions of the reporter [3], even if the SSNs weren't Base64 encoded. Bad law, but the fact that the law is bad doesn't have bearing on whether they'll decide to prosecute. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":29750347,"author":"badRNG","month":"2021-12","story":"Reporter likely to be charged for using “view source” feature on web browser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"IANAL, but the local Missouri computer crime statute is very broad [1]. Technically, the reporter seems to have factually \"Accessed a computer, a computer system, or a computer network, and intentionally examined information about another person\" without \"authorization.\" Considering the conservative PAC for the state has already pushed attack ads against the reporter [2], and the fact that the prosecutor is elected (potentially supported by conservative PACs), I definitely see a possibility of this going to court and potentially arriving at a conviction. Again, IANAL, and I don't know the jurisprudence or case history behind this particular statute. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29751100,"author":"badRNG","month":"2021-12","story":"Reporter likely to be charged for using “view source” feature on web browser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":">This entire debacle is a direct attack on journalists. As if this attack on a free press wasn't brazen enough, local PACs have already put out attack ads against the journalist, to frame this as the governor \"holding fake news accountable.\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29099646,"author":"shadowgovt","month":"2021-11","story":"Reporter who told Missouri officials of website flaw did 'nothing out of line'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"His PAC is running ads like this.","text":"Regarding \"It's playing great with his constituents:\" His PAC is running ads like this. [VIDEO] To a lot of us, that looks like nonsense, but you have to remember how far through the looking-glass a lot of the conservative populace has gone... The press is now the enemy in their mind, and they don't understand technology well enough to get why these claims are ridiculous. So he gets to build steam and any pushback is just seen as more \"fake news\" coverage that would (of course) support their fellows in the media. It's deeply frustrating because it's a form of social jujitsu built atop existing mistrust, and there aren't good ways to combat it."},{"id":28994400,"author":"tyingq","month":"2021-10","story":"Viewing website HTML code is not illegal or “hacking,” prof. tells Missouri gov.","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This bizarre video from a PAC the governor started, still trying to call this \"hacking","text":"Ah, so finally the detail. The site was apparently an ASP.NET site, and they were putting the whole SSN into the \"VIEWSTATE\" object. Which looks something like this in the html: <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"__VIEWSTATE\" id=\"__VIEWSTATE\" value=\"BASE64STUFFHERE=\"> There is a choice to encrypt it, but I'm skeptical how useful that is, or that it was enabled in this case. So the \"hack\" was \"view source\" -> decode some base64 data sitting in plain sight. Edit: A little bonus. This bizarre video from a PAC the governor started, still trying to call this \"hacking\": [VIDEO]"}]},"ECwHZlvvVH4":{"wy":"Mostly shared as a more reliable YouTube mirror, and cited as a related talk on motivation; one commenter criticizes Blow's comments on impostor syndrome.","m":[{"id":18099357,"author":"modernerd","month":"2018-09","story":"Valley Forged: How One Man Made the Indie Video Game Sensation Stardew Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on motivation in a discussion about finding your calling","quote":"","text":"> in four decades of living on this planet I don't think I've ever come across anything that fits that bill. Keep looking! But also, it's possible at 40+ that you may have already found it. Perhaps you've not given that thing the chance it needed for you to fall in love with it — the days and weeks of exploration and the obsessive iteration mentioned in the GQ article that it takes to grok something and absorb it until it feels like it's yours . I'm almost your age and now rewinding my career path. I kept chasing different tech career paths hoping to find something that “fit the bill”, which only showed me that nothing was as interesting to me than what I started my career doing (game design/animation). It took a couple of changes to realise that: - Reflecting on how I spend my free time and time on social media, and the speakers and personalities I'm fascinated by and naturally drawn to. (They are mostly game designers and devs.) - Looking back at the work in the past that has made me happiest and most fulfilled. From there it was a case of having the discipline to commit to it — I spend most mornings and evenings skilling up and having fun with game design and development, although my day job is still in front-end right now. On the motivation side of things you mentioned, I don't think it's a case of “find the right thing and you'll find your motivation.” It's more a case of discipline, projecting your self now to your future self, and knowing a few psychological techniques"},{"id":15416185,"author":"jsheard","month":"2017-10","story":"Techniques for dealing with lack of motivation, malaise, depression [video]","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube mirror of a Twitch VOD with fewer buffering problems","quote":"Here's the YouTube mirror, since Twitch VODs often have janky buffering problems in my experience","text":"Here's the YouTube mirror, since Twitch VODs often have janky buffering problems in my experience: [LINK] + [VIDEO]"},{"id":32372802,"author":"phist_mcgee","month":"2022-08","story":"Cramming 'Papers, Please' onto Phones","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I lost quite a bit of respect for Jonathon Blow when he made comments about impostor syndrome","text":"A bit of a tangent, but I lost quite a bit of respect for Jonathon Blow when he made comments about impostor syndrome: [VIDEO]"}]},"FY7DtKMBxBw":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence of Apple's parts-pairing restrictions in right-to-repair discussions.","m":[{"id":26998297,"author":"bogwog","month":"2021-04","story":"It's your device, you should be able to repair it","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple ships DRM that checks the serial numbers of all the parts","text":"No. Also, \"right to repair\" doesn't mean manufacturers have to change their product designs in any way. iPhones today aren't hard to repair because they're thin, they're hard to repair because Apple ships DRM that checks the serial numbers of all the parts, so that it will refuse to work if you try to replace something. Example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26275552,"author":"neop1x","month":"2021-02","story":"The Framework Laptop","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"you can't even swap parts in iPhone 12 unless you reprogram their EEPROMs","text":"Nah, not anymore. SSD and RAM are soldered on the motherboard in newer Macbooks [1]. And you can't even swap parts in iPhone 12 unless you reprogram their EEPROMs [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24960465,"author":"emrehan","month":"2020-11","story":"Pine64 Smarphone Spare Parts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"iPhone 12 models could only be serviced by Apple.","text":"iPhone 12 models could only be serviced by Apple. A youtuber bought 2 iPhones and swapped their main boards to have a plethora of issues with the screen, battery and cameras: [VIDEO] I’m never going to buy another Apple product. The first blow was being unable to install linux to “my” devices."},{"id":25031268,"author":"Cloudef","month":"2020-11","story":"Review: A Counterfeit, $100 iPhone X","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The device cripples itself thinking it's not genuine iPhone.","text":"There's this video where a person replaces parts with genuine apple parts from another iPhone. And guess what? The device cripples itself thinking it's not genuine iPhone. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25154797,"author":"neop1x","month":"2020-11","story":"Apple Announces App Store Small Business Program","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"iPhone 12 is no longer repairable by swapping parts between devices","text":"While it is quite nice, maybe they did it to look better in the antitrust investigation. They are still forcing devs to use their in-app purchases within iOS apps, forbidding 3rd-party payment processors. And iPhone 12 is no longer repairable by swapping parts between devices [1], Safari is still the only web engine on iOS. Finally after many years they are slowly starting to support royalty-free VP9 codec across the OSs but on the other hand are starting to use proprietary M1 chips to further lock in their users. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"GeCP-0nuziE":{"wy":"Used as a data source on how creators earn money, especially the share from YouTube Premium and sponsors in ad-blocking and advertising threads.","q":{"x":"LTT makes 9.2% of their revenue on In-Video Sponsors and 12.5% on Sponsored Projects","i":45287461,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45287461,"author":"gempir","month":"2025-09","story":"YouTube addresses lower view counts which seem to be caused by ad blockers","stance":"reference","why":"Source for sponsor revenue percentages","quote":"LTT makes 9.2% of their revenue on In-Video Sponsors and 12.5% on Sponsored Projects","text":"With the risk of sounding like a fanboy. LTT makes 9.2% of their revenue on In-Video Sponsors and 12.5% on Sponsored Projects (which are like full videos for a sponsor) I wouldn't call this the vast majority [VIDEO]"},{"id":44335343,"author":"PurestGuava","month":"2025-06","story":"YouTube's new anti-adblock measures","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Premium revenue share figures","quote":"YT Premium made up 37% of their revenue despite being 29% of their views.","text":"YT Premium revenue goes to the same creators you watch otherwise but they get compensated more for your views than they do for any other person's views. e.g. Linus Tech Tips posted up their share of revenue from AdSense in 2024; YT Premium made up 37% of their revenue despite being 29% of their views. Source: [VIDEO] Whether that makes a given creator more money versus Patreon depends on how much you watch them, frankly."},{"id":43601328,"author":"carterpaul","month":"2025-04","story":"What if we made advertising illegal?","stance":"reference","why":"Breakdown of creator income streams","quote":"See Linus Tech Tips’ video on their income streams (skip to 4:40)","text":"YouTube Premium dishes out your revenue to creators based on how much you watch. See Linus Tech Tips’ video on their income streams (skip to 4:40): [VIDEO] Sure, YouTube probably takes more off the top than Patreon. But YouTube also splits it up based on who you’re watching. I probably watch 30+ YouTube channels per week, some of which I find on the explore page and don’t even know the name of. I would never subscribe to 30+ Patreons. I think YouTube Premium is a good compromise."}]},"GmqeZl8OI2M":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference for products with absurd warnings and disclaimers.","m":[{"id":34525961,"author":"nobody9999","month":"2023-01","story":"Do Not Taunt Happy Fun Branch Predictor","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I miss Phil Hartman[0][1][2].","text":"Thank you for posting this. Somewhat OT, but I miss Phil Hartman[0][1][2]. You may remember him[3] from shows like Saturday Night Live, The Simpsons and \"Planet of the Apes.\"[4] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":42006041,"author":"AStonesThrow","month":"2024-10","story":"FDA Permits Marketing of Digital Game to Improve Attention in ADHD Children (2020)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Imagine if the FDA were tasked with cataloging and publishing side effects reported from any and all video games.","text":"Imagine if the FDA were tasked with cataloging and publishing side effects reported from any and all video games. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40799720,"author":"floatrock","month":"2024-06","story":"Ball: A ball that lives in your dock","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"All that's missing is about 3 more README's of warnings and disclosures","text":"> It's a ball. It's fun. It's a ball. All that's missing is about 3 more README's of warnings and disclosures ending with \"Do not taunt Ball.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":40086727,"author":"neilv","month":"2024-04","story":"Drive-Through Car Wash Was Too Much for Tesla Cybertruck","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Do not taunt Happy Fun Ball.","text":"> The Cybertruck’s owners manual does caution against ever washing the truck in direct sunlight, and there is a section expressly mentioning that the truck has to be switched into “Car Wash Mode” before washing to avoid damage to parts of the vehicle. Do not taunt Happy Fun Ball. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20842093,"author":"psychometry","month":"2019-08","story":"The physical Apple Card is a case of form over function","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is some Happy Fun Ball-level absurdity","text":">The Apple Card has been raked through the coals over this support document because it is not recommended for use near leather (the most common material used in wallets) or denim (one of the most common outerwear materials). Nor is it recommended to be touching any other credit cards. Nor is it recommended to be in a pocket or bag containing loose change, keys, or any other kind of potentially-abrasive items. This is some Happy Fun Ball-level absurdity ( [VIDEO] )."}]},"Hi63rXnuWbw":{"wy":"HN cites it mainly as a damning example of manipulative advertising aimed at children rather than recommending it as a watch.","m":[{"id":17563935,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2018-07","story":"The Astonishingly High Administrative Costs of U.S. Health Care","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an argument about corporate lock-in and abuses.","quote":"","text":"\"\"brutal capitalism\" is both more efficient and more compassionate in the long run\" I'm not sure about that. I've watched a lot of scuffles in capitalist markets eliminate most of the competition to turn into lock-in via strong brands, control of territory, copyrights, or patents. The lock-in typically lands on a few companies that become an oligopoly of sorts. These are most noticeable in telecoms, operating systems, big chains of any kind, and so on. Once they form, they barely compete with each other. They mostly screw customers and employees over in common ways to maintain high margins. Internally, they usually form rigid hierarchies that push control more than innovation with the evidence showing they're driven by politics more than performance. If they pay a lot of advertising money, the media will also misrepresent their incompetence taking about how they're in a competitive market, struggling, or so on. Quite a few years on my resume was in one of those companies on Fortune 100 with general public none the wiser. That doesn't even get into all the horrors of capitalism which stim from its incentives and operating environment. These companies systematically mislead people to get talent in the door, demand as much from them as possible, do as little for them as possible, do as much as possible for their controllers, discard them whenever opportune regardless of promises made, externalize as many bad things as possible, and pay bribes to lawmakers to make their evils leg"},{"id":15674090,"author":"corey_moncure","month":"2017-11","story":"YouTube says it will crack down on bizarre videos targeting children","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Our children do not stand a chance.","text":"Whereas in my jurisdiction (USA), corporate media conglomerates employ PhD trained child psychologists who research the exact pattern and timing of flashes and other stimuli to catch an iron grip on a child's attention. Our children do not stand a chance. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15146888,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-09","story":"Insane state of today's advertising part 3","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The nagging factor in children's advertising comes to mind as one really evil example","text":"> From my perspective, I've found various things via advertising that I would have never come across by other means. If you want to make a fair comparison, that should be compared to all the things you didn't find because useless ads took up your time and mental space. Also, whether or not you found something through an ad is not the point; one isn't not going to convince a smoker to quit smoking by trying to convince them that nicotine doesn't make them feel good - it's the other shit about it that is bad. In the case of advertising, there's a lot of cynical manipulation going on. The nagging factor in children's advertising comes to mind as one really evil example: [VIDEO] I've been TV- and ad-free since 2001 (yes, yes, I know: the \"how do you know someone doesn't watch TV? They'll tell you\" -smugness curse strikes again). In my case it was accidental however: I was living in a student appartment with no access to TV and Internet for over a year, having only books to read and rented movies to watch. Even when forced upon you you start noticing the difference pretty quickly, and wondering how you ever was capable of considering this normal. Nowadays ads just feel invasive, like strangers shouting at me on the street claiming they want to have a conversation while really they just want to get into my pockets."},{"id":5735092,"author":"neilk","month":"2013-05","story":"Dear American Consumers: Please don’t start eating healthfully","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"But tell me that the marketers behind the \"Nag Factor\" report don't disgust you.","text":"\"The Nag Factor\": [VIDEO] You don't have to accept the position of the documentary, that the corporate form is intrinsically a bad thing. But tell me that the marketers behind the \"Nag Factor\" report don't disgust you. It is nice to imagine that each family is an island of rationality, well-informed about choices and their eventual effects, never choosing the easy path, immune to marketing and peer pressure. In the real world, things are a little more complex. And the marketers behind reports like \"The Nag Factor\" make millions by subverting family relations to serve their purposes. It is good to be skeptical of regulation, but this is so clearly a case where more good can be done than harm."},{"id":1073590,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2010-01","story":"Ask HN:  Is marketing essentially using brainwashing to propagate a meme?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"let me contribute this little gem","text":"Viral marketing is, 'regular' marketing can be divided in to two branches, one where people are informed about a product and left to draw their own conclusions, and another - much less ethical one - where marketing is used a means of influencing decisions people make in general. The latter I have a real problem with. SwellJoe has already posted Bill Hicks, let me contribute this little gem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":810672,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2009-09","story":"Bertrand Russell, \"On Sales Resistance\"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Have a look at this for how far the 'salespeople' are willing to go","text":"The story about the salesman pushing the grand piano and a wall being knocked down into the bedroom to make room for it. It was clearly meant tongue-in-cheek, or at least that is how I took it. Of course nobody buys a piano because of a pushy salesman if their house can not accomodate it, but it is a nice analogy to describe some of the process that goes on when people buy stuff they don't need or have the ability to use. Have a look at this for how far the 'salespeople' are willing to go: [VIDEO]"},{"id":710166,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2009-07","story":"Why I won't be at my high school reunion","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"For a nice example have a look at this","text":"I think that you should take a comedians word with a grain of salt. What Bill Hicks was literally getting at was that to use your talent as a wordsmith to create wants where there are none and to pursue money without any consideration for ethics by trying to translate each and every human emotion into the dollar equivalent and a 'hook' by which to gain acess to peoples wallets is a very wrong thing. Comedians will exaggerate grossly in order to make their point. See Carlin, Connolly and many others for more examples of such behaviour. It's up to us to separate out the core facts from the poetic license. The kind of marketing that the great-grandfather post of this comment referred to is exactly the wrong way to use marketing. There is a book by some guy called 'how to win friends and influence people', it is a great example of how not to use skills with words, it is simple manipulations and there is a world of a difference between manipulation and reasonable discourse between consenting parties. The latter is what sets us - amongst many other things - apart from the animals, not our ability to 'market' or to 'sell' where no need exists. Marketeers now routinely employ psychologists in order to learn better which of our buttons they should push in order to get access to our innermost feelings so that we go and do what they want, consume their product. For a nice example have a look at this: [VIDEO] And tell me what you think of that."}]},"JLoge6QzcGY":{"wy":"Posted as a humorous aside in threads about clutter, inheritance, and consumerism.","m":[{"id":43423506,"author":"anonu","month":"2025-03","story":"The Shitthropocene","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"In the US we love our big houses and we fill them up with crap.","text":"(Maybe TMI) Last week my wife was admiring a pair of Patagonia boxers she gave me over 10 years ago. I did note they still looked great after probably a few 100 cycles of washing and drying. I commented that Patagonia made great stuff and actually cared about their materials. So this video is timely. I'm reminded about what Scott Bessent, the new Treasury Secretary, said earlier this month: Cheap goods are not part of the American Dream [0]. In the US we love our big houses and we fill them up with crap. When the crap overflows it just gets stored in our big garages were we also put our oversized cars. [1] How we got here today is just the result of the post-WW2 world-order. Global trade was designed to make cheap stuff. While labor rates skyrocketed. So you make more money but you can surround yourself with status symbols and things that supposedly make you more comfortable. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44134370,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2025-05","story":"The David Lynch Collection","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant George Carlin bit on the subject","text":"You see how big houses get in America? All the houses are FULL of stuff Relevant George Carlin bit on the subject: [VIDEO] ?"},{"id":15062739,"author":"wsc981","month":"2017-08","story":"Aging Parents with Lots of Stuff, and Children Who Don’t Want It","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Just for fun, George Carlin (RIP) on 'stuff'","text":"I like to put all my photos on Google Photos. That way I don't have to store them in my house. I would love to use a Kindle for all my reading, but physical books are still more pleasurable to read. It makes it hard for me to part with some books for now. Though some books are only worth reading once and I am happy to give them away after finishing them. Just for fun, George Carlin (RIP) on 'stuff': [VIDEO]"}]},"LSZPNwZex9s":{"wy":"Used as a joke or as a reference to Google's April Fools pranks and the idea of autonomous bikes, not as something to learn from.","m":[{"id":27597556,"author":"otagekki","month":"2021-06","story":"Engineer Builds Self-Balancing Autonomous Bicycle in Spare Time","stance":"reference","why":"Google's April Fools self-driving bicycle prank video","quote":"I remember a few years back an April fools prank on a self-balancing bike by Google","text":"Very impressive indeed! I remember a few years back an April fools prank on a self-balancing bike by Google ( [VIDEO] ). Glad to know that it actually exists five years later"},{"id":13174192,"author":"kbenson","month":"2016-12","story":"Waymo: Google's self-driving car company","stance":"reference","why":"Google Netherlands self-driving bicycle concept video","quote":"Autonomous bikes[1]. Not that you need them to drive you, but that you can rent a bike and have it show up for you","text":"Sure. I just wanted to run the numbers to see how it actually broke down currently, and what we could theoretically aim for. Light rail systems are useful in that they are efficient, and can readily move large amounts of people currently. Unfortunately their delivery characteristics are very rigid, and they have a very large up-front cost. An autonomous car system that supports easy ride sharing, and semi-public transportation through something like uber for busses can fulfill much of the same need, but without quite the expected density, but with the ability to deliver to just about any location. Both would benefit from a last-mile service . Sort of like arteries and capillaries in the circulatory system. Actually, you know what would be really cool with this? Autonomous bikes[1]. Not that you need them to drive you, but that you can rent a bike and have it show up for you, or rent it at the bus depot/train station, and have it return after you've reached your destination. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12042003,"author":"lorenzhs","month":"2016-07","story":"Bay Area 2050: The BART Metro Map","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Denmark and the Netherlands both have lots of shit weather and enormous amounts of cycling. As titzer pointed out, the Bay area has neither. Weather is not a problem with a raincoat, rain pants, and the right attitude :) I always reach the office dry. Admittedly, my commute is fairly short by US standards (5km / 15 minutes), but that's not atypical for cities in Germany. Winters are no problem with the right tyres, as bike paths are cleared of snow by the city. As for luggage: Panniers are awesome. I have 30 year old Ortlieb bags (see [LINK] for the modern version), 40 litres of cargo volume right there. That's enough for my weekly grocery shopping and you still have space on the luggage rack for a basket or whatever. Oh, and to your last point: [VIDEO] - like that? ;)"},{"id":11403900,"author":"hakvroot","month":"2016-04","story":"Google Cardboard Plastic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I found that at least one \"local\" Google is also introducing an innovative new concept today. Google Netherlands : [VIDEO] Any more from Google <your country here>?"},{"id":11404667,"author":"gotrythis","month":"2016-04","story":"Google 'mic drop' Gmail joke for April Fools' day backfires","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is a real Google style April fools joke","text":"I think this article is a joke, making it a really good April fools joke. This is a real Google style April fools joke: [VIDEO]"}]},"NwVBzx0LMNQ":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of sci-fi interfaces and whether ideas like pinch-to-zoom were prior art.","q":{"x":"Minority Report came out in 2002. It contains pinch-to-zoom. It is obvious.","i":4439225,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":42287777,"author":"johnchristopher","month":"2024-12","story":"Map UI – Ghost in the Shell","stance":"reference","why":"Scene cited to discuss data movement between screens","quote":"In minority report there's a scene","text":"In minority report there's a scene ( [VIDEO] ) in a forensic/detective office in which MC swipes data and info on the screen (hologram ?) with their hands. Then at some point another character moves a physical object (data drive ?) from one desk to another and at first I am thinking \"yeah, right.. no network to move data from one screen to the other, uh ?\". But then I remind myself of this passage from Virtual lights (Gibson): > Was it significant that Skinner shared his dwelling with one who earned her living at the archaic intersection of information and geography? The offices the girl rode between were electronically conterminous-in effect, a single desktop, the map of distances obliterated by the seamless and instantaneous nature of communication. > Yet this very seamlessness, which had rendered physical mail an expensive novelty, might as easily be viewed as porosity, and as such created the need for the service the girl provided. Physically transporting bits of information about a grid that consisted of little else, she provided a degree of absolute security in the fluid universe of data. > With your memo in the girl's bag, you knew precisely where it was; otherwise, your memo was nowhere, perhaps everywhere, in that instant of transit."},{"id":4439225,"author":"natrius","month":"2012-08","story":"Apple v. Samsung juror: we “wanted to send a message”","stance":"reference","why":"Minority Report shows pinch-to-zoom in 2002","quote":"Minority Report came out in 2002. It contains pinch-to-zoom. It is obvious.","text":"Minority Report came out in 2002. It contains pinch-to-zoom. It is obvious. [VIDEO] Edit: Another commenter linked to this 2002 video, which is a better example: [LINK]"},{"id":1270600,"author":"patrickk","month":"2010-04","story":"The Future is Movie OS","stance":"reference","why":"Prototype UI from the designer of Minority Report's interface","quote":"the guy who designed the amazing gesture interface behind those in 'Minority Report' started a company","text":"You may be onto something here. I remember reading somewhere that Bond gadgets become true after something like 30 years, however I suspect that this pace of development will be speed up dramatically as time goes on. Some examples off the top of my head: GPS - there's an old Bond movie with a analog 'homing device', weapons on cars, cars that can drive on the road and fly also, 'stealth technology' like in Die Another Day (in development). Getting back to movie inspired OSs, the guy who designed the amazing gesture interface behind those in 'Minority Report' started a company and has prototype UI in development: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"P9yruQM1ggc":{"wy":"It is posted as a punchline in threads about invasive species, pests and well-meant environmental fixes with side effects.","m":[{"id":36003615,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2023-05","story":"Satellites reveal widespread decline in global lake water storage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Always a good time for[0]: Skinner: Well, I was wrong; the lizards are a godsend. Lisa: But isn't that a bit short-sighted? What happens when we're overrun by lizards? Skinner: No problem. We simply release wave after wave of Chinese needle snakes. They'll wipe out the lizards. Lisa: But aren't the snakes even worse? Skinner: Yes, but we're prepared for that. We've lined up a fabulous type of gorilla that thrives on snake meat. Lisa: But then we're stuck with gorillas! Skinner: No, that's the beautiful part. When wintertime rolls around, the gorillas simply freeze to death. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20404771,"author":"sigmar","month":"2019-07","story":"Project Vesta – Mitigating climate change with green sand beaches","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of The Simpsons episode where they let Lizards get rid of the pigeons, then plan to have snakes kill the lizards, then have gorillas eat the snakes. Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20405371,"author":"wumms","month":"2019-07","story":"Cockroaches becoming resistant to most chemical insecticides","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Since Skinner recommended gorillas: [VIDEO] ... I had to check [0]: at least mountain gorillas can survive temperatures below freezing. [0] [LINK]"},{"id":20111320,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2019-06","story":"African Swine Fever Is Spreading and Eliminating It Will Take Decades","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Bolivian tree lizard cleanup strategy: [VIDEO] Skinner: Well, I was wrong; the lizards are a godsend. Lisa: But isn't that a bit short-sighted? What happens when we're overrun by lizards? Skinner: No problem. We simply release wave after wave of Chinese needle snakes. They'll wipe out the lizards. Lisa: But aren't the snakes even worse? Skinner: Yes, but we're prepared for that. We've lined up a fabulous type of gorilla that thrives on snake meat. Lisa: But then we're stuck with gorillas! Skinner: No, that's the beautiful part. When wintertime rolls around, the gorillas simply freeze to death. Ref: [LINK]"}]},"QxtkvG1JnPk":{"wy":"Cited as an example of corporate-mandated scripts and concentrated media ownership.","q":{"x":"So much that they even end up repeating exact phrases in their seemingly independent broadcasts","i":30239840,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34041773,"author":"EGreg","month":"2022-12","story":"Promotion of alternative social platforms policy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of employees compelled to repeat corporate scripts","quote":"Anytime an employee (e.g. a news anchor) has to stay away from topics, repeat talking points, or get fired and replaced by an organization","text":"I think putting this policy in place is actually good for Twitter's bottom line. People will be promoting all kinds of competitors and trying to get people on Twitter to retweet those things. A competitor is some site that acts as a third party, not the actual media article or primary thing to link to. So Twitter having this model makes business sense. Does it makes Elon Musk a hypocrite? Of course it does!!! Capitalism restricts free speech, and even compels other speech. Here is an example: [VIDEO] Anytime an employee (e.g. a news anchor) has to stay away from topics, repeat talking points, or get fired and replaced by an organization, that is capitalism at work. They are an employee being used as a mouthpiece, not a human being saying what they really think. Capitalism and private ownership of large organizations is not about freedom. The concept of ownership is literally about restricting other people's freedoms to do as they wish with what you own, and compelling employees to do this or that, or get fired. There's this weird assumption that capitalism somehow encourages free speech. And guys like Elon Musk and Donald Trump are therefore associated with it, and allowed to spew garbage about Free Speech, because they're billionaire capitalists. Um, no. To go in depth, watch this interview I did with Noam Chomsky last year: [LINK]"},{"id":30239840,"author":"quadrifoliate","month":"2022-02","story":"Mark Zuckerberg’s Disaster Is Taking Silicon Valley with It","stance":"reference","why":"Montage of Sinclair anchors repeating identical scripts","quote":"So much that they even end up repeating exact phrases in their seemingly independent broadcasts","text":"So much that they even end up repeating exact phrases in their seemingly independent broadcasts, as seen in [VIDEO]"},{"id":24574758,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2020-09","story":"The Cement of Society Is...","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Sinclair's scripted local news ownership","quote":"","text":"It's easier to believe when from your understanding of the world the other side is blatantly lying and misrepresenting things. It forms a bit of a positive feedback loop where seeing bad intentions leads you to being less likely to give slack so you see more bad intentions. From the left looking rightward at Fox News and many local stations (increasingly owned by and forced to run identical scripts from Sinclair Broadcasting) [0] at the response and the response to the response to coronavirus for example it's hard to ascribe that to just a different interpretation of facts. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"QyMsF31NdNc":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as the primary source for the 'multiplicative gameplay' idea and the chemistry engine behind BotW.","q":{"x":"broke down the creation of the game in a full length session with video of the original top-down 2D prototype","i":16789592,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":41809197,"author":"iamwil","month":"2024-10","story":"Game Programming in Prolog","stance":"reference","why":"Explains BotW's chemistry engine of material interactions.","quote":"The chemistry engine figures out how materials interact in an alchemy sense.","text":"The Breath of the Wild game design has a concept called the \"Chemistry Engine\". In that game engines usually have a physics engine to figure out how things interact in a motion sense. The chemistry engine figures out how materials interact in an alchemy sense. It's kinda like a rules-based engine to figure out how different things interact with different other things, so you get surprising interactions between everything in the world, like being able to light arrows on fire, because arrows are a \"wood\" material. The Youtube link is here: [VIDEO] It seems like most rule-based stuff in games is just hand-coded, as it's relatively simple and doesn't need to be general. When I asked the author of Baba is You, if he implemented a datalog engine for it, he said 'no'. I suspect it's the same for Breath of the Wild. But still, I've often wondered if that sort of chemistry engine would be best implemented in a logic language like Prolog or Datalog, for fast experimentation. Just like how we use SQL to keep the flexibility of our queries, and we end up just shipping that. I'm sure, back in the day, lots of people lamented how slow SQL queries were. The flexibility was useful enough that we ended up pouring man-centuries into making them fast. Now, we think that's just the way you ship things, and (almost) never think, \"I can hand-roll imperative code that will be faster than this query\"."},{"id":35923528,"author":"iamwil","month":"2023-05","story":"The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom Release","stance":"reference","why":"GDC talk on the chemistry engine for BotW.","quote":"And at GDC, they talked about their Chemistry engine for BotW","text":"Yes, it's rare, but they have. Game maker's toolkit has one on how they solved their open world problem for BotW: - [LINK] And at GDC, they talked about their Chemistry engine for BotW: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] Game historian has some tidbits of how they made certain design decisions for their successful games Mario Kart: - [LINK] Super Mario World: - [LINK] Super Mario Bro 3: - [LINK] GMT, Snowman, and Extra credit does analysis of how Nintendo designs their Mario levels. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] Generally, it follows the ramp up and down of the Hero's journey in storytelling. 1. Introduce a new mechanic in a safe area where it's impossible to die. 2. Increase the challenge with the mechanic by adding variations 3. Ramp up challenge even more by combining with previously learned mechanic 4. Have periods of rest inbetween the challenges."},{"id":16789592,"author":"doomlaser","month":"2018-04","story":"Why I’m Using the Cerny Method of Game Development","stance":"reference","why":"Full-length breakdown of the game's creation with 2D prototype footage.","quote":"broke down the creation of the game in a full length session with video of the original top-down 2D prototype","text":"Last year at GDC, Nintendo's Hidemaro Fujibayashi, Satoru Takizawa, and Takuhiro Dohta broke down the creation of the game in a full length session with video of the original top-down 2D prototype: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13872031,"author":"flgr","month":"2017-03","story":"The new legend of Zelda is a toy box of delights","stance":"reference","why":"GDC talk presents multiplicative gameplay concept.","quote":"one of the core concepts they present is \"multiplicative gameplay","text":"In their GDC talk ( [VIDEO] ) one of the core concepts they present is \"multiplicative gameplay\", which sounds a lot like what NetHack and Dungeon Crawl Stone Soup aim for as well. It's a surprising amount of fun to have multiple ways to solve puzzles and try to come up with one that was or was not intended to solve it."},{"id":13872298,"author":"BHSPitMonkey","month":"2017-03","story":"The new legend of Zelda is a toy box of delights","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp showing the internal 2D chemistry engine prototype.","quote":"Here's a direct link to the timestamp where they talk about this, and show off an internal 2D prototype of their \"chemistry engine\"","text":"Here's a direct link to the timestamp where they talk about this, and show off an internal 2D prototype of their \"chemistry engine\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":13883169,"author":"Agentlien","month":"2017-03","story":"The new legend of Zelda is a toy box of delights","stance":"reference","why":"Shows even a simple prototype was evidently fun.","quote":"That I can see how even in such a simple prototype the fun seemed evident.","text":"Yes, they talk about it in the GDC talk[1] and that's what I was referring to: That I can see how even in such a simple prototype the fun seemed evident. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"ROlDmux7Tk4":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke or period reference for banker stereotypes.","m":[{"id":19994228,"author":"nateburke","month":"2019-05","story":"Goldman Sachs, Patagonia, and the Mysteries of “Business Casual”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows banker attire stereotypes in NYC a decade ago","quote":"Supporting data point from 10 years ago in NYC","text":"Supporting data point from 10 years ago in NYC: [VIDEO] Banker: suit Consultant: patagonia vest Tech: not represented"},{"id":6766726,"author":"evadne","month":"2013-11","story":"Stupid Things Finance People Say ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I must present: Damn, It Feels Good to Be a Banker","text":"I must present: Damn, It Feels Good to Be a Banker [VIDEO] Also a book from Leveraged Sellout with the same title: [LINK]"},{"id":3663820,"author":"Vergle","month":"2012-03","story":"Call yourself a 'brogrammer'? Then get the hell away from me.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Damn it feels good to be a banker!","text":"\".. bro! Keep your fucking work life balance.\" ... \"This is wall street bitch you don't belong here!\" ... \"Damn it feels good to be a banker!\" [VIDEO]"}]},"XamC7-Pt8N0":{"wy":"Linked in career and burnout threads as shorthand for the freedom financial independence gives you.","q":{"x":"We should all strive to live in a position of f%%% you.","i":44620988,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45765464,"author":"reducesuffering","month":"2025-10","story":"Taking money off the table","stance":"reference","why":"Film scene about saving money to gain independence.","quote":"","text":"\"Jim Bennett: I've been up two and a half million dollars. Frank: What you got on you? Jim Bennett: Nothing. Frank: What you put away? Jim Bennett: Nothing. Frank: You get up two and a half million dollars, any asshole in the world knows what to do: you get a house with a 25 year roof, an indestructible Jap-economy shitbox, you put the rest into the system at three to five percent to pay your taxes and that's your base, get me? That's your fortress of fucking solitude. That puts you, for the rest of your life, at a level of fuck you. Somebody wants you to do something, fuck you. Boss pisses you off, fuck you! Own your house. Have a couple bucks in the bank. Don't drink. That's all I have to say to anybody on any social level. Did your grandfather take risks? Jim Bennett: Yes. Frank: I guarantee he did it from a position of fuck you. A wise man's life is based around fuck you. The United States of America is based on fuck you. You're a king? You have an army? Greatest navy in the history of the world? Fuck you! Blow me. We'll fuck it up ourselves.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45089377,"author":"JustExAWS","month":"2025-09","story":"Ask HN: If burnout tells you that you are in the wrong job/career, why avoid it?","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on financial independence.","quote":"","text":"No. Burnout has one and only one reason - the lack of courage or resources to say “no”. No company can make you overwork yourself. The worse they can do is fire you [1]. Yes I know most people need a job. But don’t put yourself in a position where you need your current job. I have worked for 30 years across 10 jobs everything from lifestyle companies, startups, medium size companies, boring old large enterprise companies and BigTech and currently full time as a staff architect at a third party cloud consulting company. Never once have I been stressed or experienced anything close to burn out because I was always prepared to look for another job. Being prepared means having a strong network, living below your means, saving money, keeping your skills in sync with the market, and being interview ready. I respond to every reputable recruiter whether I am looking or not. Also whatever you do, don’t be a ticket taker. Before you say you don’t make enough to save, if you make a standard developer wage (not BigTech wages) and you have 3-5 years of experience, you are probably making twice the median wage in your area. I don’t do side projects and never have. I close my computer when I get off work. I exercise consistently and religiously and have since I was 15 in 1990. I prioritize spending time with family and friends for my mental health In other words, work toward a position of f%%% you. [VIDEO] And before anyone says I don’t know what looking for a job is like these days, I was "},{"id":44825928,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2025-08","story":"Open AI announces $1.5M bonus for every employee","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on financial security from savings.","quote":"(NSFW language, first 45 seconds is the relevant part)","text":"If you have no mortgage, own your home, and have saved enough in investments, even if AGI/ASI materially impairs the labor economy, you are in a far better position than someone who rents, someone with a large mortgage who needs a job for the next 20-30 years, etc assuming rule of law holds up. Free money is free money, to not take it would be silly. Just don't blow it on a Lambo. I'm not qualified to touch on your point wrt constraining and wrangling machine intelligence, so no comment on that. [VIDEO] (NSFW language, first 45 seconds is the relevant part)"},{"id":44501065,"author":"scarface_74","month":"2025-07","story":"Ask HN: Took a break after burnout – what now?","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on financial security allowing you to say no.","quote":"","text":"The question is, with that can money in the bank and your expenses so low, you are already living in a position of f%%% you ( [VIDEO] ), how were you experiencing burnout? You close your computer after you put in 40 hours a week and go home. You try your best to communicate in trade offs between time, budget and requirements and if that doesn’t work and you get fired for refusing to be overworked, why do you care? I’ve been working for 30 years from startups, to boring enterprise companies, to BigTech and now working as a staff consultant for a third party cloud consulting company. Never once have I experienced burn out. Not because I’m special, I’m disciplined enough to say “no”, have savings (nowhere near what you have), and I’m always prepared to get another job - yes during the dot com bust, the 2008 recession and twice since 2023. I’m not in a position where I don’t have to work. But I am in a position where I can say no to bullshit - being overworked, mistreated, being forced to return to office, say “no” to high pressure BigTech opportunities, etc. In your position? I would travel (well I do that a lot now including nomadding), enjoy life, work for a risky startup that will probably under pay you but you might enjoy it and worse case you can walk away, volunteer, etc"},{"id":44620988,"author":"scarface_74","month":"2025-07","story":"Ask HN: How do you digest fact that you are not successful by 40","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on living in a position of financial independence.","quote":"We should all strive to live in a position of f%%% you.","text":"I wouldn’t go that far. I also commented about this about a month and half ago. We should all strive to live in a position of f%%% you. ( [VIDEO] ). I’m not there yet long term."},{"id":39092340,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: How do you know it's time for you to quit your FAANG job?","stance":"reference","why":"Scene defining a position of financial independence.","quote":"","text":"I left a FAANG-like job in 2016 (VP, CTO) to join a startup. It was a disaster. I then co-founded a startup. I left two years later, parted ways with the other two co-founders in a friendly way. All this time, I funded my life with my savings. I had enough on the side to do that, and thankfully other things went well in my life, so I don't really regret it, but... If I could go back, I'd simply tell myself that most of the time, quitting a well-paying job if you are not in a F*ck-you money [0] territory, is a bad idea. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38478836,"author":"aniken","month":"2023-11","story":"Elon Musk: \"Don't advertise. Go f*** yourself\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Elon is literally exercising his “f*ck you money” position: [VIDEO] How can a true source of truth free speech platform be anything other than a non profit model like Wikipedia? Free speech requires speaking the truth, and the truth makes most people, including companies / advertisers uncomfortable."}]},"XqZsoesa55w":{"wy":"Mentioned mainly as a statistic or joke about being the most watched video in history.","m":[{"id":48350444,"author":"breve","month":"2026-05","story":"Dav2d","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a hugely viewed video where bitrate savings matter.","quote":"","text":"When you host videos with near 17 billion views you're going to want to stream those videos in as few bits as possible: [VIDEO] That extra 25% becomes worth it. Nothing will become obsolete. AV1 will stick around for a long time. And YouTube still does H.264 encodes to support old devices."},{"id":34353573,"author":"bombcar","month":"2023-01","story":"The Disappearance of the Hit-Driven Business Model","stance":"reference","why":"Most watched video in history.","quote":"the absolute most watched video in history","text":"Disney+ is for kids I’d say. The absolute wealth of that back catalog is insane. However they’re hard pressed at the bottom by YouTube two hour repeat videos of Baby Shark (the absolute most watched video in history - [VIDEO] - 12 billion )"},{"id":32896221,"author":"gnull","month":"2022-09","story":"An X11 apologist tries Wayland","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Problem is horrible lags, and I mean just program working bad, not latency. I tried doing it with X11 now, and I gave up before I could even type \"youtube\" to address bar. Then I tried doing ssh -X remote firefox [VIDEO] and it wasn't better. I saw the new firefox window, but it stayed unresponsive. And I bet part of the problem is due to Youtube (and browser generally) involving some non-trivial drawing technologies, rendering a video is not like showing a GTK interface."},{"id":24059984,"author":"PopeDotNinja","month":"2020-08","story":"Google Music shutdown starts this month, music deleted in December","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was wondering when this would happen. YouTube Music sucks so bad. They mix in all of my liked videos w/ into my music. Just because I upvoted Baby Shark Dance [1] doesn't mean I want it in my effin' music rotation. Spotify is annoying because the radio stations are garbage & I can't (or haven't yet figured out how to) downvote music. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33121770,"author":"bombcar","month":"2022-10","story":"Reality is just a game now","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I suspect 10 billion of those may be my kids","text":"The \"customized feed for you\" is not as unique as you'd expect. It basically is the algorithm fitting you into some number of buckets and then serving you that content. And speaking of shared experiences, [VIDEO] has been watched 11 billion times. However, I suspect 10 billion of those may be my kids ..."},{"id":18412122,"author":"shawn","month":"2018-11","story":"A History of the Sentence \"Buffalo buffalo buffalo buffalo buffalo.\" (1999-2015)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Baby Shark is almost at 2 billion youtube views","text":"Dan, I have to tell you, Baby Shark is almost at 2 billion youtube views: [VIDEO] There's also a remix: [LINK] Two, in fact: [LINK] Would you be interested in collaborating on a metal remix of Baby Shark? I think it could do well. I'm looking for a vocalist. I can't pay you yet, but you'd get exposure. EDIT: His teeth still bared in a grin, the Procurator glanced at the prisoner, then at the sun rising inexorably over the equestrian statues of the hippodrome far below to his left, and suddenly in a moment of agonising nausea it occurred to him that the simplest thing would be to dismiss this curious rascal from his balcony with no more than two words : ' Hang him. ' Dismiss the body-guard too, leave the arcade and go indoors, order the room to be darkened, fall on to his couch, send for cold water, call for his dog Banga in a pitiful voice and complain to the dog about his hemicrania. Suddenly the tempting thought of poison flashed through the Procurator's mind. He stared dully at the prisoner for a while, trying painfully to recall why this man with the bruised face was standing in front of him in the pitiless Jerusalem morning sunshine and what further useless questions he should put to him."}]},"dFUlAQZB9Ng":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up as a joke about ridiculous movie computing, though some note it was a real Unix file browser (FSN).","m":[{"id":4048070,"author":"cleverjake","month":"2012-05","story":"The girl with the ANSI tattoo","stance":"reference","why":"Jurassic Park scene showing a real Unix file system browser.","quote":"actually was a real unix system running FSN","text":"Matrix 2 rather famously involved an actual ssh escalation using nmap. Takedown (aka Track Down) was another fairly accurate example of hacking (though highly inaccurate historically). One of the more famous examples of \"Movie OSes\", Jurassic Park ( [VIDEO] ), actually was a real unix system running FSN"},{"id":44265529,"author":"Arainach","month":"2025-06","story":"Microsoft Office migration from Source Depot to Git","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Piper's syntax is Perforce syntax. I moved to Google from Microsoft and back when employee orientation involved going to Mountain View and going into labs to learn the basics, it was amusing to see fresh college hires confused at not-git while I sat down and said \"It's Source Depot, I know this!\"[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18566733,"author":"somberi","month":"2018-11","story":"Show HN: Arcentry – Create Interactive Cloud Diagrams","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Couldn't help noting the similarity between your end product and Jurassic Park's layout :)","text":"Congrats on an useful service, that I will pay for. Couldn't help noting the similarity between your end product and Jurassic Park's layout :) [VIDEO] (around the 15th second)."},{"id":10685328,"author":"jasode","month":"2015-12","story":"Pricing Programmers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I can't really find an explanation for it so far. He basically hinted at that by saying: \"because I get to do what I love (programming,...)\" I believe you and gp (lemevi) are talking from 2 different perspectives. Your perspective is more about \"don't sell yourself short\" or \"don't devalue your skills\". The gp's perspective is more about the \"mystery of the universe\" as to why an enjoyable job could pay more than a boring one. It's a similar sentiment to the successful novelists, comedians, musicians saying, \"I feel lucky that people pay me lots of money for doing what I love to do.\" (Yes, there are tons of programmers who suffer through boring CRUD coding and treat it as \"just a job\" but there are also many programmers who truly enjoy the activity.) I experienced the gp's perspective in my first job out of highschool. I happened to be a decent typist so I got a job to transcribe audio to word processing documents. One day, the business owner was looking at his screen in frustration trying to make something work. I looked over his shoulder and saw things like \"DO WHILE\" and \"DEFINE PROCEDURE\" on the screen. I just looked at it and then had one of those Jurassic Park moments[1]. I said, \"you need to set a variable here, and move that line there, etc etc.\" He was totally surprised and said he'd pay me double the hourly rate if I could fix the computer program. I couldn't believe it. I had programmed computers during middle school (age 11 to 13) and treated the whole coding ac"},{"id":10556765,"author":"oldbuzzard","month":"2015-11","story":"Which Hollywood movies feature the most ridiculous code?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Jurassic Park with that weird 3-d graphical tree file system","text":"Jurassic Park with that weird 3-d graphical tree file system and the girl geek exclaiming roughly \"Cool, its Unix!\" gets my vote.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"eSBybJGZoCU":{"wy":"Posted as a favorite Kraftwerk song or an obligatory link in threads about Kraftwerk and calculators.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite Kraftwerk songs is probably “pocket calculator.","i":23096246,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":45397776,"author":"AfterHIA","month":"2025-09","story":"Samsung now owns Denon, Bowers and Wilkins, Marantz, Polk, and more audio brands","stance":"reference","why":"Lyric quoted in music history argument","quote":"","text":"I really don't think so. Punk had exploded after the Summer of Hate and in its wake we got Talking Heads, The B-52's, and Blondie at CBGB's. In parallel we have early Joy Division in Manchester which is going to inspire U2 to eventually become the biggest band in the world for the next 20 or something years. Martin Hannett is going to be a key figure in the development of EDM with his use of samplers and, \"using the studio as an instrument.\" Check out the beginning of, \"Transmission.\" He'll later go on to produce The Happy Mondays who are, \"the\" seminal Manchester dance group in the early 90's in lieu of an army of Stone Roses fans throwing lemons at me. [LINK] If you look at how Johnny Rotten was listening to Neu! and X-Ray Spex (as well as The Monkees and Herman Hermits) and what Bowie was doing after he moved to Berlin with Iggy Pop it's not outlandish to say that Electronic Music wouldn't have existed without being preempted by Punk, Glam, and the emergent, \"not progressive\" guitar music of the 1970's especially in how it paralleled the development of Krautrock. Disco didn't evolve into EDM; Disco gave DJ Kool Herc a set of turntables to invent Hip Hop with. Techno literally came out of, \"a certain guy who might be called Gerald\" listening to Kraftwerk; Afrika Bammbatta's, \"Planet Rock\" samples Kraftwerk directly. \"I'm the operator with mine pocket calculator.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37271865,"author":"defrost","month":"2023-08","story":"When Kraftwerk Issued Their Own Pocket Calculator Synthesizer (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory direct link to Kraftwerk's Pocket Calculator","text":"Obligatory direct link to Kraftwerk's Pocket Calculator : [VIDEO] as an excuse to sideload a link to the Balanescu Quartet's cover of Kraftwerk's The Robots : [LINK]"},{"id":23096246,"author":"gdubs","month":"2020-05","story":"Florian Schneider has died","stance":"reference","why":"Favorite Kraftwerk song","quote":"One of my favorite Kraftwerk songs is probably “pocket calculator.","text":"Legend. I saw Kraftwerk live years ago and it was one of the most overwhelming performances I’ve ever seen in my life. Beneath the bouncy, poppy hooks there was a massive darkness — the pairing of visuals with the music really drove that home. (The only other live musical performance that I recall giving me a similar feeling was the band ‘Godspeed You Black Emperor‘, who also made use of projected imagery in such a tightly-coupled fashion.) One of my favorite Kraftwerk songs is probably “pocket calculator.” [1] 1: [VIDEO]"}]},"k7YVxLLIuGM":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke in threads about large vehicles, car safety and absurd hypothetical scenarios.","m":[{"id":38744479,"author":"egeozcan","month":"2023-12","story":"Mercedes Gets Approval for Turquoise Automated Driving Lights","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a random channel in YouTube beats Simpsons to speculate on one absurd scenario","text":"This is the rare moment where a random channel in YouTube beats Simpsons to speculate on one absurd scenario: [VIDEO] It's not exactly what you describe but it comes close."},{"id":38272002,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"Vehicles with higher, more vertical front ends pose greater risk to pedestrians","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Your puny truck stands no chance.","text":"And this is precisely why I fly the Supermaneuverable Sukhoi Su-35 Flanker E Advanced Fighter [1]. Your puny truck stands no chance. Man up and dogfight me. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34318188,"author":"andrecarini","month":"2023-01","story":"‘That Girl Is Going to Get Herself Killed’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Your reactive armour will be worthless in the face of my active measures. Suddenly [1] isn't so ridiculous anymore... [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"mUqeBMP8nEg":{"wy":"Referenced when discussing how real the disguise technology shown in spy movies is.","q":{"x":"The CIA's former Chief of Disguise says they very much exist and are used, with some limitations","i":29310593,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35628719,"author":"cnntth","month":"2023-04","story":"Explore Washington DC through the eyes of a spy – an IRL scavenger hunt","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with the CIA's former chief of disguise.","quote":"the former 'chief of disguises' for the cia does interviews[1] all the time","text":"To tag on to the others, the former 'chief of disguises' for the cia does interviews[1] all the time, I think also on the board for the Spy Museum (and featured quite a bit in it about both disguises and being a woman). Depending on their personality, why not play up the 'former cia/fbi/nsa' thing to educate/entertain/lend legitimacy to their business? [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29310593,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2021-11","story":"Fingerprints can be hacked","stance":"reference","why":"Former CIA disguise chief discusses realistic masks and spy-film disguise tech.","quote":"The CIA's former Chief of Disguise says they very much exist and are used, with some limitations","text":"The CIA's former Chief of Disguise says they very much exist and are used, with some limitations [1]. Her comment on the 3d printer making the mask: \"What if I said we had it\". Of course that's not really surprising when you look at the kind of Halloween masks you can get if you are willing to pay [2]. I imagine if you could special order them to perfectly fit your head they would be very convincing to the casual observer and to software. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":22354560,"author":"dntbnmpls","month":"2020-02","story":"An investigation of the Pentagon's UFO program","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Interesting theory and I suppose it's possible but unlikely. > We have hundreds and thousands of eyes in the sky but they will not report because they are afraid to speak. This isn't true. Pilots report UFOs all the time. > In past decades whenever military pilot reported UFO they were laughed at, sent to psychologist, evaluated, sent behind the desk. If true, this would only be true for UFOs that acted beyond the scope of modern technology ( aka \"alien\" ufos ). Not for normals UFOs that are simply unidentified flying objects. > Goal is to encourage pilots to report possible Chinese drones. Pilots were already encouraged to report chinese or any other drones. Not only that drones can be detected by drone-targeting radar. I know we are in a trade war and everyone has to push anti-chinese propaganda, but not everything is tied to \"china\". Before long every top comment is going to be nonsense with \"china\" in it. My guess is that after the \"state\" has come under attack recently and people have lost trust in the \"state\", the \"state\" is releasing information to seem more transparent and open and likeable. Sort of like how the CIA, FBI, etc are putting people all over media/social media. \"Former CIA Chief of Disguise Breaks Down 30 Spy Scenes From Film & TV \" [VIDEO] \"Former FBI Agent Explains How to Read Body Language | Tradecraft | WIRED\" [LINK] The past few years have been relentless \"china bad\", \"russia bad\" and \"we good\" for understandable reasons. But who really knows. It coul"}]},"o48KzPa42_o":{"wy":"Posted as a joking reference to TempleOS and Terry Davis in unrelated threads.","m":[{"id":48094168,"author":"Wololooo","month":"2026-05","story":"Ratty – A terminal emulator with inline 3D graphics","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Temple OS unhinged video.","text":"Obligatory Temple OS unhinged video. [VIDEO] Joking apart, the whole thing was both an exercise in madness and genius. Sometimes I wonder what he would have done if he had not gone crazy. We will never know..."},{"id":40965673,"author":"latentsea","month":"2024-07","story":"Fusion – A hobby OS implemented in Nim","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's a claim the author of TempleOS used to make of himself.","text":"For anyone who didn't get the reference it's a claim the author of TempleOS used to make of himself. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24596660,"author":"nullsense","month":"2020-09","story":"Salary Negotiation: Make More Money, Be More Valued (2012)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And for those who didn't get the reference","text":"Yeah, I believe he did die. From what I understand he was mentally ill and lead a fairly troubled life. Watching his walkthrough of TempleOS is pretty fascinating. And for those who didn't get the reference: [VIDEO]"}]},"ogfYd705cRs":{"wy":"Referenced mostly as the primary source for what Google announced, such as AI features, rather than recommended on its own merits.","m":[{"id":18228213,"author":"WilliamEdward","month":"2018-10","story":"The Average User Checks Email 5.6 Hours Per Weekday","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote presentation on AI smart replies.","quote":"","text":"It's not really that much easier, but their new AI let's you type certain common responses like \"Thank you, I'll look at this\" in an instant. They made a presentation about this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17477447,"author":"augbog","month":"2018-07","story":"An AI system for editing music in videos","stance":"reference","why":"Google I/O keynote announcement on AI using mouth gestures.","quote":"","text":"Yup I believe they announced this at Google I/O Keynote this year actually -- they mentioned that while it might not be enough with just the audio, looking at mouth gestures can give AI enough to know what might be said by whom. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17420031,"author":"markdog12","month":"2018-06","story":"The Constrained Directional Enhancement Filter in AV1","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sorry, they're very likely running A/B testing of some sort, as this link was AV1 (Codecs: avc1.64002a (299) / mp4a.40.2 (140)) for me earlier, but no longer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17420132,"author":"wolf550e","month":"2018-06","story":"The Constrained Directional Enhancement Filter in AV1","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"avc1 is [LINK] not AV1 The available formats for that video are: $ youtube-dl -F [VIDEO] [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading webpage [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading video info webpage [info] Available formats for [VIDEO] format code extension resolution note 249 webm audio only DASH audio 70k , opus @ 50k, 37.58MiB 250 webm audio only DASH audio 88k , opus @ 70k, 45.92MiB 140 m4a audio only DASH audio 136k , m4a_dash container, mp4a.40.2@128k, 98.65MiB 171 webm audio only DASH audio 148k , vorbis@128k, 70.46MiB 251 webm audio only DASH audio 158k , opus @160k, 95.71MiB 160 mp4 256x144 144p 114k , avc1.4d400c, 30fps, video only, 29.91MiB 278 webm 256x144 144p 147k , webm container, vp9, 30fps, video only, 61.11MiB 242 webm 426x240 240p 226k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 75.75MiB 133 mp4 426x240 240p 252k , avc1.4d4015, 30fps, video only, 54.98MiB 243 webm 640x360 360p 413k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 140.49MiB 134 mp4 640x360 360p 644k , avc1.4d401e, 30fps, video only, 182.59MiB 244 webm 854x480 480p 764k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 227.69MiB 135 mp4 854x480 480p 1193k , avc1.4d401f, 30fps, video only, 377.25MiB 247 webm 1280x720 720p 1521k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 423.38MiB 136 mp4 1280x720 720p 2375k , avc1.4d401f, 30fps, video only, 730.12MiB 248 webm 1920x1080 1080p 2674k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 810.25MiB 137 mp4 1920x1080 1080p 4461k , avc1.640028, 30fps, video only, 1.34GiB 17 3gp 176x144 small , mp4v.20.3, mp4a.40.2@ 24k, 57.43MiB 36 3gp 320x180 small , mp4v.20.3, mp4a.40.2, 162.63M"},{"id":19014513,"author":"oldgradstudent","month":"2019-01","story":"A Waymo One Rider’s Experiences Highlight Autonomous Rideshare’s Shortcomings","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It may not have been easy to see that another breakthrough is required, but it was very easy to see that most predictions were laughable. The CEO of Waymo announced[1] in May 2018: > Phoenix will be the first stop for Waymo's driverless transportation service, which is launching later this year. Soon, everyone will be able to call Waymo, using our app, and a fully self-driving car will pull up--with no one in the driver's seat-- to whisk them away to their destination. And that's just the beginning! [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"rrBxZGWCdgs":{"wy":"Cited in political threads as a partisan example of how liberals view minority voters, with some commenters treating it as funny.","m":[{"id":21039149,"author":"sverige","month":"2019-09","story":"Sanders wants to replace credit reporting companies with public credit registry","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is the silliest argument against voter ID laws ever, and should be retired. [0] [1] [2] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20068305,"author":"CryptoPunk","month":"2019-06","story":"California is cracking down on the gig economy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ami Horowitz questioned people of liberal political persuasions on the ability of black voters to meet photo ID requirements","text":">>I'm going to go out on a limb and say that these individuals are more qualified to make their own life choices than people on the internet who know nothing about their lives. At the risk of going down the partisan rabbit hole, this reminds me of the video segment where Ami Horowitz questioned people of liberal political persuasions on the ability of black voters to meet photo ID requirements, and then asked black Americans the same questions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18404737,"author":"burfog","month":"2018-11","story":"“A Horrifically Bad Idea”: Smartphone Voting Is Coming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the first third interviewing people at UC Berkeley with that idea and the remainder interviewing actual minorities","text":"FYI, \"IDs which most minorities don't have\" is incorrect and it looks sort of racist. Here, the first third interviewing people at UC Berkeley with that idea and the remainder interviewing actual minorities: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17480912,"author":"mikejholly","month":"2018-07","story":"Utah voting system fending off 1B hacking attempts per day","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I found this video pretty funny while searching around","text":"This argument seems like grasping me to. How would one function in society at all without an ID? Opening a bank account, cashing a check, driving a car, visiting a doctor, taking a train or plane, going to court. These are all activities which require ID. Sure, there is a tiny fraction of people who can't engage in those activities. But how likely is it that those folks are going to be voting, anyhow? I found this video pretty funny while searching around: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12912882,"author":"exclusiv","month":"2016-11","story":"Donald Trump is the president-elect of the U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The left pretends to champion minorities with their stance, but it's really a disdain and underlying racism against those groups","text":"Voter ID is a good example of what you shared. The left pretends to champion minorities with their stance, but it's really a disdain and underlying racism against those groups: [VIDEO] They think they can't use the internet, get to the DMV, obtain an ID, etc."}]},"s93KC4AGKnY":{"wy":"HN cites it as shorthand for the tic-tac-toe lesson, especially in threads about AI and nuclear strategy or games that cannot be won.","q":{"x":"Make LLM's win every game of tic-tac-toe and see if it reaches the same conclusion of WOPR.","i":47157536,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47157536,"author":"Bender","month":"2026-02","story":"AIs can't stop recommending nuclear strikes in war game simulations","stance":"reference","why":"WarGames ending where WOPR learns tic-tac-toe lesson","quote":"Make LLM's win every game of tic-tac-toe and see if it reaches the same conclusion of WOPR.","text":"They have no real stake in any decision they make. And they are not human. Not even a sociopath or psychopathic human. At best they might be able to estimate casualties. LLM's probably can't even reach the logic conclusion of the fictional WOPR Joshua from the movie Wargames [1]. Make LLM's win every game of tic-tac-toe and see if it reaches the same conclusion of WOPR. [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video][6m][tic-tac-toe]"},{"id":8724211,"author":"at-fates-hands","month":"2014-12","story":"You can't win the game, it exists only to destroy your mind","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I thought the computer went by Joshua, since that's what Dr, Falkan referred to it in the last scene of the movie","text":"\"How about a nice game of chess?\" I thought the computer went by Joshua, since that's what Dr, Falkan referred to it in the last scene of the movie: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47450636,"author":"IIAOPSW","month":"2026-03","story":"Drugwars for the TI-82/83/83 Calculators","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the game was tic-tac-toe with a perfect","text":"Same, but it was a TI-84, and the game was tic-tac-toe with a perfect \"ai\" that would let you enter \"number of players: 0\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35217302,"author":"IIAOPSW","month":"2023-03","story":"Learning BASIC Like It's 1983 (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For me it was making an implementation of tic tac toe that never lost","text":"For me it was making an implementation of tic tac toe that never lost, just so that I could put 0 players as an interface option [VIDEO]"}]},"xO1zxPRRf4g":{"wy":"Linked as a joke or reference whenever Robert's Rules or formal meeting procedure comes up.","q":{"x":"Fans of The Wire will of course be reminded of this classic scene","i":32623090,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29913450,"author":"reggieband","month":"2022-01","story":"Dark Web – Justice League","stance":"reference","why":"Scene of gang adopting Robert's Rules as a facade","quote":"forces them to conduct their meetings using Rules of Order.","text":"There is a comic I remember from my childhood, \"Tom the Dancing Bug\" written by Ruben Bolling. He had a character called \"Harvey Richard, Lawyer for Children\". I linked a couple of examples [1], [2], [3], [4] but the satire, IMO, is incredible. It lampoons humanities proclivity to layer pseudo-rationality onto the irrational things that we do. It also reminds me of the TV show \"The Wire\" when Stringer Bell, a senior member of a street drug distribution ring of urban thugs, forces them to conduct their meetings using Rules of Order. [5] If you watch to the end, you see that in those circumstances order means nothing, it is just a facade. I guess you could say that I'm cynical about the prospects of such a court and I tend to see the underlying truth in the satires that point it out. 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37996053,"author":"Y_Y","month":"2023-10","story":"Astrophysicists Put All Objects in Universe into One Pedagogical Plot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Pardon me, but I believe this would be a \"point of information\" (aka request) rather than a \"point of order\" which should refer to a procedural matter rather than factual. For reference of course I would refer you to Robert's Rules of Order: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32623090,"author":"gorgoiler","month":"2022-08","story":"Robert's Rules of Order (1876)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Fans of The Wire will of course be reminded of this classic scene","text":"Fans of The Wire will of course be reminded of this classic scene where an early 2000s West Baltimore crack gang adopts Rules of Order to lend a professional approach to their meetings: [VIDEO]"}]}});
